edit Cards (KCC). As on 25\n-\n02\n-\n2022, 291.67 lakh new KCC \napplications have been sanctioned with a sanctioned credit limit of Rs. 3,19,902 crore \nas part of the drive.\n \n \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |194", "t": "Promotion of Sports in Tribal Areas of Madhya Pradesh", "head": "Page 1 of 2 \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. †*194 \nANSWERED ON 15.03.2022 \n \nPromotion of Sports in Tribal Areas of Madhya Pradesh \n \n†*194. SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the schemes being implemented to promote sports in tribal \nareas; \n \n(b) whether any special project is being implemented  in Madhya Pradesh, a \ntribal dominated State, so as to  enable tribal children  to showcase their \ntalent in traditional sports and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of various sports related schemes being implemented in tribal \nareas; \n \n(d) the details of various sports related schemes  being implemented in \nKhargone-Bharwani Parliamentary Constituency in the State of Madhya \nPradesh; and \n \n(e) whether the Government proposes to open any  Sports Academy in the \nState of Madhya Pradesh and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n\nPage 2 of 2 \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. †*194 TO BE ANSWERED ON 15/03/2022 \nREGARDING “ PROMOTION OF SPORTS IN TRIBAL AREAS OF  MADHYA \nPRADESH” ASKED BY SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH PATEL , HON’BLE MEMBER \nOF LOK SABHA \n \n(a) to ( d) ‘Sports’ being a State subject, the responsibility to promote and \ndevelop sports in the country, including tribal areas to enable tribal children \nto showcase their talent in tradi tional sports, rests primarily with the \nrespective State/Union Territory Governments. The Union Government \nsupplements their efforts.  However, the Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nimplements the following schemes for development of sports in the country, \nincluding the tribal areas of Madhya Pradesh: \n \n(i) Khelo India Scheme; (ii) Assistance to National Sports Federations; (iii) \nSpecial Awards to Winners in International Sports Events and their \nCoaches; (iv) National Sports Awards; (v) Pension to Meritoriou s \nSportspersons; (vi) Pandit Deendayal Upadhyay National Sports Welfare \nFund; (vii) National Sports Development Fund; and (viii) Running Sports \nTraining Centres through Sports Authority of India. \n \nDetails of the above schemes are available in the public do main on the \nwebsites of this Ministry and the Sports Authority of India. \n \nFurther, one of the verticals of the Khelo India Scheme, namely, “Promotion \nof rural  and indigenous/tribal games” , is specifically dedicated to \nencouraging and showcasing of traditional sports of the country. \n \n(e) This Ministry has accreditated 16 sports academies  of various sports \ndisciplines in the State of Madhya Pradesh  under the “Support to \nNational/Regional/State Sports Academies” vertical of the Khelo India \nScheme. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |195", "t": "Inter-Operable Criminal Justice System Project", "head": "-----------------------~-\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EARm SCIENCES\nLOKSABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 195\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2NDFEBRUARY, 2022\nCOASTAL AREAS SUBMERSION\n195. SHRI ANUBHAV MOHANTY:\nWill the Minister of EARm SCIENCES be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has conducted researches/studies to determine if any of India's vast\ncoastal areas are under the threat of submersion in the near future or in the coming decades;\nand\nif so, the details of coastal areas which are under imminent threat of such submersion and\nthe stepstaken/proposed to betaken by the Government to deal with such threats?\n(b)\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR\nMINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY\nAND EARTH SCIENCES\n(DR. nTENDRA SINGH)\n(a) Yes, Sir. Indian National Centre for Ocean Information Services (INCQIS) an autonomous\norganization of MoES has prepared and published an atlas of Coastal Vulnerablllty Index\n(CVI) maps for the entire coastline of India at 1:100000 scale using data on sea level rise,\ncoastal slope, shoreline change rate, coastal elevation, coastal geomorphology, tidal range\nand significant wave height. In addition, National Center for Coastal!Research (NCCR),\nChennai, an attached office of MoES is monitoring the shoreline changes along the Indian\nCoast using satellite images. The study of 28 years satellite data by NCCR, i.e. (1990-2018)\nindicates that 33.6 % of Indian coast is eroding with varying rate of changes.\nBased on the studies conducted, the length of coastal stretch recorded under very high CVI\nineach state and their percentage w.r.t total coastline of the State is as under:\n(b)\nState /Region Coastline under v~ h~ CVI class\nLength (kID) Percenta_g_eof toJhl coastline\nGujarat 124 5.36\nMaharashtra 11 1.22\nKarnataka and Goa 48 9.54\nKerala 15 _j_ 2.39\nTamil Nadu 65 6.38\nAndhra Pradesh 6 0.55\nOdisha 37 7.51\nWest Bengal 49 2.56\nLakshadweep Islands 1 0.81\nAndaman Islands 24 0.96\nNicobar Islands 8 0.97\nMinistry through its institutes is also providing technical solutions and advice to the State\nGovernments and UTs to deal with coastal erosion threats.\n*****\n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |196", "t": "Demand for Jobs under MGNREGS", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 196 \n  ANSWERED ON 15/03/2022 \n \nDEMAND FOR JOBS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*196.  SHRI DAYANIDHI MARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has conducted any  assessment or study on the demand for jobs \nunder Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee  Scheme (MGNREGS) \nbefore, during and after the pandemic and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Government has held consultations  at the grassroots level to understand \nviews of stakeholders in availability and demand of jobs under  MGNREGS during the \nlast three years and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government has taken cognizanc e of media reports indicating high demand \nfor jobs under MGNREGS amid increasing rural unemployment and lack of opportunities \nand if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  the manner in which the Government proposes  to manage reduced budgetary allocations \nin the current  fiscal considering that as many as 19 States have crossed  MGNREGS \nbudgets and the demand-driven nature of the scheme? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n  \n\n \nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 196 for 15.03.2022 \n  \n(a) to (c):   Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS) is a demand driven wage employment programme which  provide for the enhancement \nof livelihood security of the households in rural areas of the country by providing at least one \nhundred days of guaranteed wage employment in every financial year to every household whose \nadult members volunteer to do unskille d manual work. It provides livelihood security i.e., fall \nback option for livelihood for the rural households when no better employment opportunity is \navailable. As per the information uploaded by the States/UTs in Management Information \nSystem (MIS) of NR EGASoft, the details of  households demanded employment, households \noffered employment  and households who have turned up at the worksites and thus availed \nemployment under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS in the financial years 2019 -20, 2020-21 and 2021-\n22 (as on 08.03.2022) is given below: \n \n (Figure in crore) \nFinancial Year 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n(as on 08.03.2022) \nHouseholds demanded employment  6.17 8.55 7.89 \nHouseholds offered employment  6.15 8.54 7.85 \nHouseholds turned up at the worksite and \nthus availed employment* \n5.48 7.55 7.01 \n *Acceptance of offer of wage employment depends upon the beneficiaries. \n  \n  \n(d):      Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS) is a demand driven wage employment programme and funds are released to the \nStates/UTs on the basis of \"agreed to\" Labour Budget (LB) and performance of the States/UT s \nduring the financial year. Fund release to States/UTs is a continuous process and Central \nGovernment is committed in making funds available keeping in view the demand for work on \nthe ground.  \n \nThe Government had enhanced the financial allocation under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS \nfor financial year 2020 -21 from Rs.61,500 crore at Budget Estimate (BE) stage to Rs.1,11,500 \ncrore at Revised Estimate (RE) stage.  \n \nIn the current financial year 2021-22, the budget provision at Budget Estimate (BE) stage \nwas Rs. 73,000 crore.   The Ministry has requested for additional budget allocation which has \nbeen enhanced to Rs. 98,000 crore at the Revised Estimate (RE) stage as per the requirement of \nfunds. The Ministry s eeks additional fund for Mahatma Gandhi NREGS as and when required \nfor meeting the demand for work on the ground. \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |197", "t": "National Bamboo Mission", "head": " \n \n \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.197 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \n \nHEIGHT OF PLATFORM IN VALLIKKUNNU STATION \n \n \n197. DR. M.P. ABDUSSAMAD SAMADANI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of difficulties being faced by the \npassengers and the accidents that have taken place due to low height of \nplatform in the Vallikkunnu Railway Station under the Palakkad Division \nin Kerala;  \n \n(b) if so, the reasons behind the ongoing delay in raising the height of the \nplatform even after a long period and repeated demands from the people; \n \n(c) whether the Government is likely to take necessary steps to sanction \nthe work of raising the platform at the earliest; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n \n(a) to (d) Considering the safety aspects, Ministry of Railways in April` \n2018 have revised the norms of Minimum Essential Amenities and \ndecided to provide High Level Platforms at Railway St ations irrespective \nof their category on Broad Gauge which was not the case earlier, as only \n\nerstwhile ‘A1’, ‘A’ & ‘C’ category stations were provided with High Level \nplatforms. H owever, priority is accorded to a higher category station \nover a lower category station while sanctioning and executing the work \nof raising the height of platform. \n \nAt present, Railway stations are categorized as NSG1 to NSG6, SG1 to \nSG3 and HG1 to HG3. Va llikunnu station is a NSG6 category railway \nstation. The railway station has 02 medium level platforms, which have \nbeen planned to be raised to high level according to the priority assigned \nto it under the said policy. However, sanction of the work depends  upon \nrelative priority of similar works proposed for various stations and the \navailability of resources and technical feasibility. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |198", "t": "Decline in Demand for Work under MGNREGS", "head": "  \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 198 \n  ANSWERED ON 15/03/2022 \n \nDECLINE IN DEMAND FOR WORK UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*198.  SHRI V.K.SREEKANDAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that there has been a demand to allocate a sum of Rs. 2.6 lakh crore \nwhich would cover the guaranteed 100 days of work under Mahatma Gandhi National \nRural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) for all active job card holders; \n(b)  if so, whether it is also a fact that the allocation less than Rs.1.4 lakh crore which was the \namount spent in 2020-21 plus inflation will suppress the demand and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(c)  whether it is also a fact that the demand for work under MGNREGS has declined due to \nfunding constraints; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n \n \nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 198 for 15.03.2022 \n  \n(a)&(b):   No, Sir. Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS) is a demand driven wage employment programme and funds are re leased to the \nStates/UTs on the basis of \"agreed to\" Labour Budget (LB) and performance of the States/UTs \nduring the financial year. Fund release to States/UTs is a continuous process and Central \nGovernment is committed in making funds available keeping in  view the demand for work on \nthe ground.  \n  \n(c)&(d):       The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (Mahatma \nGandhi NREGA), 2005, is an Act to provide for the enhancement of livelihood security of the \nhouseholds in rural areas of the country by providing at least one hundred days of guaranteed \nwage employment in every financial year to every household whose adult members volunteer to \ndo unskilled manual work.  The details of persondays generated under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS \nin the financial year 2019-20, 2020-21 and 2021-22 (as on 08.03.2022) is given below: \n \nFinancial Year 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n(as on 08.03.2022) \nPersondays in crore 265.35 389.09 333.21 \nIn the current financial year 2021 -22 (as on 08.03.2022), 99.47% households are offered \nemployment against their demand for work under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |199", "t": "One School-One Sport", "head": "Page 1 of 3 \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *199 \nANSWERED ON 15.03.2022 \n \nOne School-One Sport \n \n*199. SHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government’s plan of One School -One Sport is a unique \nconcept to create world-class sportsmen; \n \n(b) whether the Government has finalized the focus  areas/sports and if so, \nthe details thereof, State/UT-wise; \n \n(c) whether the tradit ional games like Kabaddi and  Kho-Kho will be part of \nthe list of sports to be included in the said plan, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether cricket will also be a game to be  considered in the plan and if \nso, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) whether the Government proposes to use the  expertise of accomplished \nsportsmen for this new venture and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n\nPage 2 of 3 \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *199 TO BE ANSWERED ON 15/03/2022 \nREGARDING “ ONE SCHOOL -ONE SPORT ” ASKED BY SHRI ANNASAHEB \nSHANKAR JOLLE, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \n(a) One School-One Sport policy is an initiative undertaken by the Ministry of \nDefence for Sainik Schools located across the country s o that they can \nfocus on at least one sporting discipline identified for the respective \nState/UT. \n \nUnder the Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports, the concept  of One State One \nGame (OSOG) has been designed keeping in mind India’s preparedness in \nachieving excellence in Olympics 2024 and beyond. The long-term plan is \naimed at making each State or Union Territory (UT) proficient in 2 -3 sports \ndisciplines in which it possesses a special aptitude for winning an Olympics \nmedal and where in the Central Government could provide support for soft \ncomponents such as coaching, sports science equipment, sports \nequipment, support staff, etc.  The principle of OSOG has been aligned with \nthe setting -up of Khelo India State Centre s of Excellence (KISCE) in all \nStates/UTs, where  sports-specific support , sports science support and \nnecessary technical handholding are provided to such Centres. \n \n(b) For OSOG, the Government has finalis ed the State/UT -wise list of \ndisciplines which have been recommended by the respective States/UTs as \ndetailed at Annexure. \n \n(c) Since the long -term vision is to develop excellence in Olympics, 14 \npriority Olympics disciplines have been identified out which  the respective \nStates/UTs have identified 03 priority disciplines for further development  as \nper Annexure. \n \n(d) The game of cricket has not been included under OSOG. \n \n(e) Funding is provided to States/UTs to engage high -quality manpower, like \nHead Coaches  and High -Performance Directors. Accomplished \nsportspersons meeting the eligibility criteria are considered for such \npositions. \n\nPage 3 of 3 \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *199 TO BE ANSWERED ON 15/03/2022 REGARDING  “ONE \nSCHOOL-ONE SPORT ” ASKED BY SHRI ANNASAHEB SHANKAR JOLLE , \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nLIST OF STATES - OSOG / PRIORITY SPORTS DISCIPLINES \n          \nS. No. State / UT OSOG Discipline 2nd Priority Discipline 3rd Priority Discipline \n1.  Andaman & Nicobar Islands Rowing  Athletics Cycling \n2.  Andhra Pradesh Weightlifting Archery Athletics \n3.  Arunachal Pradesh Weightlifting Archery Judo \n4.  Assam Boxing Shooting Weightlifting \n5.  Bihar Athletics Weightlifting  Wrestling \n6.  Chandigarh Hockey Boxing Rowing \n7.  Chhattisgarh Archery  Athletics Hockey \n8.  Dadra & Nagar Haveli and \nDaman & Diu \nTable Tennis  Archery Athletics \n9.  Delhi Wrestling Athletics Boxing \n10.  Goa Swimming  Badminton Table Tennis \n11.  Gujarat Athletics Table Tennis Archery \n12.  Haryana Boxing Badminton Athletics \n13.  Himachal Pradesh Boxing Athletics Judo \n14.  Jammu & Kashmir Fencing  Rowing Judo \n15.  Jharkhand Archery  Hockey Athletics \n16.  Karnataka Swimming Athletics Shooting \n17.  Kerala Athletics Judo Boxing \n18.  Ladakh Archery  Boxing Athletics \n19.  Lakshadweep Athletics Badminton Swimming \n20.  Madhya Pradesh Shooting  Hockey Rowing \n21.  Maharashtra Shooting  Athletics Cycling \n22.  Manipur Weightlifting  Archery Hockey \n23.  Meghalaya Boxing Archery Athletics \n24.  Mizoram Boxing  Judo Weightlifting \n25.  Nagaland Wrestling Archery Boxing \n26.  Odisha Hockey  Weightlifting  Athletics \n27.  Puducherry Weightlifting Badminton Athletics \n28.  Punjab Hockey Shooting Athletics \n29.  Rajasthan Archery Cycling Shooting \n30.  Sikkim Archery  Athletics Boxing \n31.  Tamil Nadu Table Tennis Athletics   Fencing \n32.  Telangana Rowing  Fencing  Weightlifting \n33.  Tripura Judo  Swimming Athletics \n34.  Uttar Pradesh Hockey Athletics Badminton \n35.  Uttarakhand Athletics Bad"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |2", "t": "Modernisation of Post Offices", "head": "��\n�\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF POSTS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 2 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2ND FEBRUARY, 2022 \n \nMODERNISATION OF POST OFFICES \n \n*2.  MS. DEBASREE CHAUDHURI:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has undertaken any programme for computerisation and \nmodernisation of Post Offices in the country;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, State-wise including West Bengal;  \n \n(c)  the number of Post Offices computerised as o n date in each State including West Bengal; \nand  \n \n(d)  the number of Post Offices yet to be computerized? \n \nANSWER \n \n MINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS &  \nELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (d)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\n��\n�\nSTATEMENT TO BE LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE LOK SABHA IN RESPECT OF \nPARTS (a) TO ( d) OF  THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 2 FOR 2ND \nFEBRUARY, 2022 REGARDING µ02'(51,6$7,21\u00032)\u00033267\u00032)),&(6¶\u0011 \n \n(a)  Yes, Sir.  \n \n(b) & (c)  Under the Information Technology Modernisation Project, the Department of \nPosts has computerised and modernised Post offices to provide better delivery of services. \nDetails are as follows:  \n \nAll the 25,109 Departmental Post Offices in the countr y including 1,770 Departmental \nPost Offices in West Bengal Postal Circle, have been computerised. The State wise list including \nUTs is at Annexure-I.  \n \nThe Department has also modernised 1,29,238 Branch Post Offices  including 7,249 \nBranch Post Offices in West Bengal by providing Subscriber Identification Module (SIM) based \nHand Held Point of Sale Devices, for carrying out online postal and financial services. The State \nwise details including UTs is at Annexure-II.  \n \nCore Banking Services (CBS) have been p rovided in 25006  Post Offices in the country  \nincluding 1,737 Post Offices in West Bengal Circle . The State wise list including UTs is at \nAnnexure-III. This facility enables customers to transact their Post Office Savings Bank \n(POSB) accounts from any Post Office across the country.  \n \nBesides there are interoperable Post Office ATMs available in 1000 locations including \n65 locations of West Bengal Postal Circle. State wise list including UTs is at    Annexure-IV.  \n \nPostal Life Insurance (PLI) services are being provided through Core Insurance Solution \n(CIS). Through this solution, the customers can make premium payment  online as well as at any \nPost Office. It has also enabled Central Processing Centres (CPCs) to provide faster claim \nsettlement and after-sales service.  \n \n(d)  All 25109 Departmental Post Offices have been computerised.   \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |20", "t": "Telecommunication Facilities", "head": "��\n�\n������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�������������� ������������������ �\n�\n��� ������ �\n������������������� �����\n������������������ ��� ��������� �������\n�\n���������������������������� �\n \n������������������������������������������������������������ \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has conducted any survey in Rajasthan to find out the areas \nwithout the facility of telecommunication till date;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof, district-wise including Dausa;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government is aware that the people of the State living in villages \nparticularly in Dhania village have to face a lot of difficulties especially during emergencies \ndue to the lack of telecommunication facility and if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(d)  the steps taken or proposed to be taken by the Government to provide telecommunication \nfacility there? \n�\n������ �\n�\n���������������������������������������� �\n�������������������������������������� �\n����������������������� �\n�\n(a) to (d)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n� �\n\n��\n�\n����������������������������������������� ��� ���������������������\n������ ���� ��� ���� ������ ���� ������� �������� ��������� ���� ������� ��� �\n��������� ��������������� ���������������������������������������������������������������\n \n(a) & (b)  Yes Sir. The Department of Telecommunications (DoT) identifies uncovered \nvillages of telecom connectivity of the country. As per data provided by Telecom Service \nProviders (TSPs) and field units of Department of Telecommunications as of December2020, it \nis estimated that out of 43,264 inhabited villages (as per Census -2011) in the state of Rajasthan, \n941 villages have no telecom connectivity. The District wise details of the uncovered villages are \ngiven in �������� ����\n \n(c) & (d)  As per data provided by Telecom Service Providers (TSPs) and field unit of DoT, \nit is estimated that out of 1079  inhabited villages (as per Census -2011) in Dausa district of state \nof Rajasthan, only 2 villages namely Dhaun (Census code -78756) and Ghata (Census code -\n78755) have no telecom connectivity. To further enhance the mobile connectivity in the State of \nRajasthan, 226 villages of Rajasthan State have been included in Universal Service Obligation \nFund (USOF) schemes. The remaining villages of the country, including the villages of \nRajasthan, are covered by the Government and TSPs in a phased manner. \n� �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |200", "t": "Crops under MSP", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 2\n00\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n15\nTH\n \nMARCH,\n \n2022\n \n \n \n \nCROPS UNDER MSP\n \n \n \n*2\n00\n.  \n \nSHRI D.K. SURESH\n:\n \n           \nSHRI PARTHIBAN S.R.\n:\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n     \nwhether the Government has received any complaint/suggestions on the coverage of \ncrops under Minimum Support Price (MSP) scheme and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(b)  \n \nthe percentage of MSP coverage\n \nfor all crops during the last three years;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether it is true that there are crops covered under MSP but not procured from \nfarmers at that price and if so, the reasons therefor, crop\n-\nwise;\n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government is taking steps to increase the c\noverage of MSP on all crops \nand if so, the details thereof; and\n \n(e) \n \nif not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n         \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (e\n)\n:       \n \nA Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT \nREFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO\n.\n \n200* DUE FOR ANSWER ON 15.03.2\n022 REGARDING \n‘CROPS UNDER MSP’\n \n \n(a) \nto \n(\ne\n)\n:    \n \nGovernment fixes Minimum Support Prices (MSP) for 22 mandated \nagricultural crops and Fair & Remunerative Price (FRP) for sugarcane on the basis of \nthe recommendations of the Commission for Agricultural Costs\n \n& Prices (CACP), after \nconsidering the views of State Governments and Central Ministries/Departments \nconcerned and other relevant factors.\n \n \nSuggestions/Representations from State Governments and various farmer \norganisations are received from time to time \nfor the coverage of crops under MSP. \nBefore finalizing its recommendations on MS\nP, CACP holds consultations with \nstakeholders and seeks \nsuggestion\ns\n \nfor both price and non\n-\nprice \nrecommendations. \nDetails\n \nof crop\n-\nwise procurement at MSP for last three years i\ns given at \nannexure.\n \n \nThe inclusion of crops under MSP framework is depe\nndent on several factors \nwhich include relatively large shelf life, widely grown,\n \nitem of mass consumption, among \nothers\n.  Time to time, Government examine the above mentioned factors \nwhile \nconsiderin\ng new crop under the MSP regime\n. \nAdditionally, agricultural and horticultural \ncrops for which MSP is not fixed and are generally pe\nrishable in nature, are covered \nu\nnder Market Intervention Scheme (MIS).\n \nF\narmers are free to sell their produc\ne to the \nGo\nvernment procurement agencies at MSP or in the open market whichever is \nadvantageous to them.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \nAnnexure \n \nreferred \n  \nto in\n \n \nreply to part \n(a) to \n(\ne\n) of Lok Sabha starred Question \nNo.\n \n200 \ndue for reply on \n15.03\n.2022\n \n \n \nCrops procurement at Minimum Support Price (MSP)\n \n \n \n                                                                                                       \n    \n(\nFigures in LMT\n \nunless stated otherwise\n)\n \nSl. \nNo.\n \nCommodity\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n1\n \nPADDY\n \n660.11\n \n770.93\n \n895.83\n \n2\n \nWHEAT\n \n357.95\n \n341.33\n \n389.93\n \n3\n \nOIL SEEDS \n \n18.29\n \n15.43\n \n2.95\n \n4\n \nPULSES\n \n19.45\n \n28.54\n \n8.16\n \n5\n \nCOARSE \nGRAIN\nS\n \n2.19\n \n4.32\n \n11.88\n \n6\n \nCOTTON\n*\n \n10.80\n \n105.22\n \n91.90\n \n7\n \nCOPRA \n \n-\n \n0.0031\n \n0.0509\n \n8\n \nJUTE**\n \n0.73\n \n0.82\n \n0.04\n \n \n* In lakh bales of 170 kg\ns\n \n**In lakh bales of 180 kgs\n \n \n \n \n \n        \n******\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |201", "t": "Line Capacity Utilization", "head": "�������������������� �\n������������� ������������������������������������� �\n��������� �\n������������������� ��������\n���������������������������� �\n�\n�\n���������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������ ��\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������\n�������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������ �\n��������������������������� ��\n�\n���� ����������� ������ ���������� ��� ��������� ��� ������ ���� ����� ����� ����� ���������� ������ ���\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ��������� ���������� ������� ������ ���� ���������� ����� ��������� ���� ���� ��������� ����\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������������������ ���������������� ���������� �������� ���������������� �������������������� �������� ������������ ������ �������������� ������ ������������ ���������� ������������������ ��������\n������������ ���������� ����������� ���� ��������� ���� ������������ ����������� ��������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n\n�������� ���� �������� ��� ���� ������������� ������������ ���������� ��� ���� ������� ����� ��� ���� �����\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��������\n� �\n\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |202", "t": "One Nation One Ration Card Plan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.202 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \n \nMODERNISATION OF RAILWAY STATIONS \n \n†202. SHRI JYOTIRMAY SINGH MAHATO: \n          SHRI DILIP SAIKIA:  \n          SHRIMATI GEETA KORA:  \n          SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \n          SHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA:  \n          SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK: \n           \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of railway stations likely to be modernised by the year \n2025 in the country; \n \n(b) the number of railway stations in North -Eastern States including \nAssam, West Bengal, Jharkhand, Bihar, Jammu -Kashmir and Madhya \nPradesh where modernisation process is going on at present including \nthe details thereof; \n \n(c) the number of railway stations of Adr a and Ranchi divisions under \nSouth Eastern railway likely to be upgraded; \n \n(d) the estimated expenditure of modernisation of railway stations under \nthe Railway including North -Eastern Frontier railway, South Eastern \nrailway and East Central railway;  \n \n(e) the number of railway stations under the railways including North -\nEastern Frontier railway, South Eastern railway and East Central railway \nin which wi-fi facilities have been provided by the year 2021; and  \n \n(f) the number of railway stations out of the to tal railway stations \nequipped with electronic display system? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (f):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n******  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.202  BY SHRI JYOTIRMAY SINGH MAHATO, \nSHRI DILIP SAIKIA, SHRIMATI GEETA KORA, SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT, \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA AND SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 02.02.20 22 REGARDING MODERNISATION \nOF RAILWAY STATIONS \n \n(a) Presently, Railway stations are upgraded/modernized under Adarsh \nStation Scheme based on identified need of providing better enhanced \npassenger amenities at stations. 1253 railway stations over Indian \nRailways have been identified for development under Adarsh Station \nScheme. Out of these, 1212 railway stations have been developed and \nthe remaining 41 stations are targeted for development in Financial Year \n2022-23. Further upgradation/modernization of stati ons including \nplatforms on Indian Railways is a continuous and on -going process and \npriority is accorded to higher category of stations over lower category of \nstations while sanctioning and executing the works.  \n \n(b)  In all 551 stations were identified fo r development under the Adarsh \nStation Scheme in the North -Eastern States including Assam, West \nBengal, Jharkhand, Bihar, Jammu -Kashmir and Madhya Pradesh. Out of \nthe 551 stations, 544 stations have been developed under the scheme \nand the remaining 07 stat ions are targeted for development in Financial \nYear 2022-23. \n \n(c) All the stations falling under Adra and Ranchi Division of South \nEastern Railway identified for development under Adarsh Railway Station \nScheme have already been developed. \n \n(d) The expenditure on works of modernization/upgradation/ augmentation \nof railway stations is generally funded under Plan Head –53 ‘Passenger \nAmenities’. The estimated expenditure  on  Plan Head - 53 for the last 03 \nyears and the current Financial Year (upto De c, 2021) of Northeast \nFrontier Railway, South Eastern Railway and East Central Railways and \nIndian Railways as a whole is as under:- \n\n(` in crore) \nZonal Railway 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 \n2021-22 \n(till \nDec,2021) \nNortheast \nFrontier Railway 78.06 100.27 104.07 71.60 \nSouth Eastern \nRailway 56.93 82.01 118.22 67.40 \nEast Central \nRailway 98.57 141.92 165.46 95.36 \nIndian Railways 1585.88 1903.10 2582.92 1234.74 \n \n \n(e) Total number of railway stations provided with Wi -Fi facilites so far is \n6095 including 382 stations of Northeast Frontier Railway, 248 stations \nof South Eastern Railway and 406 stations of East Central Railway.  \n \n \n \n(f) Train Indication Boards at 1176 stations and Coach Display Board at \n684 stations have been provided so far. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |203", "t": "Survey of Khargone-Barwani Rail Project", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 203 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nCOMMISSIONING OF THAWE-CHAPRA RAIL LINE  \n \n203.     DR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN: \n \n   Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:   \n \n(a) whether third line commissioning of Thawe -Chapra, Maharajganj and \nCaptanganj is pending since long despite repeated requests to complete it; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons for delay;  \n \n(c) whether there is alleged nexus between private bus operators and \nRailway officials to not complete the work so that more private buses can run, \nif so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) whether the Railways has fixed responsibility on the construction \ndepartment f or not completing the third line commissioning and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and  \n \n(e) whether any time frame has been fixed for completion of this work and if \nso, the details thereof? \n \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n(a): There is no sanctioned work of third line from Thawe -Chapra and \nMaharajganj- Kaptanganj. Hence, commissioning of these sections as third line \ndoes not arise.   \n \n(b) to (e): Do not arise. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |204", "t": "Antimicrobial Copper", "head": "         GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n         MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n                                                       \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 204 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.03.2022 \n \nANTIMICROBIAL COPPER \n \n*204. DR. RAJDEEP ROY: \n \n      Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of U.S. Environmental \nProtection Agency’s (EPA) announcement in February, 2021 that \ncertain copper alloys provide long -term effectiveness against \nviruses, including SARS-COV-2; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Government has any plans to utilize \nantimicrobial copper in trains coaches, railway stations and \nplatforms to prevent the spread of infections during times such \nas the current pandemic and provide inbuilt 24x7 safety system s \nto the passengers; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n (a) to (d) A Statement is laid on the Table of the house. \n**** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d ) OF \nSTATRRED QUESTION NO. 204 BY DR. RAJDEEP ROY TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 16.03.2022 REGARDING ANTIMICROBIAL \nCOPPER. \n \n \n(a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) to (d) Following items, with “Antimicrobial copper coating” have \nbeen provided in 300 coaches by Rail Coach Factory/ Kapurthala:- \n(i) Entrance hand rail complete with copper coating. \n(ii) Handle for Vestibule area with copper coating. \nIn order to control the spread of infections during the time such as \ncurrent pand emic, all Zonal Railways have been advised for use of \nSodium Hypochlorite (1%) including disinfection of contact area/ fittings \nlike seat with rexine covers, door handles, toilet and toilet fittings such \nas door handles and latches, water taps, hand rails,  switches, etc. for \ncleaning and sanitization of coach when Corona infected / suspected \npassenger travels. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |205", "t": "Approval for K-Rail Project", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA                                                                                                            \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA                                                                                                                                  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 205                                                                                                              \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.03.2022 \n \nAPPROVAL FOR K-RAIL PROJECT \n \n*205.   SHRI HIBI EDEN: \n            SHRI BENNY BEHANAN : \n \n          Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government of Kerala has submitted new \ndocuments/clarifications for the K-Rail (Silver Line) project for approval \nand permission, on or after 02 February, 2022 and if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Union Government has re -examined the project on or after \n02 February, 2022 and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the grant of approval/permission/NOC by the Government for \nthe project is likely to depend on the technological/economic/financial \nviability of the said project and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether it has come to the notice of the Union Government that the \nState Government is moving ahead with the project without conducting \nproper environmental impact studies and if so, the steps taken by the \nMinistry in this regard; and \n \n(e) the measures taken b y Government to increase the speed of existing \ntrains passing through the State? \n \n   ANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \n ELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n \n   (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 205 BY SHRI HIBI EDEN AND SHRI BENNY BEHANAN  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 16.03.2022 REGARDING APPROVAL FOR K -\nRAIL PROJECT \n \n(a) No, Sir. \n \n(b) No, Sir. \n \n(c) Project of Semi High Speed Rail from Thiruvananthapuram to Kasargod \n(530.6 Km) in Kerala has been identified   by Kerala Rail Development \nCorporation Limited (KRDCL), a Joint Venture company of State \nGovernment of Kerala (51%) a nd Ministry of Railways (49%) for \ndevelopment.  In- Principle approval (IPA) for taking up pre -investment \nactivities has been conveyed by Ministry of Railways. After survey, \nKRDCL has submitted Detailed Project Report (DPR) of Semi High Speed \nRail Project to the Ministry of Railways for approval. Cost of the project \nhas been assessed as ₹  63941 Cr. DPR is under examination by Ministry \nof Railways. Sufficient details for technical feasibility are not available in \nthe DPR. Therefore, KRDCL has been advised to  provide detailed \ntechnical documents such as alignment plan, particulars of railway land \nand private land, crossings over existing railway network, duly depicting \naffected railway asset through Zonal Railway after site verification for \ndetailed examinatio n of the project and to arrive at conclusion about \nfeasibility of project. Financial viability will be examined and firmed up \nthereon. Consideration of the project will depend upon techno -economic \nviability of the project. Project is not yet sanctioned. Ap proval of Cabinet \nCommittee of Economic Affairs will be required as project is costing more \nthan ₹  1000 Cr.  \n \n(d) No, Sir. Project is not yet sanctioned. \n \n \n(e) Indian Railways do not operate train services on State -wise basis as \nRailway network straddle across State boundaries. During 2020 -21, 179 \nExpress train services and 17 passenger services have been speeded \nover Southern Railway, which inter alia caters to the stations located in \nthe State of Kerala. Besides, speeding up of train services is a constant \nendeavour and continuous exercise on Indian Railways. \n \n*****  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |206", "t": "Vacancies in Railways", "head": "������������������� �\n�������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n���������������������� ���� ��\n���������������������������� �\n�\n����������������������� �\n���