������� ���������������� ������ ������\n����������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������� ���������������������������� ���������� ������ ������ �������������������� �������������������� ������������ ������ ����������������\n���������� �������������� ���������� ������ ���������������� ������ ������ ���������� �������� �������������������������� �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |259", "t": "Reservation in Ph.D Programmes at IITs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 259 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.03.2022 \n \nReservation in Ph.D Programmes at IITs \n \n259. SHRI GIRISH CHANDRA: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   whether students from the Scheduled Caste and Scheduled Tribe \n communities have significantly poor representation and acceptance \n rate in Ph.D programmes at the Indian Institutes of Technology (IITs) \n in the country; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof during the last five years and the current  year, \n course and category-wise and the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c) the corrective steps taken/to be taken by the Government in  this \n regard? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n \n(SH. DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (c) : A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.259 TO BE ANSWERED ON 21.03.2022 \nREGARDING “RESERVATION IN Ph.D  PROGRAMMES AT IITs” RAISED BY \nSHRI GIRISH CHANDRA, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT \n \n(a) to (b): The details of the application received, accepted and acceptance rate \nof the candidates f rom the Scheduled Caste (SC) and Scheduled Tribe (ST) \ncommunities in Ph.D  programmes at the Indian Institutes of Technology (IITs) \nare as under:- \nYear \nSC ST TOTAL \n(ALL CATEGORIES) \nApplications \nreceived  \nApplications \naccepted  \n% \nApplications \nreceived  \nApplications \naccepted  \n% \nApplications \nreceived  \nApplications \naccepted  \n% \n2016-17 12416 413 3.33 2242 92 4.10 114748 4372 3.81 \n2017-18 12794 427 3.34 2175 97 4.46 108526 4803 4.43 \n2018-19 15397 468 3.04 2787 106 3.80 127478 5292 4.15 \n2019-20 16197 524 3.24 3302 139 4.21 134338 5868 4.37 \n2020-21 21581 660 3.06 4572 154 3.37 182763 6521 3.57 \n2021-22 19603 605 3.09 3805 145 3.81 166808 6016 3.61 \n \nSome of the  reasons for poor representation and acceptance rate in PhD \nProgrammes can be attributed to:  (i) High level attrition due to better \nemployment opportunities; (ii) Family reasons for earning after graduation ; (iii) \nPriority towards management and allied subjects by Engineering Graduates ; \n(iv) Self-employment after gradu ation with soft loan opportunities  (Start-ups); \n(v) Higher salary/income on employment after Graduation vis -a-vis lesser \nfellowship/scholarship amount for pursuing research programmes etc.  \n(c): IITs are autonomous institutes governed by the Institutes of Technology \nAct, 1961 and the Statutes framed there under from time to time. Government \nhas ma ndated IITs to comply with the statutory provisions of the Central \nEducation Institutes (Reservation in Admission) Act, 2006, as amended from \n\ntime to time, for admission of reserved category candidates to various \nprogrammes including PhD. IITs duly follow guidelines, statutes, norms and \ndirections issued by Central Government. IITs have taken various steps to fill \nup the PhD seats for SC/ST Students that include (i) Adherence of guidelines \nfor implementation of reservation policy of the Government; (ii) Age  relaxation \nupto 5 years; (iii) Relaxation in minimum qualifying marks; (iv) Exemption of \ntuition fee to SC/ST students; (v) Subsidized application fee; (vi) apart from \nTuition fee waiver, assistant-ship is extended to SC/ST scholars of the Institute \nfor the sixth year of their PhD programme to ensure that the students do not \ndrop out due to lack of financial resources; (vii) More PhD applicants are \nshortlisted against the number of reserved category seats available for PhD \nadmission to ensure that enough a pplicants are available for admission under \nreserved categories; (viii) wide publicity to attract more applications on various \nplatforms including social media, newspapers, websites, etc.  \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |26", "t": "Nai Roshni Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *26 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n \nNAI ROSHNI SCHEME \n \n \n*26. SHRI TALARI RANGAIAH:  \nSHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state : \n \n (a) the details of funds sanctioned under the Nai Roshni Scheme during the \nlast three years, State-wise; \n (b)  the details of funds utilized, out of the allocated funds, during the said \nperiod;  \n(c)  the details of physical and financial progress made so far under the said \nscheme; and  \n(d)  whether the Government has taken steps to ensure that beneficiaries have \nattained socio economic empowerment post training under the scheme and \nif so, the details thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) to (d) :  A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *26 asked by SHRI TALARI RANGAIAH and SHRIMATI VANGA \nGEETHA VISWANATH regarding “NAI ROSHNI SCHEME”. \n \n(a) to (d) : Nai Roshni scheme is a Central Sector scheme which aims to empower \nand enhance confidence in women by providing knowledge, tools and techniques for \nLeadership Development of Women.    \n  \nIt is a six-day non-residential/five-day residential training programme conducted by \nselected Programme Implementing Agencies for the women belonging to minority \ncommunity in the age group of 18 to 65 years. The training programme cover areas \nrelated to programmes for women, health and hygiene, legal rights of women, \nfinancial literacy, digital literacy, Swachch Bharat, Life Skills, and advocacy for social \nand behavioral changes, etc. Scheme-specific other detailed information is available \nat website http://nairoshni-moma.gov.in . The scheme provides for hand-holding of \nall the beneficiaries for a period upto 12 months after the training.  \n \nDuring the last three year i.e. 2018-19 to 2020-21, funds to the tune of Rs. 26 crores \nhave been sanctioned under the scheme through which around one Lakh women \nhave been trained. State-wise details of physical and financial progress for the last \nthree years is placed at Annexure. \n \nSo far, Rs.108.24 Crore have been released to the Programme Implementing \nAgencies and around 4.35 Lakh women have been trained under the scheme. \n \n \n***** \n  \n\nANNEXURE  \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to Parts (a) to (d) of LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *26 asked by SHRI TALARI RANGAIAH and SHRIMATI VANGA \nGEETHA VISWANATH regarding “NAI ROSHNI SCHEME”. \n \nState-wise details of funds sanctioned under the Nai Roshni Scheme during \nthe last three years (2018-19 to 2020-21) \n \nS. \nNo. \nStates Fund Released  \n(in Rs. crores) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 0.39 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0.03 \n3 Assam 1.34 \n4 Bihar 0.14 \n5 Chhattisgarh 0.14 \n6 Delhi 0.59 \n7 Gujarat 0.03 \n8 Haryana 0.51 \n9 Himanchal Pradesh 0.03 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 0.30 \n11 Jharkhand 0.12 \n12 Karnataka 0.29 \n13 Kerala 0.16 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 3.11 \n15 Maharashtra 0.50 \n16 Meghalaya 0.02 \n17 Nagaland 0.08 \n18 Odisha 0.15 \n19 Punjab 0.29 \n20 Rajasthan 1.08 \n21 Telangana 0.13 \n22 Tamil Nadu 0.22 \n23 Uttar Pradesh 15.91 \n24 Uttarakhand 0.65 \n25 West Bengal 0.47 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |260", "t": "Proposals on Tribal Development", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 260 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF CLEAN ENERGY INFRASTRUCTURE \n \n+260. SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL \n        SHRIMATI MALA RAJYA LAXMI SHAH \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is making efforts for the development of clean energy infrastructure in \nUttarakhand and Bundelkhand; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to set up a mega solar power plant on the barren/infertile land \nlying idle in Uttarakhand and along the Yamuna and other rivers in Bundelkhand; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a)to(d) Ministry of New and Renewable Energy(MNRE) is implementing various programmes / \nschemes for the development of renewable energy in the country , including in the State of Uttarakhand \nand the Bundelkhand region.  \n \nMNRE has approved  the following solar parks in the Bundelkhand region under the scheme \n“Development of Solar Parks and Ultra Mega Solar Power Projects”.  \n \nS. \nNo. \nName of \nPark \nCapacity \n(MW) \nState Location \n1)  \nChhattarpur \nSolar Park 950 Madhya \nPradesh \nChhatarpur \nDistrict  \n2)  Barethi Solar \nPark 550 Chhatarpur \nDistrict \n3)  Kalpi Solar \nPark 65 \nUttar \nPradesh \nJalaun \nDistrict  \n4)  Lalitpur \nSolar Park 600 Lalitpur \nDistrict \n5)  Jhansi Solar \nPark 600 Jhansi \nDistrict \n6)  Chitrakoot \nSolar Park 800 Chitrakoot \nDistrict \n7)  Solar Park in \nUP* 265 Jalaun \nDistrict \n8)  Jalaun Solar \nPark 1200 Jalaun \nDistrict \n*Total park capacity is of 440 MW capacity, of which 265 MW falls under Jalaun District.  \nNo solar park has been sanctioned in the State of Uttarakhand under the above scheme. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |261", "t": "Centrally Sponsored Schemes of Scholarship for SC Students", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 261\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2022\n \n \n   \n \nMARKETING AND LIQUIDITY CRUNCH IN MSME\n \n \n261.\n \n \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI\n:\n \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE\n:\n \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL \nSAMBHAJIRAO MANE\n:\n \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK\n:\n \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA\n:\n \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO\n:\n \nDR. G. RANJITH REDDY\n:\n \nSHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU\n:\n \nSHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR\n:\n \nSHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA\n:\n \nSHRI SUBRAT PATHAK\n:\n \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV\n:\n \nSHRI RAVI \nKISHAN\n:\n \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether MSMEs are facing problems of marketing and liquidity due to COVID \npandemic\n;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the extent to which Rupees three lakh crore\n \npackage under Atmanirbhar Bharat \nhelped this sector\n;\n \n(c) \n \nthe status of disbursement of the above package, State\n-\nwise;\n \n(d) \n \nthe manner in which the Government is facilitating marketing of MSME products;\n \n(e) \n \nwhether the Prime Minister’s direction to clear\n \nall dues to MSMEs has not been \nimplemented and still Rs. 6,900 crores are due to MSME sector and if so, the details thereof \nand the reasons therefor;\n \n(f) \n \nthe details of the dues to be paid by private sector to MSMEs; and\n \n(g) \n \nthe details of e\n-\nmarket link\nages that the Government has for MSME sector ?\n \n \n    \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES \n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a):\n \nThe lockdown imposed during the Covid\n-\n19 period had its impact on the overall \neconomy including the MSME sector in the form of decline in sales, problems of relating to \nmarketing, liquity, etc. \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n(b) & (c):   The details of the state\n-\nwise benef\niciaries under two loans schemes announced \nunder Aatmanirbhar Bharat package viz. Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme \n(ECLGS) and Credit Guarantee Scheme for Subordinate Debt (CGSSD) are at Annexure I \nand Annexure II, respectively. \n \n \n(d):\n \nThe Ministry o\nf MSME provides business opportunities to Micro, Small and \nMedium Enterprises to expand their market base through various initiatives, including \nProcurement and Marketing Support Scheme, Coir Vikas Yojana (CVY), Scheme for \nInternational Corporation, Public\n \nProcurement Policy for Micro and Small Enterprises \n(MSEs).\n \n \n(e)\n:\n \nThe Ministry of MSME has written to other Central Ministries/Departments/State \nGovernments for the clearance of pending dues to MSMEs. The Government has taken \nmany steps to get the dues pay\nable to the MSMEs cleared by public sector units of State and \nCentral Governments. After the announcement made by the Hon’ble Finance Minister \nunder Aatmanirbhar Bharat, the Ministry of MSME has created a special sub\n-\nportal within \nSAMADHAAN portal to track\n \ndelayed payments to MSMEs from the CPSEs/Government \nDepartments/Ministries. From May 2020 to 27.01.2022, dues of Rs 88,391.94 crore have \nbeen settled through this sub\n-\nportal. Transaction in business is a continuous process in \nwhich some amount may remain \nstuck in the business process. As per the SAMADHAAN \nsub\n-\nportal, as on 27.01.2022, Rs 1,673.84 crore is pending for payment at the end of \nGovernment of India Ministries/Departments/PSUs.\n \n \n(f)\n:\n \nAs per the data available on SAMADHAN Portal, as on 28.01.2022, \nthe total dues to \nbe paid by the Private Sector to MSEs are Rs 8,869.67 crore.  \n \n \n(g)\n:\n \nIn order to provide an easy on\n-\nline access to all the procurement needs/demands \nfrom Government Buyers, an on\n-\nline portal Government e\n-\nmarketplace (GeM) is in place. \nThi\ns portal is meant for the all the vendors, including MSMEs. More than 7 lakh MSEs are \nalready registered on GeM portal. In addition to this, National Small Industries Corporation \n(NSIC) has also developed a Business to Business (B2B) portal viz. ‘MSME Glob\nal Mart’. \nKhadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) has also developed two online portals \n“\nwww.khadiindia.gov.in\n” and “\nwww.ekhadiindia.com\n” for providing e\n-\nmarket link\nages to \nMSMEs.\n \n \n*******\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n-\nI \n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nreferred to in reply to part (\nb\n)\n&(c)\n \nof the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n261\n \nfor answer on 03.02.2022\n \n \nState\n-\nwise number and amount of guarantees issued under ECLGS to beneficiaries            \n                 \nas on 31\nst\n \nDecember, 2021\n \nS. No.\n \nName of State/UT\n \nTotal\n \nNumber of guarantees issued\n \nGuarantee \nAmount\n \n(In Rs crore)\n \n1\n \nAndaman & Nicobar\n \n2048\n \n122.84\n \n2\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n284623\n \n10388.88\n \n3\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n2296\n \n82.28\n \n4\n \nAssam\n \n549765\n \n3102.96\n \n5\n \nBihar\n \n818895\n \n4390.99\n \n6\n \nChandigarh\n \n6892\n \n1086.62\n \n7\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n198587\n \n4975.73\n \n8\n \nDadra & Nagar Haveli\n \n2171\n \n333.81\n \n9\n \nDaman & Diu\n \n957\n \n151.98\n \n10\n \nDelhi\n \n102887\n \n19380.15\n \n11\n \nGoa\n \n12410\n \n1154.63\n \n12\n \nGujarat\n \n365537\n \n26408.89\n \n13\n \nHaryana\n \n20088"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |262", "t": "Establishment of Fisheries Centres", "head": "�\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA �\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \n  DEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \n����������\n����������������������� †����\nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������   \n  †262. SHRI JYOTIRMAY SINGH MAHATO \n             SHRIMATI RITI PATHAK \n             SHRI JUGAL KISHORE S \n             SHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK \n             SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT  \n \n Will the Minister of ����������  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether factors responsible for increasing wate r pollution during festivals in various rivers of t he \ncountry including in West Bengal and Yamuna river i n Delhi have been identified and if so, the details  \nthereof;  \n(b) whether any guidelines or advisory have been is sued by the Government to the States in this regard  \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether any increase or decrease in pollution level in Yamuna river has been witnessed during the last \nthree years and if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether any steps have been taken by the Govern ment of Delhi in this regard and if so, the details  \nthereof; and  \n(e) the details of the funds allocated and utilized for this purpose? \n�\n�������\n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n \n(a) to (c)    Rivers in the country are polluted du e to discharge of untreated and partially treated s ewage from \ncities/towns and industrial effluents in their resp ective catchments, problems in operation and mainte nance of \nsewage/effluent treatment plants, lack of dilution and other non-point sources of pollution. It is the responsibility of \nthe States/Union Territories (UTs), Local Bodies an d Industrial Units to ensure required treatment of sewage, \nindustrial effluents to the prescribed norms before  discharging into rivers and other water bodies, co astal waters or \nland and prohibit dumping of puja materials & other items of religious offerings to prevent and control of pollution \ntherein.  \nAs per the Provisions of Environment (Protection) A ct, 1986 and  the Water (Prevention & Control of Po llution), \nAct 1974, industrial units are required to install effluent treatment plants (ETPs) and treat their ef fluents to comply \nwith stipulated environmental standards before disc harging into river and water bodies. Accordingly, C entral \nPollution Control Board (CPCB), State Pollution Con trol Boards (SPCBs)/Pollution Control Committees (P CCs) \nmonitor industries with respect to effluent discharge standards and take action for non-compliance under provisions \nof these Acts.  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |263", "t": "Availability of Certified Quality Seeds", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nS\nT\nARRED QUESTION NO\n.263\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22\nND\n \nMARCH, 2022\n \n \nAVAILABILITY OF CERTIFIED QUALITY SEEDS\n \n \n*\n263. \n \nSHRI C.N. ANNADURAI: \n \nSHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe details of the estimated demand and supply of seeds in the country including \nMaharashtra and Tamil Nadu; \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government has reviewed the adequacy of research support for the \ndevelopment of seeds and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has launched any scheme to provide seeds at lower prices \nto farmers living below poverty line in t\nhe country including Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu, if \nso, the details thereof; \n \n(d)\n \n \nthe number of farmers benefitted from the scheme during the last three years; \n \n(e) \n \nwhether the Government has utilized the services of Non\n-\nGovernmental Organisations \nto pro\nvide seeds to farmers and if so, the details of such organisations; and \n \n(f) \n \nwhether the Government has fixed any time period for these organisations to execute \nthe task and if so, details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n(SHRI NARENDRASINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nto (f):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEME\nNT IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) TO (f\n) OF THE LO\nK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 263\n \nFOR \n \n22\nND\n \n \nMARCH, 2022\n \nREGARDING \nAVAILABILITY OF \nCERTIFIED \nQUALITY SEEDS\n.\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \nGovernment of India coordinates with the States \nin meeting their seed \nrequirement \nand a\nvailability through the mechanism of \nbiannual \nZonal Seeds Review Meetings and \nNational Conference on Agriculture prior to each sowing season. \nFor \nfuture seed planning, \nGovernment of India a\nsk States/UTs to prepare\n \nSeed Rolling Plan to ensure \navailability \nof \nseed\ns\n \nin future also. \nAs reported by the States, t\nhe details of \nthe demand\n \nand the supply\n \nof \ncertified/quality seed\n \nin the country (including Ma\nharashtra and Tamil Nadu) \nis\nas under:\n \n \n(Quantity in Lakh Quintals)\n \nYear\n \nRequirement\n \nAvailability\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n387.31\n \n431.01\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n443.16\n \n483.66\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n465.36\n \n498.83\n \n \n(\nb\n):\n \nI\nndian \nC\nouncil of Agricultural Research (IC\nAR\n)\n \nis carrying various research \nprogrammes including 25 All\n-\nIndia Coordinated Research Projects (AICRPs) and All\n-\nIndia \nNetwork Projects (AINPs) and seven other projects through their 732 \ncenters\n \nlocated in \n65 \nICAR\n-\nInstitutes, 3 Central Agricultural Universities (CAUs), 48 \nState Agricultu\nr\nal \nUniversities (SAUs) and 726 Krishi\n \nVigyan\n \nKendras (KVKs). \nThese programmes are \nreviewed annually through the Annual Group Meetings. The varietal and other technology \nde\nvelopment programme of the ICAR\n-\nInstitutes is reviewed by the Research Advisory \nComm\nittee, and Quinquennial Review Team. \nThrough \nBreeder Seed Review Meetings\n,\n \nregular review is \nbeing \ndone \nfor promotion of new high\n \nyield\ning\nvarieties\n.\n \nA total \n1956 high \nyielding\n, stress\n-\ntolerant crop varieti\nes\n \nof field crops which include 924\n \nvarieties \nof cereals \n(rice 442, wheat 127), 291 \nvarieties\nof oilseeds, 304 \nvarieties\nof pulses, 239 \nvarieties\nof \nfiber/commercial crops, 118 \nvarieties\nof forage crops, 64 \nvarieties\nof sugarcane and 16 \nvarietiesof other crops\n \nfor different agro\n-\nclimatic zones of the count\nry \n(\nin\n \nwhich more than \n1600 v\narieties are climate resilience\n)\n \nwere release since 2014 onwards.\n \n\n(c):\n \nGovernment \nof India \nprovides financial assistanceto different \nState governments \nand\n \nUTs\n(including Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu)\nfor production, \ndistribution of seeds and \nfor other interventions related to seed sector \nthrough various \nschemes \nviz. \nSub\n-\nMission on \nSeeds \nand Planting Materials (SMSP), \nNational\n \nFood Security Mission (NFSM),\n \nRashtriya\n \nKrishi\n \nVikas\n \nYojana (RKVY)\n \nfor small and marginal farmers of the country.\n \nThe pattern of \nassistance \nfor seed production, distribution and other related activities under \nthe\nse\nschemes of DA&FW \nis\nplaced \nat \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n.\n \n(d):\n \nThe details of \nfarmers benefitted under \nSeed Village Programme\n \ncomponent of \nS\nub\n-\nMission on Seeds and Planting Materials (SMSP)\n \nscheme \nis\n \nas under:\n \nFinancial Year\n \nNo. of farmers benefitted\n \n(in lakhs)\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n34.69\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n32.20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n36.44\n \n \n(e)\n \n& (f)\n: \n   \nGovernment of India provides assistance \nto different \nState\n \ngovernments and \nUTs\nunder various \nscheme\ns\n,\n \nwhich further\ndistribute it\n \nto small and marginal farmers\n.\n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nDETAILS OF ASSISTANCE/SUBSIDY AVAILABLE FOR SEED PRODUCTION AND DISTRIBUTION UNDER \nSCHEMES/PROGRAMMES OF DA&FW, MOA&FW, GOI\n \n \nS.\n \nNo.\n \nScheme/\n \nComponent\n \nCrop\n \nScale of Assistance\n \n1.\n \nSu"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |264", "t": "Welfare Schemes for Sportspersons", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 264 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n \n \nRIVER CITIES ALLIANCE \n \n \n264.   SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK \nSHRI SUBRAT PATHAK \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA \nSHRI RAVI KISHAN \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has recently launched Ri ver Cities Alliance (RCA), a platform for river cit ies in the \ncountry and if so, the details thereof along with the aims and objectives thereof;  \n(b) the details of cities which are a part of this alliance;   \n(c) whether the Government has fixed any criteria f or selection of cities to be part of the RCA and pr oposes to \nadd more cities in future in this platform and if s o, the details thereof and the time by which it is likely to be \nimplemented;  \n(d) whether the Government is taking any other meas ures to ensure better coordination among States tow ards \nriver water management and if so, the details thereof;  \n(e) whether the RCA would help in promotion of rive r management and its conservation and if so, the de tails \nthereof; and  \n(f) the details of the pending inter-State water disputes in the country, State/UT-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n \n(a) to (c) :  Yes sir, the River Cities Alliance (RCA) has been launched with the objective to provide the member \ncities with a platform to discuss and exchange info rmation on aspects that are vital for sustainable m anagement \nof urban rivers, sharing best practices and supporting innovation. The alliance has been launched initially with 30 \ncities namely Dehradun, Rishikesh, Haridwar, Srinag ar, Varanasi, Kanpur, Prayagraj, Farrukhabad, Mirza pur, \nMathura, Bijnor, Ayodhya, Patna, Bhagalpur, Begusar ai, Munger, Sahibganj, Rajmahal, Howrah, Jangipur, \nHugli-Chinsurah, Behrampore, Maheshtala, Aurangabad , Chennai, Bhubaneshwar, Hyderabad, Pune, Udaipur \nand Vijaywada.  \n \nThe Alliance is open to all river cities of India. Any river city can join the Alliance at any time.  \n \n(d): State Governments are implementing Action Plans for  restoration of water quality of the identified pol luted \nriver stretches. The implementation is being monito red regularly at State level by Chief Secretary of the \n\nrespective State/UT and at Central level by the Cen tral Monitoring Committee under the Chairmanship of  \nSecretary, Ministry of Jal Shakti.  \n \nIn addition, under Namami Gange Programme, the prog ress and activities are reviewed regularly by Natio nal \nMission for Clean Ganga (NMCG), State Governments and District Ganga Committees. \n \n(e): RCA is envisaged as a facilitatory platform for ini tiating river-sensitive planning and development. N amami \nGange through its integrated and comprehensive fram ework has been working towards rejuvenating Ganga r iver \nbasin. As cities start to implement the river-sensi tive development agenda, the River Cities Alliance will support \nthem to learn from each other and inspire others to take up progressive action on this front. \n \n(f): For adjudication of disputes relating to waters of inter-State rivers and river valley thereof, the Pa rliament \nhas enacted the Inter-State River Water Disputes (I SRWD) Act, 1956. When any request under the said Ac t is \nreceived from any State Government in respect of an y water dispute on the inter-State rivers and the C entral \nGovernment is of the opinion that the water dispute  cannot be settled by negotiations, the Central Gov ernment \nconstitutes a Water Disputes Tribunal for the adjud ication of the said water dispute. Currently, 5 suc h water \ndisputes Tribunals are active, the details of which  are given at Annexure . In addition, Inter State Water disputes \nhas been raised by Government of Bihar in respect o f Tilaiya-Dhadhar Diversion scheme concerning State s of \nBihar, Jharkhand and West Bengal and by Government of Tamil Nadu (TN) in respect of use, distribution and \ncontrol of Pennaiyar river and its tributaries conc erning States of Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Andhra Prad esh and \nUT of Puducherry.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE  \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART OF UNSTARRED QUESTION No. 264 TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.02.2022 REGARDING “RIVER CITIES ALLIANCE”.  \n \nStatus of active Inter-State Water Disputes Tribuna ls relating to the sharing of \nriver water \n \nS. \nNo \nName of \nTribunal \nStates \nconcerned \nDate of \nconstitution \nPresent Status \n1. Krishna \nWater \nDisputes \nTribunal – \nII(KWDT-\nII) \nKarnataka, \nTelengana, \nAndhra \nPradesh and \nMaharashtra \nApril, 2004 Report and decision given on 30.12.2010 . Further \nreport given by the Tribunal on 29.11.2013.  \n \nHowever, as per Supreme Court Order dated \n16.9."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |265", "t": "Projects under PMKSY", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *265 \nANSWERED ON 22ND MARCH, 2022 \n \nPROJECTS UNDER PMKSY \n  \n*265. SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV: \n           SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of Food Processing Industries be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the projects sanctioned and the financial outlay under the Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada \nYojna (PMKSY); \n \n(b) the details of the number of Mega and Mini Food Parks, Cold Chain Storage Systems and \nFood Testing Laboratories established under the PMKSY in the States of Bihar, Rajasthan, \nOdisha, Uttar Pradesh and Maharashtra since the inception of the Yojna, district-wise; \n \n(c) whether there is an increase in the conservation and processing capacity of the agricultural \nproduce in the above said States due to the projects undertaken under PMKSY in 2021-22; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the number of direct and indirect jobs generated due to the projects undertaken thereunder \nin the above said States in 2021-22? \n \n  \nANSWER \n  \n \nTHE MINISTER FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SHRI PASHUPATI KUMAR PARAS) \n  \n  \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n  \n\n  \n  \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE PARTS (a) TO (e ) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  265 FOR ANSWER ON 22 ND MARCH, 2022 REGARDING \n“PROJECTS UNDER PMKSY”. \n  \n(a): Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana  (PMKSY) was approved on 03.05.2017 with \ntotal outlay of Rs 6000 Crore and has recently been approved to be continued till 2025-26 with an \noutlay of Rs 4600 Crore.   A total of 1088 projects have been approved since inception of its \ncomponent schemes. \n \n(b): PMKSY is not any region or state specific but demand driven and financial assistance is \nprovided to the eligible applicants selected on the basis of Expression of Interest (EoI) issued from \ntime to time under its component schemes. The number of Mega Food  Parks, Agro Processing \nClusters (Mini Food Parks), Integrated Cold Chains and Food Testing Laboratories completed \nunder the PMKSY, which are located in the States of Bihar, Rajasthan, Odisha, Uttar Pradesh and \nMaharashtra is placed at Annexure-I. \n \n(c) to (e): Processing & preservation capacity created and estimated direct/indirect employment \nfrom the completed projects in the States of Bihar, Rajasthan, Odisha, Uttar Pradesh and \nMaharashtra during 2021-22 is placed at Annexure – II. \n \n \n***** \n  \n \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE - I \n  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.265 FOR ANSWER ON 22 ND MARCH, 2022 REGARDING “PROJECTS \nUNDER PMKSY”. \n \nNumber of projects completed under PMKSY in Bihar, Rajasthan, Odisha, Uttar Pradesh \n& Maharashtra \n  \n  Number of \nprojects \ncompleted in \nBihar \nNumber of \nprojects \ncompleted in \nRajasthan \nNumber of \nprojects \ncompleted in \nOdisha \nNumber of \nprojects \ncompleted in \nUttar Pradesh \nNumber of \nprojects \ncompleted in \nMaharashtra \nMega Food Parks 0 1 1 0 2 \nAgro Processing \nClusters (Mini \nFood Parks) \n0 0 0 2 3 \nIntegrated Cold \nChains \n3 12 3 18 60 \nFood Testing \nLaboratories \n0 1 2 8 20 \n  \n  \n  \n \n  \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE– II \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c ) AND (e)  OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.265 FOR ANSWER ON 22 ND MARCH, 2022 REGARDING \n“PROJECTS UNDER PMKSY”. \n \nProcessing & Preservation Capacity and Direct & Indirect Employment estimated from \nprojects completed during 2021 -22 under PMKSY in Bihar, Rajasthan, Odisha, Uttar \nPradesh & Maharashtra \n \nState No. of Projects  \nCompleted  during  2021-\n22 \nProcessing and Preservation \ncapacity created from projects \nCompleted during 2021-22 \n(LMT/Annum) \nEstimated Direct & \nIndirect \nemployment from \nprojects completed \nduring 2021-22 \nBihar       \n  \n1 0.45 390 \nRajasthan \n  \n3 0.537 724 \nOdisha 1 0.1 600 \nUttar Pradesh 9 1.47 3841 \nMaharashtra 20 6.915 16372 \n  \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |266", "t": "Computers to Panchayats under RGSA", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 266 \nANSWERED ON 22.03.2022 \n \nCOMPUTERS TO PANCHAYATS UNDER RGSA \n \n \n*266.  DR. G RANJITH REDDY: \nSHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU: \n \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n(a) the details and the number Panchayats in the country, State-wise;  \n(b) whether the Government has provided computers under Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan \n(RGSA) to Panchayats; \n(c) if so, the details of Panchayats that have been given computers and technical manpower \ni.e. computer literate persons in the State of Telangana, district-wise; \n(d) the details of requests received for sanction of computers and technical manpower from the \nGovernment of Telangana during each of the last five years; and \n(e) the steps taken by the Ministry in this regard to provide computers and manpower to \nPanchayats under RGSA? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \n \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN PARTS (a) to (e) IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 266 FOR ANSWER ON 22.03.2022 REGARDING \nCOMPUTERS TO PANCHAYATS UNDER RGSA \n___________________________________________________________________________ \n \n(a) Details of all levels of Panchayats in the country is at Annexure-I.  \n \n(b) Yes Sir.  \n \n(c) to (e) Panchayat being State subject, providing computers and manpower for Gram \nPanchayats (GPs) is primarily the responsibility of States. However, Ministry, under the \nscheme of Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan (RGSA) being implemented w.e.f. 2018 -19, \nhas been supplementing the efforts of all States, including Telangana, towards supporting \nfunctioning of GPs by providing computers for GPs and technical manpower at Block/ \ncluster of Panchayats as proposed by States in their Annual Action Plan and approved by \nCentral Empowered Committee on a limited scale.  Financial support for  computer and \ntechnical support for Gram Panchayats were not provided under the erstwhile scheme of \nCapacity Building -Panchayat Sashaktikaran Abhiyan (CB -PSA) implemented during \n2016-17 & 2017-18.  District -wise information is not maintained centrally. Details of \ncomputer and technical support proposed by State of Telanagana and approved there upon \nunder the scheme is at Annexure-II. \n \n \n \n \n****  \n \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexe referred to in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 266 answered \non 22.03.2022 \nDetails of all levels of Panchayats in the country \nSl. No. State Name District \nPanchayats \nIntermediate \nPanchayats \nVillage \nPanchayats \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 2 N.A. 70 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 13 660 13371 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 25 N.A. 2108 \n4 Assam 26 192 2197 \n5 Bihar 38 534 8151 \n6 Chhattisgarh 27 146 11658 \n7 Goa 2 N.A. 191 \n8 Gujarat 33 248 14288 \n9 Haryana 22 142 6225 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 12 81 3615 \n11 Jammu and Kashmir 20 275 4291 \n12 Jharkhand 24 263 4351 \n13 Karnataka 31 229 5963 \n14 Kerala 14 152 941 \n15 Ladakh 2 31 193 \n16 Lakshadweep 1 N.A. 10 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 51 313 22735 \n18 Maharashtra 34 351 27899 \n19 Manipur 6 N.A. 161 \n20 Odisha 30 314 6798 \n21 Puducherry N.A. 10 108 \n22 Punjab 22 151 13241 \n23 Rajasthan 33 352 11304 \n24 Sikkim 4 N.A. 185 \n25 Tamil Nadu 37 388 12525 \n26 Telangana 32 540 12769 \n27 The Dadra and Nagar Haveli and \nDaman and Diu \n3 N.A. 38 \n28 Tripura 8 35 591 \n29 Uttarakhand 13 95 7791 \n30 Uttar Pradesh 75 826 58189 \n31 West Bengal 22 344 3339 \n  Total  662 6672 255296 \n * NA- Not Applicable  \n\nAnnexure-II \nAnnexe referred to in reply to part (c) to (e) of the Lok  Sabha Starred Question No. 266 \nanswered on 22.03.2022 \nDetails of Computer and technical manpower proposed and approved to State of Telanagana \n \nYear \nComputers Administrative & Technical \nManpower \nProposed New Approved New Proposed Approved \n2018-19 532 532 205 205 \n2019-20 1569 1569 539 539 \n2020-21 245 245 539 539 \n2021-22 0 0 539 539 \nTotal 2346 2346 1822 1822 \nNote: The carryover activities under computerisation are also approved as proposed by State.  \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |267", "t": "Promotion of Indigenous Sports", "head": "Page 1 of 4 \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *267 \nANSWERED ON 22.03.2022 \n \nPromotion of Indigenous Sports \n \n*267. DR. NISHIKANT DUBEY: \nSHRIMATI KESHARI DEVI PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether a number of indigenous sports are being  played in different \nStates as per their culture and tradition and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) the details of steps taken/being taken by the  Government to promo te \n‘indigenous sports‘ in the country including Jharkhand and Uttar Pradesh; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to conduct  special training \nprogramme for the development and promotion of traditional sports; \n \n(d) if so, whether any review/evaluation has b een done for development of \nsports at the grassroot level and if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise \nincluding Jharkhand and Uttar Pradesh including Prayagraj district; \n \n(e) the details of places where traditional sports  competitions are being \norganized in the country; \n \n(f) the sports competitions which are being  proposed to be organized at \nPancyat/District/State level  to develop sportmanship in the country, \nState/UT-wise; and \n \n(g) the details of the financial and training assistance  being provided by the \nGovernment in such events? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) to (g) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n\nPage 2 of 4 \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( g) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *267 TO BE ANSWERED ON 22/03/2022 \nREGARDING “ PROMOTION OF INDIGENOUS SPORTS ” ASKED BY DR. \nNISHIKANT DUBEY AND SHRIMATI KESHARI DEVI PATEL , HON’BLE \nMEMBERS OF LOK SABHA \n \n(a) There are a number of indigenous sports being played in different States \nas per their cu lture and tradition . ‘Sports’ being a State subject, the \nresponsibility to promote indigenous sports in the country  and conduct \nspecial training programme s for the development and  promotion of \ntraditional sports  rests primarily with the respective State/Un ion Territory \nGovernments. The Union Government supplements their efforts.  As such, \nthe details of the various indigenous sports being played in different \nStates/UTs of the country are not maintained in this Ministry. \n \n(b) This Ministry runs a Central Sect or Scheme, namely, the ‘Khelo India – \nNational Programme for Development of Sports’ Scheme  (Khelo India \nScheme), of which one of the Components, namely, ‘Promotion of rural and \nindigenous/tribal games’, is specifically dedicated to the development and \npromotion of rural and indigenous/tribal games in the country.  Indigenous \ngames of Mallakhamb, Kalaripayattu, Gatka, Thang -Ta, Yogasana and \nSilambam have been identified for promotion under this Component. Grants \nare sanctioned for infrastructure development, equipment support, \nappointment of coaches, training of coaches and scholarships under this \nComponent. Further, scholarship to 283 medal  winners of Mallakhamb, \nKalaripayyattu, Gatka and Thang -Ta (₹ 10,000/ - per month, per athlete) , \nincluding 6 from the State of Uttar Pradesh,  have been provided by this \nMinistry. In addition, Mallakhamb, Kalaripayyattu, Gatka , Thang-Ta and \nYogasana have also been included in the forthcoming third edition of Khelo \nIndia Youth Games at Panchkula, Haryana.  \nIn addition, t his Ministry has also made documentaries of various \nindigenous games in order to showcase and promote/revive them, namely, \nGatka, Roll Ball, Tug of War, Kalaripayattu, Thang-Ta, Kho-Kho, Mallakhamb, \nShooting Ball, Sqay, Kabaddi, Gilli Danda, Sikkim Archery, Dhoop Khel and \nCowrie Khel, Paika Akhada and Chhau and Akhada Kushti, Hekko, Mizoram \nGames, Silabam, Lagori and Langadi. The documentaries have been  \nuploaded on the digital repository of the Ek Bharat Shreshtha Bharat \nwebsite, Facebook page of the Fit India Movement, Youtube channel of Fit \nIndia Movement and Youtube channel of MyGov India.  \n \n(c) This Ministry implements various Sports Promotional Sche mes across \nthe country through the Sports Authority of India (SAI), viz. National Centres \nof Excellence (NCOE), SAI Training Centre (STC), Extension Centre of STC, \n\nPage 3 of 4 \nNational Sports Talent Contest (NSTC) - (with its s ub-schemes for Regular \nSchools, Indigenou s Games & Martial Arts (IGMA) School & Akharas), \nwhereby services of expert coaches, sports equipment, boarding and \nlodging, sports kit, competition exposure, educational expenses, medical/ \ninsurance and stipend as per the approved scheme norms are provide d. The \ndisciplines in these schemes include traditional Indian games, like Kabaddi, \nArchery, Wrestling, Kho-Kho, etc. Additionally, with a view to promote other \ntraditional sports, SAI also provides support for training as well as financial \nin Silambam, Kalaripayatu, Mallakhamb, Khomlainai, Gatka, Mukna, Thang -\nTa, Kabaddi , etc. u"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |268", "t": "Food Park Policy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 268\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n0\n3.\n0\n2.2022\n \n \nEXPANSION OF MSME SECTOR\n \n \n268.\n \n \nSHRIMATI RITI PATHAK:\n \n  \n \n \n \nSHRI JUGAL KISHORE SHARMA: \n \n  \n \n \n \nSHRIMATI GEETA KORA: \n \n \n \n \n \nSHRI DILIP SAIKIA:\n \n \n \n \n \nSHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has formulated any action plan for the expansion of small \nand medium enterprises and to increas\ne employment opportunities in rural areas of the \ncountry particularly in North Eastern states including Assam;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details of the action plan formulated for Assam, Sonipat in Haryana, Jammu \nand Kashmir, Madhya Pradesh, Singhbhum\n \narea of Jharkhand; and\n \n(c) \n \nthe nature of the action plan and the funds allocated by the Government for this \npurpose?\n \n  \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES \n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) to (c):\n \n \nPromotion and development of enterprises is\n \na State subject. The Central \nGovernment supplements the efforts of the State/UT Governments through various schemes, \nprogrammes and policy initiatives for promotion, development and enhancing the \ncompetitiveness of MSMEs and to increase employment opportu\nnities in the country. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nThe Ministry of MSME implements various schemes and programmes for growth and \ndevelopment of MSME Sector in the country. These schemes and programmes inter\n-\nalia \ninclude Prime Minister’s Employment Generation programme\n \n(PMEGP), Scheme of Fund \nfor Regeneration of Traditional Industries (SFURTI), A Scheme for Promoting Innovation, \nRural Industry & Entrepreneurship (ASPIRE), Credit Guarantee Scheme for Micro and Small \nEnterprises, Micro and Small Enterprises Cluster Develo\npment Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP) etc. \nBenefits under these schemes are available to all eligible MSMEs throughout the country. \nMinistry of MSME has also been implementing a special scheme viz. ‘\nPromotion of MSMEs \nin North Eastern Region and Sikkim’ which focuses \non improving the condition of MSME \nSector in North Eastern States including Assam. The Scheme\n-\nwise Budget allocation of these \nschemes is annexed.\n \n \n********\n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \n                                                                                   \n \nBudget Allocation (Rs. in crore)\n \nS.\n \nNo.\n \nName of the Scheme\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n \n \n2021\n-\n22 \n \n(As on 31.12.2021)\n \n \n1\n \nPrime Minister's \nEmployment Generation \nProgramme\n \n(PMEGP)\n \n2,327.10\n \n2,500.00\n \n2,000.00\n \n2\n \nScheme of Fund for \nRegeneration of Traditional \nIndustries\n \n(SFURTI)\n \n125.00\n \n464.85\n \n170.00\n \n3\n \nA Scheme for Promoting \nInnovation, Rural Industry & \nEntrepreneurship (ASPIRE)\n \n50.00\n \n30.00\n \n15.00\n \n4\n \nCredit Guarantee Scheme\n \n597.00\n \n100.00\n \n0.04\n \n5\n \nMicro and Small Enterprises \nCluster Development \nProgramme (MSE\n-\nCDP)\n \n227.90\n \n390.69\n \n156.60\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |269", "t": "PLI Schemes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 269\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2022\n \n \nPMEGP\n \n \n269. \n \nSHRI RAJA AMARESHWARA NAIK: \n \nDR. JAYANTA KUMAR ROY: \n \nSHRIMATI SANGEETA KUMARI SINGH DEO:\n \nSHRI RAJVEER SINGH (RAJU BHAIYA): \n \nDR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n \nSHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \n \nSHRI BHOLA SINGH:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has implemented a credit\n-\nlinked subsidy program\nme named Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP); \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof along with the quantum of financial assistance given under this \nprogramme to the beneficiaries so far; \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the objectives/targets to generate empl\noyment thereunder in rural and urban areas of \nthe country have been achieved; \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor and the steps taken by the \nGovernment to generate employment under the programme; \n \n(e) \n \nthe\n \nnumber of women entrepreneurs benefited under the said programme during the above \nsaid period, State/UT\n-\nwise; and \n \n(f) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government for effective implementation of the programme including \nhike in the existing ceiling of financial ass\nistance for MSMEs?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(\nSHRI NARAYAN\n \nRANE\n)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nMinistry of MSME is implementing \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation \nProgramme (PMEGP) \nsince 2008\n-\n09 through Khadi\n \nand Village Industries Commission (KVIC) as \nnodal agency at the national level for generating employment opportunities in the country\n \nby setting \nup micro\n-\nenterprises in non\n-\nfarm sector. \n \n \nUnder PMEGP, General Category beneficiaries can avail of Margin Mon\ney subsidy of 25 % \nof the project cost in rural areas and 15% in urban areas. For Special Categories such as \nSC/ST/OBC/minorities/women/ex\n-\nserviceman/physically handicapped /NER/Hill and Border areas, \netc., the Margin Money subsidy is 35% in rural areas an\nd 25% in urban areas.  Maximum project cost \nfor manufacturing unit is Rs.25 lakh and for service sector is Rs.10 lakh.\n \n \n \nSince its inception upto 31.12.2021, about 7.38\n \nlakh\n \nnew micro units have been assisted \nutilizing margin money subsidy of Rs.17819.23 c\nr. generating estimated employment opportunities \nfor about 60.60\n \nlakh\n \npersons.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n(c) & (d):\n \nThe scheme has been successful in generating rural employment spread across various \nsocial categories. Around 80% of the PMEGP units have been set up in\n \nrural areas and about 50% of \nthe units have been set up by SC/ST/Women entrepreneurs. Target set and achievement made under \nthe PMEGP Scheme in the country during the last three years and current year as on 27.01.2022 is \ngiven below:\n \n \n( \n \nMargin Money\n \n:\n-\n \nR\ns. in lakh)\n \nYear\n \nTarget\n \nAchievement \n \nNo. of \nUnits \nassisted\n \nMargin \nMoney \ndisbursed\n \nEstimated \nEmployment \nGenerated\n \nNo. of \nUnits \nassisted\n \nMargin \nMoney \ndisbursed\n \nEstimated \nEmployment \nGenerated\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n72381\n \n206880.00\n \n579048\n \n73427\n \n207000.54\n \n587416\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n79236\n \n239644.00\n \n633888\n \n66653\n \n195082.20\n \n533224\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n78625\n \n228968.52\n \n629000\n \n74415\n \n218880.15\n \n595320\n \n2021\n-\n22 (as \non \n27.01.2022)\n \n92666\n \n285000.00\n \n741328\n \n60180\n \n185122.76\n \n370840\n \n \n(e):\n \nState/UT\n-\nwise number of Women beneficiaries benefitted under PMEGP scheme \nduring last \nthree years and current year is given at Annexure.\n \n \n(f):\n \nFollowing steps have been taken for the effective implementation of PMEGP:\n \ni.\n \nThe process of application flow, including bank sanctions and disbursement,\n \nhas been \nmade faster and transparent\n \nthrough introduction of on\n-\nline portal.\n \nii.\n \nThe process of selection of entrepreneurs has been further streamlined with the \ndiscontinuation of the role of District Level Task Force Committee (DLTFC) since April, \n2020, in recommending proposals/applications to\n \nfinancing banks. In the revised \nscenario, the proposals are now sent directly by Implementing Agencies to financing \nbanks based on a Score Card model, thereby reducing the overall approval period.\n \niii.\n \nOrganizing of Entrepreneurship Training Programme\n \n(EDP), mandatory before disbursal \nof loan by Banks, has been made online since October 2019 to provide expedited training \nand release of loan to the beneficiaries.\n \niv.\n \nFree two day online pre\n-\n \nEDP training has been introduced for interested applicants.  \n \nv.\n \nThe \nlist of activities has been expanded to attract entrepreneurs for setting up diverse \nunits.\n \nvi.\n \nImplementing Agencies like Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC), State \nKhadi and Village Industries Board (KVIB) and District Industries Centres (DICs) ar\ne \nproviding handholding to applicants, through call centres, online tutorials as well as with \nhelp desks with banking and marketing exp"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |27", "t": "Impact of River Bank Erosion", "head": "������������������� �\n������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�\n�� ��������������������� ����\n��������������������������������� ���� �������� ��������\n�\n����������������������������� �\n�\n����� ���������������� ������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������ ����������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� �������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������� �\n�\n�����