����� �������� ������������ ���������� ������������ ��������\n�������������������� ���� ������������������ �������������� ���� �������������������������� ������������������ ������ ������������������\n���������������� ������ �������������� �������������������������������� ������ �������������� ���������������� ������������������ ������ ��������������������\n�������������� �������������������������������� �������������������� ������������������ �������������� ������ ���� �������������� ���������� ����������������\n��������������������������������������������������\n�������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������\n������ ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ���������������������������������������������������������������������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������\n���������� ���������������������������� ������ ������������ ���������� �������� ���������������� ���������������� ������ ������������ ������������ ����������������������\n��������������������������������\n������������ ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ ������������������������������������������������\n������������\n��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |353", "t": "Incentive Programmes to Increase Forest Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,DEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & \nGANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 353 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n \n \nWATER PROJECTS IN MADHYA PRADESH \n \n \n†353. SHRI GYANESHWAR PATIL \nSHRI GAJENDRA SINGH PATEL \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of major water resources related projects being implemented in the State of Madhya Pradesh;  \n(b) whether any specific project is proposed to be implemented in tribal dominated Parliamentary \nConstituencies of Khargone-Barwani and Khandwa Burh anpur in Madhya Pradesh and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(c) the details of water resources related projects in rural areas of Madhya Pradesh State; and  \n(d) whether the Government proposes any specific project in Madhya Pradesh so as to achieve self reliance \nin water resources in the State and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n \n(a) to (d) Water resources projects are planned, funded , executed and maintained by the State \nGovernments themselves as per their own resources and priorities. Role of Government of India is limited \nto being catalytic, providing technical support and, in some cases partial financial assistance in terms with \nthe existing schemes being implemented by the Department of Water Resources . \n All projects supported by Government of India in t he water resources sector aim to improve water \nsecurity in the state. Details of major projects are as follows: \n  \nPradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY)  was launched during 2015-16 by the Central \nGovernment with an overarching vision to ensure acc ess to some means of protective irrigation for all \nagricultural farms in the country, and to produce ‘ per drop more crop’, with the following components,  \nAccelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme (AIBP), H ar Khet Ko Paani (HKKP)-Command Area \nDevelopment & Water Management (CADWM), Surface Min or Irrigation (SMI) / Repair, Renovation & \nRestoration (RRR) of Water Bodies and Ground Water Irrigation in this Department. \n  \nDuring 2016-17, fourteen ongoing major & medium Irr igation projects (and 7 phases) under PMKSY – \nAIBP of Madhya Pradesh having balance estimated cost of Rs. 3404.21 crore as on 01.04.2016 have been \nprioritized. Out of these 14 projects (and 7 phase) , AIBP works of 10 projects (and 3 phases) has been  \nreported to be completed. Details of these projects  are given at Annexure-I.  Details of Central \nAssistance released under Command Area Development and Water Management (CADWM) component \n\nfor 14 prioritized projects (and 7 phases) and Cult ivable Command Area (CCA) for these projects in the  \nState of Madhya Pradesh is given at Annexure-II.  \n  \nThe scheme of Surface Minor Irrigation (SMI) and Repair, Renovati on and Restoration (RRR) of \nWater Bodies  provides Central Assistance to States for creation  and restoration of Irrigation Potential. \nFour Clusters of 276 SMI schemes in MP were include d for the release of Central Assistance. So far, a \ntotal of Rs. 987.69 crore has been released as Cent ral Assistance and total irrigation potential of 0. 65 \nlakh-hectare has been achieved.. Under Repair, Reno vation & Restoration (RRR) of Water Bodies \nschemes, cluster of 125 water bodies was included with a planned potential creation of 0.33 lakh-hectares. \nSo far, Rs. 37.70 crore has been released as Centra l Assistance and total irrigation potential of 0.33  lakh-\nhectare has been achieved on the scheme.  \n  \nGovernment of India with financial assistance from the World Bank implemented the Dam \nRehabilitation and Improvement Project (DRIP)  during April 2012 to March 2021, with rehabilitati on \nof 223 dams located in 7 States. Under this scheme 25 nos. of dams of Madhya Pradesh have been \nrehabilitated. The list of these projects is given in Annexure-III . Presently DRIP Phase II and Phase III \nScheme envisages rehabilitation of 736 dams located  in 19 States with the budget outlay of Rs 10,211 \ncrore. The Scheme is of 10 years duration. Under th is scheme, Madhya Pradesh has proposed 26 numbers \nof projects for rehabilitation at an estimated cost  of Rs. 186.00 crore. The list of Dams proposed und er \nDRIP Phase-II and III in Madhya Pradesh is given in Annexure-IV.  \n  \n \nAtal Bhujal Yojana (ATAL JAL) is being implemented in identified water stressed rural areas of seven \nStates including Madhya Pradesh. The main focus of the scheme is on community participation and \ndemand side interventions for sustainable ground wa ter management. The scheme is being implemented \nin 9 Blocks and 672 Gram Panchayats of 6 districts viz., Chhatarpur, Sagar, Tikamgarh, Damoh, Panna \nand Niwari of Madhya Pradesh.  \n  \nThe Government of India has approved the implementa tion of Ken-Betwa Link Project  on 8 th  \nDecember, 2021 with an estimated cost of Rs. 44,605  crore with central support of Rs. 39,317 crore. \nApart from Daud"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |354", "t": "SAATHI Initiative", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 354\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.02.2022\n \n \n   \n \nGENERATION OF EMP\nLOYMENT IN MSME SECTOR\n \n \n \n \n†354. \n \n \nSHRI VIVEK NARAYAN SHEJWALKAR:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a)     \n \n \nthe action plan prepared by the Government for the creation of employment through \nSmall and Medium enterprises;\n \n(b)     the facilities being provided by the Governmen\nt for setting up of Small and Medium \nscale industries; and \n \n(c)     \n \n   \nthe steps taken by the Government for the implementation of these schemes \nespecially in rural areas?\n \n \nANSWE\nR\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES \n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nto\n \n(c)\n:\n \nPromotion and development of enterprises is a State subject. The Central \nGovernment supplements the efforts of the State/UT Governments through various schemes, \nprogrammes and policy initiatives for promotion, development and enhancing the \ncompetitiv\neness of MSMEs and to increase employment opportunities in the country. The \nMinistry of MSME implements various schemes and programmes for growth and \ndevelopment of MSME Sector in the country. These schemes and programmes inter\n-\nalia \ninclude Prime Minister’\ns Employment Generation programme (PMEGP), Scheme of Fund \nfor Regeneration of Traditional Industries (SFURTI), A Scheme for Promoting Innovation, \nRural Industry & Entrepreneurship (ASPIRE), Credit Guarantee Scheme for Micro and Small \nEnterprises, Micro and\n \nSmall Enterprises Cluster Development Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP) etc. \nBenefits under these schemes are available to all eligible MSMEs throughout the country \nincluding those in rural areas.  \n \n \n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |355", "t": "Proposal from Jharkhand to Include more Tourism Circuits/Parks", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.355 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n \nSCHOLARSHIP SCHEMES \n \n355. SHRI  M. BADRUDDIN  AJMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of applications received by the Government from meritorious students \nof minority communities for Post-matric, Pre-matric and Merit-cum-Means \nscholarship during the last five years; \n \n(b) the details about the number of applicants for each scholarship separately, year \nand scheme-wise; \n \n(c) the number out of them who succeeded ingetting scholarship during the above \nsaid period along with the details of the beneficiaries, year and scheme-wise; and \n \n(d) the amount of funds allocated and spent for these scholarship schemes, year and \nscheme-wise? \n  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) to (c): The Ministry of Minority Affairs implements three Scholarship Schemes \nviz.Pre-Matric, Post Matric and Merit-cum-Means based Scholarship Schemes for the \neducational empowerment of students belonging to economically weaker sections of \nthe six notified minority communities i.e. Buddhist, Christian, Jain, Muslim, Parsi and \nSikh in all States/UTs across the country. A total of thirty (30) lakh Pre-Matric, 5 lakh \nPost Matric and 60,000 Merit-cum-Means scholarships are targeted to be distributed \nas ‘Fresh’ Scholarships every year, besides, Renewal scholarships. The  three  \nscholarship  schemes are  being  implemented  through  the  National  Scholarship  \nPortal  (NSP)  and are  being  disbursed  under  direct  benefit  transfer (DBT)  mode  \nto  improve  the  efficiency  and  bring  transparency  by  removing duplicity  and  \nstopping  pilferages.    \nThe details of number of applications received, verified by States/UTs and \nsanctioned by the Ministry of Minority Affairs under the above mentioned schemes \nduring 2016-17 to 2021-22 are as under: \n \n \n \n \n\nYear Pre-Matric Scholarship \nNumber of verified \napplications received \nNumber of applications \nSanctioned \n2016-17 74,25,553 41,53,524 \n2017-18 85,70,708 53,11,257 \n2018-19 86,88,465 56,91,854 \n2019-20 70,84,410 55,68,025 \n2020-21* 60,38,248 52,29,148 \n2021-22** 78,29,283 24,05,498 \nTotal 4,56,36,667 2,83,59,306 \nPost Matric Scholarship Scheme \n \n2016-17 13,65,838 6,24,990 \n2017-18 14,07,780 6,98,069 \n2018-19 12,80,958 6,84,265 \n2019-20 11,24,202 7,43,141 \n2020-21* 11,61,252 6,62,912 \n2021-22** 9,91,534 1,38,105 \nTotal 73,31,564 35,51,482 \nMerit-cum-Means based Scholarship Scheme \n \n2016-17 189584 121937 \n2017-18 180421 119472 \n2018-19 180274 117771 \n2019-20 145398 118359 \n2020-21* 137340 120208 \n2021-22** 118351 52720 \nTotal 9,51,368 6,50,467 \n   *   Disbursement of Scholarships for 2020-21 continues in 2021-22. \n** It includes sanction for finally verified renewal applications of 2021-22 only. Disbursement \nfor fresh application 2021-22 is yet to begin. \n(d):  The details of budget allocated and released under the above mentioned \nschemes from 2016-17 to 2021-22 are as under:- \n(In ₹ Crore) \nScheme Pre-Matric Post Matric Merit-Cum-Means \nYear Budget \nAllocated \nAmount \nReleased  \nBudget \nAllocated \nAmount \nReleased  \nBudget \nAllocated \nAmount \nReleased  \n2016-17 931.00 585.94 550.00 287.11 335.00 220.54 \n2017-18 950.00  1108.13 550.00 479.72 393.54 388.79 \n2018-19 980.00  1176.19 692.00 354.89 522.00 261.17 \n2019-20 1220.30  1324.84 496.01 428.77 366.43 285.63 \n2020-21 1330.00  1325.54 535.00 512.81 400.00 396.34 \n2021-22 1378.00  474.52* 468.00 80.58* 325.00 134.09* \nTotal 6789.30  5995.16 3291.01 2143.88 2341.97 1686.56 \n*Expenditure as on 31.01.2022. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |356", "t": "Curriculum of Open Universities in Regional Languages", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 356 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.03.2022 \n \nCurriculum of Open Universities in Regional Languages \n \n*356. SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has any plan to start education curriculum of Open Universities in \nregional languages including Marathi like those of Post Graduate Diploma in International Business \nOperations conducted by the Indira Gandhi National Open University; and  \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \nPart (a) & (b) A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *356 TO BE ANSWERED ON 28.03.2022 ASKED BY SHRI \nHEMANT TUKARAM GODSE, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING \n“CURRICULUM OF OPEN UNIVERSITIES IN REGIONAL LANGUAGES”. \n \n(a) &  (b)      The National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 has envisioned use of mother \ntongue/local language as a medium of instruction and/or offer programmes bilingually, in order \nto increase access and GER and also to promote the strength, usage, and vibrancy of all Indian \nlanguage. Also it has been envisaged to encourage Private HEIs to use Indian language as \nmedium of instruction and/or offer bilingual programmes.   \n \nThe Indira Gandhi National Open University (IGNOU) is offering Post Graduate \nDiploma in Inter national Business Operation (PGDIBO) in two languages i.e. in Hindi and \nEnglish. IGNOU has also  signed agreement  with Yashwantrao Chavan Maharashtra Open \nUniversity (YCMOU) for sharing and translation of its study materials in Marathi language. \n**** \n  \n\n \n \n \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |357", "t": "Environmental Clearance in Sanctuaries/Protected Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI, \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA \nREJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED STARRED QUESTION NO. 357 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n \nPRAKASAM BARRAGE UNDER DRIP \n \n357.  SHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHNA RAJU \n SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY \n \n  Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Ministry proposes to include Prakasam Barrage for release of financial assistance under \nDam Rehabilitation and Improvement Project (DRIP);  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) the details of the other projects which are being considered for sanction of DRIP aid in the State of \nAndhra Pradesh? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n \n(a) & (b)  The number of dams identified for inclus ion under DRIP programme are based on \nproposals received from the participating States. T he individual States have done a review of the \nstatus of their dams and have identified those dams  that are in need of rehabilitation and \nimprovement. \n  \nGovt. of Andhra Pradesh has not proposed the Prakas am Barrage for its rehabilitation under the \nexternally funded Dam Rehabilitation & Improvement Project (DRIP) Phase-II & III.  \n(c)  State of Andhra Pradesh has shown willingness to participate in the DRIP Phases-II & III. Thirty \none (31) dams of Andhra Pradesh have been proposed for comprehensive rehabilitation under the \nsaid programme at a tentative cost of Rs. 777.00 Cr ore. List of dams proposed by Andhra Pradesh \nunder DRIP, Phase-II & III are given at Annexure . \n   \n*****  \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( (c) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.   \n357 TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 03.02.2022 REGAR DING “PRAKASAM \nBARRAGE UNDER DRIP \n” \n List of included projects of Andhra Pradesh under DRIP-II & III  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n \nSr.No.  Name of Dam  \n1 Alaganur Balancing Reservoir \n2 Andra Reservoir Project \n3 Bhairavanitippa \n4 Chennaraya Swami Gudi Project \n5 Cheyyeru Project(Annamayya) \n6 Chitravathi Balancing Reservoir \n7 Gotta Barage (Vamsadhara Project) \n8 Gundlakamma Reservoir \n9 Jalleru Reservoir \n10 Konam Reservoir Project \n11 Kovvaddakalva \n12 Mid Pennar Stage - 1 \n13 Mylavaram Reservoir \n14 Owk Reservoir Complex(Palair, Gollaleru, Timmare rvoir) \n15 Pampa Reservoir \n16 Pedderu Reservoir Project \n17 Penna Ahobilam (Dr. K S P A B R) \n18 Raiwada Reservoir Project \n19 Rallapadu Project \n20 Sanjeevaiah Sagar Project( Gajuladinne Project) \n21 Sir Aurther Cotton Barrage (Godavari Barrage) \n22 Somasila Reservoir \n23 Sri Kotla Vijaya Bhaskara Reddy Barrage (Sunkesu la Project)  \n24 Srisailam Project(NSRSP) \n25 Thammileru \n26 Thandava Reservoir Project \n27 Thatipudi Reservoir Project \n28 Veligallu \n29 Velugodu Balancing Reservoir \n30 Yeleru Reaervoir \n31 Yerrakalva "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |358", "t": "Central Government Schools in Andhra Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.358 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.03.2022 \n \nCENTRAL GOVERNMENT SCHOOLS \nIN ANDHRA PRADESH \n \n*358. SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the list of Central Government Schools in Andhra Pradesh, district-\nwise; and  \n(b)  the list of number of students enrolled, drop -out rate and teacher -\nto-pupil ratio at primary, secondary and senior secondary levels for \nall the schools in the above list, gender-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n****** \n \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 358 RAISED BY SHRIMATI \nGODDETI MADHAVI , HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO BE \nANSWERED ON 28/03/2022 REGARDING CENTRAL GOVERNMENT \nSCHOOLS IN ANDHRA PRADESH.  \n \n(a) Central Government Schools namely Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) and \nJawahar Navodaya Vidyalayas (JNVs) are under the Department of School \nEducation & Literacy, Ministry of Education. Presently, 35 KVs and 15 \nJNVs are functioning in the State of Andhra Pradesh. The District -wise \ndetails are at Annexure-I. \n(b)  The details of the gender -wise students enrolled, teacher -to-pupil \nratio at primary, secondary and senior secondary in each KV in the State of \nAndhra Pradesh are at Annexure – II. The details of the gender -wise \nstudents enrolled, teacher-to-pupil ratio at secondary and senior secondary \nin each JNV in the State of Andhra Pradesh are at Annexure – III.  No case \nof dropout has been reported in KVs and JNVs functioning in the State of \nAndhra Pradesh.  \n***** \n \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 358 RAISED BY SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI , HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO \nBE ANSWERED ON 28/03/2022 REGARDING CENTRAL GOVERNMENT SCHOOLS IN ANDHRA \nPRADESH. \nDISTRICT-WISE DETAILS OF KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS AND  \nJAWAHAR NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS IN THE STATE OF ANDHRA PRADESH \n \nSl. No.  Name of District Name of KVs Name of JNVs \n \n1.  Ananthapur SK University, Gooty  and    Guntakal  Ananthapur \n2.  Chittoor  No. 1 Tirupati and No. 2 Tirupati  Chittoor  \n3.  East Godavari Rajahmundary (ONGC) and Kakinada  East Godavari –I and \nEast Godavari -II \n4.  Guntur Guntur, Surya Lanka (AFS), Tenali, \nSattanpali and Irlapadu Village (Nadendla \nMandal  \nGuntur \n5.  Kadapa  Cuddappah and Rajampet  Kadapa  \n6.  Krishna Machhlipatnam, Vijayawada No.  I and \nVijayawada No. II  \nKrishna \n7.  Kurnool Kurnool  Kurnool \n8.  Nellore Kothuru and Venkatagiri  Nellore \n9.  Prakasam Ongole, Rajampalli Yerragondapalem and \nKandukur Town  \nPrakasam – I  and  \nPrakasam – II  \n10.  Srikakulam Srikakulam  Srikakulam \n11.  Visakhapatnam Bheemunipatnam (INS Kalinga), \nMalkapuram, NAD Vizag, Nausena Bagh No.  \nI, Nausena Bagh No. II, Srivijaya Nagar No. I, \nSrivijaya Nagar No.  II, Visakhapatnam Steel \nProject and Waltair  \nVisakhapatnam \n12.  Vizianagaram Vizianagaram  Vizianagaram \n13.  West Godavari Eluru  West Godavari \n \n***** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 358 RAISED BY SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI , HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT TO \nBE ANSWERED ON 28/03/2022 REGARDING CENTRAL GOVERNMENT SCHOOLS IN ANDHRA \nPRADESH. \nTHE DETAILS OF GENDER-WISE STUDENTS ENROLLED  \nAND TEACHER PUPIL RATIO IN KENDRIYA VIDYALAYA  \nIN THE STATE OF ANDHRA PRADESH \n \nSl. \nNo.  \nName of KV Gender Teacher Pupil Ratio \nBoys Girls  Transgender Primary Secondary Senior \nSecondary \n1.  Anantapur 239 222 0 1:36 1:21 0* \n2.  Eluru 324 296 0 1:33 1:28 1:04 \n3.  Gooty 204 226 0 1:30 1:23 1:05 \n4.  Guntakal 224 225 0 1:34 1:24 0* \n5.  Guntur  825 691 0 1:33 1:27 1:14 \n6.  INS Kalinga 268 212 0 1:34 1:25 1:06 \n7.  Irlapadu 168 138 0 1:32 1:21 0* \n8.  Kadapa 470 416 0 1:34 1:33 1:06 \n9.  Kakinada 269 240 0 1:40 1:26 0* \n10.  Kandukur Town 154 149 0 1:32 1:21 0* \n11.  Kurnool 507 447 0 1:35 1:33 1:10 \n12.  Machilipatnam 443 434 0 1:32 1:37 1:08 \n13.  Malkapuram 644 723 0 1:32 1:33 1:15 \n14.  NAD Visakhapatnam 731 683 0 1:35 1:34 1:13 \n15.  Nellore 446 433 0 1:32 1:32 1:07 \n16.  No.1 Nausenabagh 737 724 0 1:35 1:35 1:15 \n17.  No.1 Srivijayanagar 806 856 0 1:41 1:43 1:15 \n18.  No.1 Tirupati  953 939 0 1:33 1:28 1:15 \n19.  No.1 Vijayawada 831 831 0 1:42 1:41 1:12 \n20.  No.2 Nausenabaugh 664 688 0 1:32 1:33 1:15 \n21.  No.2 Srivijayanagar 1183 1025 0 1:40 1:41 1:19 \n22.  No.2 Tirupati 214 221 0 1:32 1:21 0* \n23.  No.2 Vijayawada 380 390 0 1:34 1:24 0* \n24.  ONGC Rajahmundry 387 427 0 1:44 1:37 1:10 \n25.  Ongole 628 610 0 1:31 1:35 1:08 \n26.  Rajampalli 243 162 0 1:34 1:18 0* \n27.  Rajampet 219 183 0 1:30 1:19 0* \n28.  Sattenapally 205 191 0 1:32 1:19 0* \n29.  Srikakulam 485 527 0 1:37 1:36 1:09 \n30.  Steel Plant \nVishakhapatnam \n635 690 0 1:42 1:34 1:15 \n31.  Tenali 345 374 0 1:37 1:26 1:06 \n32.  Ve"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |359", "t": "PM Poshan Shakti Nirman Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 359 \nANSWERED ON 3RD FEBRUARY, 2022  \n  \nROAD CORRIDOR PROJECT BETWEEN HILI AND MAHENDRAGANJ \n \n359.   DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has approved the development of road \ncorridor between Hili in West Bengal and Mahendraganj in \nMeghalaya via Bangladesh; \n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof including the \nfinancial sanctions;  \n(c) whether the Government of Bangladesh has agreed with the said \nroad project; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reaction of the government \nthereto? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a)     No sir. \n  \n(b) to (d)     The proposal on developing a link road between Hili in West \nBengal and Mahendraganj in Meghalaya through Bangladesh was raised \nwith Bangladesh during the virtual summit held in December’ 2020, as \nwell as during bilateral Summit in March’ 2021. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |36", "t": "Power Generation by Renewable Sources", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.36 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nEXTENSION OF NUAPADA-GUNUPUR RAILWAY LINE  \n \n36. SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the current status on the extension of Nuap ada - Gunupur railway \nline upto Therubali railway project; \n \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to mitigate any delays; \n \n(c) the updates on setting up new Railway Division at Rayagada under             \nECoR; and \n \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to expedite the project and the \ntime by when the project would be made operational? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 36 BY SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 02.0 2.2022 REGARDING \nEXTENSION OF NUAPADA-GUNUPUR RAILWAY LINE. \n(a), (b) and (d): Extension of Nuapada -Gunupur line upto Therubali \n(79.15 km) was included in the Budget 2017 -18 subject to requisite \napprovals. Government of Odisha was requested to share 50% cost of \nconstruction and to provide land free of cost. Government of Odisha \nhas agreed only for providing land free of cost. Detailed Project Report \n(DPR) of the project has been prepared. As per DPR, the cost of the \nproject is approx. �1398 crore.  DPR of the project has been taken up \nfor firming up the  results thereon. At present, the project is not \nsanctioned, as such, no time line can be fixed for making it \noperational. \n(c): Subsequent to the approval by the Government for setting up of a \nnew Railway Division with its headquarters at Rayagada, Chief \nTransportation Planning Manager, East Coast Railway (ECoR), \nBhubaneswar was nominated as Nodal Officer from ECoR to undertake \nplanning and preparatory work for setting up and operationalisation of \nnew Rayagada Division. Detailed Project Report (DPR) has bee n \nsubmitted by ECoR and an Umbrella work titled “Creation of new South \nCoast Railway Zone and new Rayagada Division in East Coast \nRailway” has been included in Budget 2020 -21 under Capital \nExpenditure at an estimated cost of �170 crore.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |360", "t": "Education Programmes Offered by Autonomous Colleges", "head": "1 \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *360 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 28.03.2022 \n \nEducation Programmes Offered by Autonomous Colleges \n \n*360. SHRI VIJAYAKUMAR (ALIAS) VIJAY VASANTH: \nSHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of autonomous colleges functioning in the country, State -wise along with the \nspecial features enjoyed by them over affiliated colleges; \n(b)  whether the University Grants Commission (UGC) has suggested modifications in rules to \nallow autonomous colleges to offer online and distance education programmes from \nacademic session 2022-23 without prior approval from UGC and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government h as consulted all the stakeholders and the general public before \nimplementation of these modified rules and if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether these modified rules also allow employment of education technology firms to \ndevelop content and evaluation system and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)   the steps taken/being taken by the Government to ensure the quality of online and distance \neducation being provided by these autonomous colleges in the country and the monintoring \nsystem in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \nPart (a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n2 \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *360 TO BE ANSWERED ON 2 8.03.2022 ASKED BY SHRI VIJAYAKUMAR \n(ALIAS) VIJAY VASANTH , SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU ,HON’BLE MEMBER (S) OF \nPARLIAMENT REGARDING “ EDUCATION PROGRAMMES OFFERED BY AUTONOMOUS \nCOLLEGES”. \n \n(a) UGC has notified its Regulations : University Grants Commission (Conferment of \nAutonomous Status Upon Colleges and Measures for Maintenance of Standards in Autonomous \nColleges) Regulations, 2018,  for granting autonomous status to colleges. The autonomous \ncolleges have administrative and academic autonomy for promoting broad based qua lity \neducation and excellence. The details of regulatory provisions can be accessed at \nhttps://www.ugc.ac.in/pdfnews/2838506_182734.pdf.  \n \nState-wise List of UGC approved autonomous colleges is enclosed at Annexure-I. \n \n(b) to (d)  Based on the recommendations of t he Expert Committee,  UGC has proposed \nmodifications in ODL and Online Programme Regulations. As per the proposed modifications, \nautonomous colleges having a minimum NAAC score of 3.26 on a 4 -point scale or having a \nrank in the top -100 NIRF, at least twice in three preceding cycles , may be permitted to offer \nprogrammes in online mode without the prior approval of the Commission. The HEIs may avail \nthe services of education al technology f irms for the purpose of technology maintenance, \nlearning platform, advertisement and marketing etc. However, entire responsibility of offering the \nonline programme with or without support of educational technology firms will be with HEIs. Any \nadvertisement or marketing activity in respect of such programmes shall be solely and expressly \nin the name of the Higher Educational Institution.  \n \nThe proposed modification was uploaded on UGC website at https://deb.ugc.ac.in/ for seeking \ncomments/suggestions from stakeholders.  \n \n(e) UGC has notified UGC (Open and Distance Learning Programmes and Online Programmes) \nRegulations, 2020 and an amendment on 4 th September, 2020 & 1 st July, 2021 resp ectively in \nthe Gazette. The same is available on UGC website at https://deb.ugc.ac.in/.  \nThe above regulations have specific provisions to ensure the quality of online and distance \neducation and its monitoring system. The same provisions shall be applicable to autonomous \ncolleges after notification of UGC (ODL Programmes and Online Programmes) (Second \nAmendment) Regulations, 2022. \n \n********* \n  \n\n3 \n \nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO PART (A) OF THE REPLY OF  LOK SABHA  STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *360 TO BE ANSWERED ON 28.03.2022 ASKED BY SHRI VIJAYAKUMAR \n(ALIAS) VIJAY VASANTH  AND SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU,  HON’BLE MEMBER(S) OF \nPARLIAMENT REGARDING “EDUCATION PROGRAMMES OFFERED BY AUTONOMOUS \nCOLLEGES”. \nUNIVERSITY GRANTS COMMISSION \n \nSTATUS LIST OF APPROVED 871 AUTONOMOUS COLLEGES AS ON DATE 08.03.2022 \n \nS.No. NAME OF STATE Sub-Total \n1. ANDHRA PRADESH 121 \n2. ASSAM 04 \n3. BIHAR 02 \n4. CHHATTISGARH 13 \n5. GOA 01 \n6. GUJARAT 05 \n7. HARYANA 01 \n8. HIMACHAL PRADESH 05 \n9. JAMMU & KASHMIR 04 \n10. JHARKHAND 05 \n11. KARNATAKA 85 \n12. KERALA 22 \n13. MADHYA PRADESH 43 \n14. MAHARASTRA 137 \n15. MANIPUR 02 \n16. NAGALAND 03 \n17. ODISHA 49 \n18. PONDICHERY 04 \n19. PUNJAB 10 \n20. RAJASTHAN 07 \n21. TAMIL NADU 228 \n22. TELANGANA 85 \n23. UTTAR PRADESH 13 \n24. UTTRAKHAND 04 \n25. WEST BENGAL 18 \n  TOTAL 871 \n\n4 \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |361", "t": "National Programme for Organic Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 361 \nANSWERED ON 03.02.2022 \n \nDEVELOPMENTS IN ENERGY SECTOR \n361. SHRI S. MUNISWAMY \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether 500 GW of energy would be generated taking into account the current developments in \nthe new and renewable energy sector; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether a committee is being set up to achieve a power production of 500 GW by the year 2030; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether this quantum of energy if generated would suffice and ensure electricity for every \nhousehold in the country; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) &(b) In accordance with Hon’ble Prime Minister’s announcement at COP26, Ministry of New \nand Renewable Energy is committed to achieving  500 GW of installed  electricity  capacity from \nnon-fossil fuel sources by 2030.  \n \n(c) & (d) T he Ministry of  New and Renewable energy has constituted a committee for preparing a \nroadmap to achieve 500 GW of non-fossil fuel based energy capacity by 2030. \n \n(e) & (f) The projected power generation capacity in the country in 2030 would be 817 GW out of \nwhich 500 GW will be non-fossil fuel based capacity.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|STARRED   |362", "t": "Guidelines for Recognized Sports", "head": "������������������� �\n������������������������������������ �\n���������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�\n�������������������� ������� �\n������������������ ���������� �\n�\n�������������������������������� �\n�\n������ �������������������� �\n�\n��������������������������������������������� ��������������������� �\n�\n���� �������� ������ ��� �� ����� ��� ������ ����������� ���������� �������\n��������������������������������������������������������� �\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������� ����������������������������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������