alaya for saffron cultivation. In Arunachal Pradesh, there \nis a good growth of organic saffron with flowers. In Meghalaya, sample \nplantations were grown at Cherrapunji, Mosmoi and Lalingtop sites.  \n \n(b)  The total cost of the whole project is Rs. 17.68 lakhs for Arunachal \nPradesh and Meghalaya. Out of which, a tentative amount of Rs.6.00 lakhs \nhas been earmarked for Barapani (Meghalaya) site. \n \n(c) & (d) The following sites have been identified in Meghalaya under the \nsaid project: \ni. Barapani,  \nii. Cherrapunji,  \niii. Mosmoi,  \niv. Shillong and  \nv. Lalingtop. \n \n(e)  Does not arise. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |126", "t": "Problems of Sugar Mills", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 126 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 10, 2022 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF SMART CITIES \n \nNO. 126.  SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY: \n          ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \n state: \n \n(a)  the total number of projects under the Smart C ities Mission  which have \nbeen completed and the total number of  projects  for  which work \norders were issued as on 31st December, 2021; \n \n(b)  the quantum of funds released as of December 2 021, out of the  total \ncommitment made by the Union Government to spend Rs.  48,000 \ncrore over a period of five years; \n \n(c)  the status of the execution of the work for de velopment of smart  cities \nin the country particularly in the States of Andhra Pradesh  and \nTelangana; \n \n(d)  whether any review has been conducted on the p ace of  execution \nof the said projects and if so, the details thereof ; and \n \n(e)  the steps being taken by the Union Government to expedite the \n completion of smart city projects in the country? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF  HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a)  to (e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF PARTS (a) to (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *126 (6 TH POSITION) FOR ANSWER ON \n10.02.2022 REGARDING DEVELOPMENT OF SMART CITIES. \n \n(a) to (e):   Government of India launched the Smart Cities Missi on (SCM) on \n25 June 2015.  100 Smart cities have been selected through 4 rounds of \ncompetition from January 2016 to June 2018.   As pe r SCM Guidelines, \nCentral Government will provide financial support t o the extent of \u0001 48,000 \ncrore over five years i.e. on an average \u0001 100 crore per city per year.  An \nequal amount on a matching basis will be contribute d by the State \nGovernment/Urban Local Body.  As on 21 January 2022 , Government of India \nhas released \u0001 \u0001 28,413.60 crore for 100 Smart Cities, out of which  23,668.27 \ncrore (83%) have been utilized.   \n \nSo far, these Smart Cities have tendered out 6,721 projects worth        \u0001 \n1,88,506 crore; work orders have been issued in 6,1 24 projects worth \u0001 \n1,62,908 crore; 3,421 projects worth \u0001 58,735 crore have been completed.  \nState-wise/city-wise progress of projects of Smart Cities, including Smart \nCities of Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, is given in Annexure-I. \n \nThe implementation of SCM at the city level is done  by a Special Purpose \nVehicle (SPV) created for the purpose.  At State le vel, Mission \nimplementation is coordinated by the State level Hi gh Powered Steering \nCommittee (HPSC) chaired by the Chief Secretary of the State.   At the \nNational level, implementation of SCM is monitored by an Apex Committee \nheaded by Secretary, Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoHUA).  \nNominee Directors of MoHUA on the Boards of SPVs mo nitor progress in \nrespective cities on a regular basis.  MoHUA regula rly interacts with the \nStates/Smart Cities through video conferences, revi ew meetings, field visits, \nregional workshops etc. at various levels to assess  the performance of cities \nand handhold them for improving the same. Smart Cit ies are assessed on \nvarious parameters including, but not limited to, p roject implementation and \nutilization of funds through the Online Geospatial Management Information \nSystem (GMIS). \nThe period of implementation of SCM has been extend ed upto June 2023 and \nall Smart Cities are expected to complete their pro jects within the stipulated \ntime.  \n**** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I in reply to parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabh a Starred Question No. *126 for answer on \n10.02.2022 regarding Development of Smart Cities as ked by Shri Y.S. Avinash Reddy and Adv. \nAdoor Prakash, Hon’ble MPs.  \nState-wise/City-wise progress of projects of Smart Cities \n(Amount in ₹ crore) \nState/City \nDPR Stage Tender Stage Work Order Stage Work Completed Total \nNo. of \nProjects  Amount No. of \nProjects  Amount No. of \nProjects  Amount No. of \nProjects  Amount No. of \nProjects Amount \nAndaman Nicobar Islands 10 355.50  4 221.67  5 367.25  7 39.65  26  984.07  \nPort Blair 10 355.50  4 221.67  5 367.25  7 39.65  26  984.07  \nAndhra Pradesh 3 9.80  4 74.20  124  5788.89  138  1885.03  269  7757.92  \nAmaravati 0 0.00  0 0.00  21  2046.00  0 0.00  21  2046.00  \nKakinada 3 9.80  3 22.20  33  891.62  60  1015.37  99  1938.99  \nTirupati 0 0.00  0 0.00  53  1506.29  34  189.41  87  1695.70  \nVisakhapatnam 0 0.00  1 52.00  17  1344.98  44  680.25  62  2077.23  \nArunachal Pradesh 41 1733.11  6 202.17  13  96.11  11  187.10  71  2218.49  \nItanagar 18 683.68  1 19.52  5 81.14  4 92.79  28  877.13  \nPasighat 23 1049.43  5 182.65  8 14.97  7 94.31  43  1341.36  \nAssam 1 2.60  12  901.60  5 54.96  6 52.57  24  1011.73  \nGuwahati 1 2.60  12  901.60  5 54.96  6 52.57  24  1011.73  \nBihar 30 546.91  16  1203.29  72  3963.75  53  1428.30  171  7142.25  \nBhagalpur 7 5.00  5 474.28  18  1475.24 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |127", "t": "Deaths of Elephants on Tracks", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 127 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \nDEATHS OF ELEPHANTS ON TRACKS \n \n127.  SHRI KARTI P. CHIDAMBARAM: \n  \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether  the Government is cognizant of the deficiencies in the \nprovision of Elephant Passages in Indian Railways as presented in \nthe CAG Report No.5 of 2021; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Railways has taken steps towards \nimplementing any of the suggestions detailed by the CAG in the \nreport to bridge these deficiencies for preventing Elephant deaths \non rail tracks;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and   \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 127 BY SHRI KARTI P. CHIDAMBARAM \nTO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 02.02.2022 REGARDING \nDEATHS OF ELEPHANTS ON TRACKS \n \n(a) to (d) Yes, Sir. Railways have already taken steps to prevent the \ndeaths of elephant on railway tracks in line with recommendations \nmade in Comptroller and Auditor General of India report no. 5 of \n2021.   \n \nA Permanent Co -ordination Committee has been constit uted \nbetween Ministry of Railways and Ministry of Environment, Forest \nand Climate Change for preventing elephant death in train \naccidents. Steps taken by Railways in coordination with Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change to prevent accidental  \ndeath of elephants due to train hits are as follows:  \n(i) Imposition of speed restrictions in identified vulnerable locations. \n(ii) Providing signage boards at suitable points to alert train drivers \nabout elephant presence.   \n(iii) Sensitization of Train Crew and Station Masters on a regular \nbasis.  \n(iv) Clearing of vegetation along railway tracks to allow unhindered \nand clear view for train drivers. \n(v) Provision of fencing at selected locations. \n(vi) Avoid  dumping of food waste along railway track to avoid \nunnecessary congregation of elephants and other wild animals near \nrailway track.  \n(vii) Installation of innovative Honey bee sound systems at \nlocations, which are prone to crossing of elephants. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |128", "t": "Leakage in Public Distribution Scheme", "head": "LOK SABHA\nUISTARRED OUESTIOI{ iIO'{28\nTO BE ANSWERED OIt zND FEBRUARY:2D2?\nLEAKAGE IT{ PUBLIC DISTRIBUTIOT{ SCHEME\nI28. SHRI G.M. SIDDESHWAR:\nTIgiIt the Minister of qoNsUMER AFFAiRS, FooD AND PUBLIG\nDrsrRrBUTrOil 3'nrl+ar enr*, vr<-r ct{ 8rffif,6, fuCrur *+ be preased tostate:\n(a) the steps taken by the Govemment to minimaze leakage in pubtic\nDistribution $cheme (pDS) for Antyodaya, Betow poverty Lrne (BpL) andAbove Poverty Lime (ApL) ration card hotders;\n(b) whether there aie any change in the distribution of pDs ration afterthe antroduction of Aadhaar linking and Biometric authentacation; and\n(c) if so, the detaits thereof?\nANSTYER\n]UIIIIISTER OF STATE FOR MIilISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT AIIDcoNsumER AFFA|RS, FOOD & puBl.tc DtsTRtBUTtOl{\n(sADHVt NTRANJAN JYOTT)\n(a): Under the Nationar Food security Act, 2o{3 (NFSA), this Departmentprovides foodgrains to states/urs for distribution through the TargetedPublic Distribution system (TpDs) to the beneficiaries/househords coveredunder the two categories of NF$A, namely Antyodaya Anna yoiana (AAy)and Priority Households (pHH).\nTo minimize the reakages and diversion of tire subsidized foodgrains,this Depaftment, in association with all states/urs, imprementJd thescheme of 'End to End computerization of rpDs operatioisr which inter-alia comprised digitization of ration cards/ beneficiariesr data, insta[ationof electronic Point of sate (epos) devices at at! Fair price shops (Fpss),etc. for transparent distribution of foodgrains to atl beneficiaries under1{FSA.\n......2t-\nGOVERNMENT OF TNDIA\nMlNlsrRY oF GoI{suMER aFFAlRs, FooD a puBltc DrsrRrBuTroil\nDEPARTMEiIT OF FOOD AND PUBLIG DISTRIBUTIOT{\n\n\"2-\n(b) & (c): Under ilFSA, this Department altocates foodgrains tostates/urs for dlistribution to around go crore ltFsA beneficiaries in thecountry on a monthly basis. Further, due to Aadhaar seeding andinstallation of eltos devices at the Fpss, at present, around 9oo/o of totat\nePos transactions an the country are done in a transparent manner throughbiometric autherntication on a monthly basis.. All states/urs have been\nadvised vide letter dated Z4llOt2OlZ that no genuine\nbeneficiary/househotd shalt be denied from receiving entifled quota ofsubsidized foodgrains only for want of Aadhaar or due to failure ofbiometric/Aadhaar authentication due to networldconnectivity/linking\nrelated issuesr other technicat reasons or poor biometrics of thebeneficiary, As per provisions of this Depattmentrs Aadhaar Notificationdated 8 Februan/ 2017, rar the Aadhaar is assigned to the beneficiaries,\neither of the eight identification documents shal! be used for identificationpurpose i.e. (Voter lD Card, pAl{ Gard, passport, Driving License,\nGertificate of ldentity with photo issued by Gazetted officer/rehsildar on\nofficial letter head, Address card having Name and photo issued byDepartment of Posts, Kisan photo passbook and any other document as\nspecified by state/UT Governments). AIso, the timerine given to allstates/urs for linking.of Aadhaar numbers of beneficiaries with their rationcards has been e:xtended up to gl t0gl2022.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |129", "t": "Auction of Coal Mines", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 129 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n  \nAuction of Coal Mines \n129. SHRI MAHESH SAHOO: \n             \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the coal mines auctioned by the Government during the last five years, State \nand value-wise; \n(b) the number of such auctions that have seen taken place in Odisha; \n(c) the details of the total value of the auctioned mines in Odisha; and \n(d) the percentage of the money generated through auction  in Odisha that has been  utilised \nin the State? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF COAL, MINES & PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n  \n(a)               Total of 32 coal mines have  been auctioned by Ministry of Coal from FY 2016 -\n17 onwards to till date. Details of coal mines auctioned may be found in the Annexure \nenclosed. \n  \n(b) & (c)      Out of aforementioned 32 coal mines successfully auctioned, 4 coal blocks \nhaving Annual production capacity of 21.6 Million Tonne are situated in the State of Odisha. \n  \n(d)          Total of Rs.7,37,51,41,459/- has been received as Upfront Payment from allocation \nof these four coal blocks and has been transferred to State Government. \n    \n**** \n  \n\n  \nAnnexure \nDetails of coal mines auctioned by Ministry of Coal from FY 2016-17 onwards \nSl \nNo Name of coal mine State Successful bidder \nAllocation \ndate \nPRC \n(in MT) \n1 Ardhagram West Bengal OCL Iron & Steel Limited 14-07-2016 \n0.4 \n2 Bikram \nMadhya \nPradesh Birla Corporation Limited 10-02-2020 \n0.36 \n3 Brahmapuri \nMadhya \nPradesh Birla Corporation Limited 10-02-2020 \n0.36 \n4 Jamkhani Odisha Vedanta Limited 10-02-2020 2.6 \n5 Jagannathpur B West Bengal \nPowerplus Traders Private \nLimited 13-02-2020 \n0.8 \n6 Brahmadiha Jharkhand \nThe Andhra Pradesh \nMineral Development \nCorporation ltd 02-03-2021 \n0.15 \n7 \nRajhara North-Central and \nEastern Jharkhand Fairmine Carbons Pvt. Ltd. 03-03-2021 \n0.75 \n8 Chakla Jharkhand \nHindalco industries \nLimited 03-03-2021 \n5.30 \n9 UrmaPaharitola Jharkhand \nAurobindo Reality and \ninfrastructure private \nlimited 03-03-2021 \n10.00 \n10 Sahapur West \nMadhya \nPradesh \nSarda energy and Minerals \nLimited 03-03-2021 \n0.60