  Indian  workers  including  those  from  Odisha\nposted/working in Kuwait including their per capita details?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n[SHRI V. MURALEEDHARAN]\n(a) Yes. During the visit of External Affairs Minister to Kuwait in June, 2021,\na Memorandum of Understanding on Cooperation on the Recruitment of\nDomestic Workers (DSWs) between India and Kuwait was signed on 10 June,\n2021.\n1\n\n(b) The Memorandum of Understanding aims to protect the rights of both\nDomestic  Sectors  Workers  (DSWs)  and  Employers  and  regulate  the\ncontractual relationship between them. Besides, MoU facilitates safe and\nlegal  recruitment  which  makes  a  good  relationship  between  India  and\nKuwait. The details and salient features of the MoU are as under:-\n(i)   T o facilitate opening of a bank account by the employer in the name of\nthe Domestic Workers for deposit of their monthly salary as provided in the\nemployment contract in accordance with laws and regulations applicable of\nthe State of Kuwait according to MoU.\n(ii) T o  ensure  that  the  employer  shall  provide  the  worker  with  food,\nhousing,  clothing,  and  registering  the  Domestic  Worker  in  the  health\ninsurance system that covers worker’s treatment in case of illness or injury\ndue to work as well as being committed to compensate for job injuries and\ntransporting the mortal remains of the Domestic Worker to his/her home\ntown.\n(iii) T o ensure that the aggrieved Domestic Worker shall have access to the\nDepartment of Domestic Labour for settlement of dispute and to competent\ncourt  in  case  of  need.  Domestic  workers  are  exempted  from  judicial\ncharges/fees at all levels of the judicial proceedings.\n(iv) T o ensure that the employer does not retain the Domestic Worker’s\npassport.\n(v) T o ensure that the runaway Domestic Workers shall be covered by\ninsurance for an initial period of six months towards the cost of return air\ntickets and unpaid wages, if any.\n(vi) T o take legal measures against recruitment and deployment agencies,\nofficers or companies in case of violation of law in both countries.\n(c) As per available information, total number of Indians including workers\nin Kuwait is estimated to be about 10 lakhs.\n                                                ***\n2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |563", "t": "Dredging Policy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 563\nANSWERED ONO4IO2I2O22\nDREDGING POLICY\n563. DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI:\nwilt the Minister of PoRTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nqtr{, fi-d qftTt\"T .lilt wcqrtf qfr\n(a)whethertheGovernmenthasundertakenstepstoadoptadvanceddredging\nitrategies such as maintaining local and regional sediment levels, recharging salt marsh\nareas with fine grained dredging to meet demand of all dredging activities of the ports\nand if so, the details thereof; and\n(b)whethertheGovernmentproposestoformulateadredgingpolicyandifso'the\ndetails thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) & (b) The Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways has promulgated in 2021\nbieoging Guidelines for Major ports with standard operating procedure (soP) for\nundertaking capital and Maintenance Dredging at Major Ports. Most of the material\nfrom dredging is unsuitable for recharging salt marsh areas as they are contaminated- lt\nisuneconomicaltousethespoilsasthechannelsarefarawayfrommarshyland'"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |564", "t": "National Waterways", "head": "(b)\n(c)\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 564\nANSWERED ON O4IO2I2O22\nNATIONAL WATERWAYS\n564. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nqf,-{, frd qfa{6;l efu qsqrrf q*\n(a) the details of the work in progress on the 37 prioritised national waterways in the\ncountry, State-wise particularly in Andhra Pradesh;\nthe amount of funds allocated to Andhra Pradesh for the development of these\nwaterways during the last two years;\nwhether the Government is considering to develop waterways by dredging and\nrestricting the movement of barges with dirty cargo such as coal, fly ash and iron ore\nthat have many serious adverse ecological impacts and if so, the details thereof,\n(d) whether the Government is aware that the States' Ferries Acts govern cross ferry\nmovement and this is creating a barrier to inland navigation, as the regulations are not\nbeing taken into account for safety considerations; and\n(c) whether the Government has taken any measures to address this concern and if so,\nthe details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(sHRr SARBANANDA SONOWAL)\n(a) To promote lnland water Transport (lwT) in the country, 111 waterways (including 5\nexisting and '106 new) have been declared as National Waterways (NWs) under the National\nWateniays Act, 2016 which came into effect from 12'h April, 2016. Based on the.o,utcome of\ntechno-economic feasibility and Detailed Project Reports (DPRs) of NWs, 25 NWs which\nhave been found viable by lnland Waterways Authority of lndia (lWAl) for cargo/ passenger\nmovement is at Annex-'|. Development activities have been initiated in the first 13 NWs out\nof the 25 viable NWs.\n(b) Rs. 96.00 crore has been sanctioned for development of the stretch between\nVijayawada to Muktayala in Krishna River of NW-4 in Andhra Pradesh for following\nworks:-\nEstablishment of four Floattng Pontoons,\nDredging as per requirement,\n(i)\n( ii)\n\n-2-\n(iii) Land acquisition for construction of 3 Nos. of fixed Ro-Ro terminals at Muktayala,\nHarichandrapuram and lbrahimpatnam.\nDuring the last two years i.e. 2019-20 and 2O2O-21 , Rs. 18.87 crore and Rs. 7.66 crore\nrespectively has been allocated to Andhra Pradesh.\n(c) Bulk cargo like coal, fly-ash and iron ore are found economical & cost-effective for\nmovement through lnland water Transport (lwr) which is environment friendly and less\npollutant mode of transport than Rail & Road. The cargo through lnland waterways is\ntransported in barges and there is no direct contact of the cargo with water and therefore\nno adverse ecological impact.\n(d) & (e) Yes, sir. operation of Ferries is a State subject. lnland waterways Authority of\nlndia (lwAl) established through lwAl Act, 1985 for the regulation and development of\nNational waterways for purposes of shipping and navigation has notified (with the prior\napproval of the Central Govt.) following regulations for safety considerations-\n(i) Prevention of Collision on National Waterways Regulations, 2002 and\n(ii) National waterway, Safety of Navigation and Shipping Regulations, 2002\nFurther Government has enacted the lnland Vessels (1.v.) Act, 2021 replacing l.v. Act\n1917 for uniformity of rules and regulation for inland navigation in the country covering\nsafety considerations.\n\n-3-\nAnnex.-1\nLIST OF 25 NATIONAL WATERWAYS FOUND FEASIBLE FOR CARGO/ PASSENGER\nMOVEMENT\nst. National\nWaterway Details of Waterways STATES Status\n1\nNational Watenruay\n1\nGanga-Bhagirathi-Hooghly\nRiver System (Haldia\nAllahabad)\nUttar Pradesh,\nBihar,\nJharkhand &\nWest Bengal\nDevelopment\ntaken up with\nAssistance from\nWorld Bank Jal\nMarg Vikas\nProiect\n2 National Wateruuay\n2\nBrahmaputra River\n(Dhubri - Sadiva) Assam Development\ntaken up as per\napproved SFC for\nFY 20-21 to 2024-\n25\n3 National Watenruay\n16 Barak River Assam\n4 National Waterway\n3\nWest Coast Canal\n(Kottapuram - Kollam),\nChampakara and\nUdvoqmandal Canals\nKerala\nMostly\nOperational\nWatenruays and\ndevelopment and\nmaintenance\nwork taken up.\n5 National Watenruay\n4\nKrishna River (Vijayawada\n- Muktyala)\nAndhra\nPradesh\n6 National Waterway\n5\nDhamra-Paradio via\nMangalagadi to Pankopal Odisha\n7 National Watenruay\nI\nAlappuzha-\nChanganassery Canal Kerala\n8 National Waterway\nI\nAlappuzha-Kottayam\nAthirampuzha Canal\nKerala\nAlternate route:\n11 Skm\n9 National Watenruay\n27 Cumberjua River Goa\n10 National Watenruay\n68 Mandovi River Goa\n11\nNational Waterway\n86 Rupnarayan River West Bengal\n12 National Waterway\n97 Sunderbans Waterway West Bengal\n13 National Watenruay\n111 Zuari River Goa\n14 National Wateruuay\n10\nAmba River Maharashtra\nProjects at\nappraisal stage.\n15 National Waterway\n40 Ghagra River Bihar\n16 National Waterway\n44 lchamati River West Bengal\n17 NationalWateruay\n52 Kali River Karnataka\n18 National Watenruay Kopili River Assam\n\n!\n- -t-\nsl.\n*: National\nWaterway Details of Waterways STATES Status\n57\n19 National Watenruay\n25 Chapora River Goa\n20 National Watenruay\n37 Gandak River Bihar\n21 National Wa"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |565", "t": "Funds Allocation for Infrastructural Development under NAM", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 565 \nANSWERED ON 4th FEBRUARY , 2022 \n \nFUNDS ALLOCATION FOR INFRASTRUCTURAL DEVELOPMENT UNDER NAM \n \n565.   SHRI B.B.PATIL:  \n          SHRI ARVIND DHARMAPURI:  \n \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of funds allocated for infrastructural development under National AYUSH Mission \n(NAM) in the country, State/UT-wise including Telangana; \n \n(b) whether any special grant or aid has been provided for setting up of educat ional institutions \nfor AYUSH system of medicine/treatment in Telangana; and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of funds allocated/granted/released under various Schemes of the Ministry, \nState/UT-wise including Telangana during the last five years, Schemewise; and \n \n(d) the details of funds yet to be released/due to Telangana and time -frame fixed in this regard?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH  \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) The details of funds allocated for infrastructural development under National AYUSH \nMission (NAM) in the country, State/UT-wise including Telangana is at Annexure -I. \n(b) Under NAM, Grant -in –aid has been provided to State /UT including Telangana as per th e \nproposals reflected in the State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs). However, State of Telangana has \nnot proposed  for establishment of new educational institutions for AYUSH system of \nmedicine/treatment through State Annual Action Plan (SAAP) under NAM. \n(c) The details of funds allocated/granted/released under  Centrally Sponsored Scheme of NAM ,   \nState/UT-wise including Telangana during the last five years is at Annexure –II. \n\n2 \n \n(d)  Under NAM, State Government of Telangana has submitted State Annual Action Plan \n(SAAP) for the current financial year 2021 -22 and an  amount of Rs.6157.84  lakhs has been \napproved as per NAM guideline subject to compliance of Department of Expenditure  guideline \ni.e. (i) comply with the procedure of implementation of PFMS, and (ii) submission of Utilization \nCertificate & Expenditure  upto 75% against the previous year’s released grant - in aid. Since, \nthese conditionalities have not b een fulfilled by the State Government of Telangana, Grant-in –\naid has not been released for NAM activit ies. However, as per relaxation given by the Dept. of \nExpenditure, Centre Share of Rs. 3132.252 lakhs has been released for  421 new  Ayush Health \nand  Wellness Centres (HWCs).  \n\n3 \n \n \nAnnexure-I \n(i) State/UT wise details of grants approved for upgradation of AYUSH Hospitals and \nupgradation of AYUSH Dispensaries from 2014-15 to 2021-22 \nSI. No. Name of State/UTs \nUpgradation of AYUSH \nHospitals \nUpgradation of AYUSH \nDispensaries \nNo. of \nHospitals \nAmount \napproved  \n(Rs. in lakhs) \nNo. of \nDispensaries \nAmount \napproved  \n(Rs. in lakhs) \n1 \nAndaman& \nNicobar Islands 1 75.00 0 0.00 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 10 188.00 23 130.00 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1 5.20 0 0.00 \n4 Assam 0 0.00 0 0.00 \n5 Biha