28 Sikkim 18 122.860 \n29 Tamil Nadu 250 990.060 \n30 Telangana 421 1730.310 \n31 Trip"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |566", "t": "Parental Consent to Vaccinate Minors", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION. NO. 566 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE FEBRUARY 4TH 2022 \n \nPARENTAL CONSENT TO VACCINATE MINORS \n \n566.:  SHRI KARTI P. CHIDAMBARAM, MP \nSHRI RAKESH SINGH, MP \n \nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government initiative to vaccinate children aged 15 -18 across the country has \nmobilised interest in the children and their guardians, if so, the details thereof and the \nvaccination status of children as on date, State/UT-wise including Madhya Pradesh; \n(b) whether parental consent is a prerequisite for minors aged 15 -18 to get vaccinated, if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(c) whether doctors vaccinating minors will be he ld liable for vaccinating a minor without \nparental consent, if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether there are any recourses available for minors who wish to get vaccinated but whose \nparents oppose such vaccination, if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) whether the children are likely to be administered the second dose of COVID -19 vaccination \nalso, if so, the details thereof along with the target fixed by the Government to achieve full \nvaccination coverage of children in the said age group? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE \n(DR. BHARATI PRAVIN PAWAR) \n \n(a) to (e) Covid-19 vaccination program for adolescents aged 15 to 18 years started from 3rd January \n2022 and as on 31st January 2022, 4,59,99,539 have received the 1 st dose of COVID -19 vaccine \n(62.1%). Covid vaccination is voluntary in nature and t he vaccine doses are administered to this \npopulation group after providing knowledge regarding the safety, efficacy and possible adverse -\nreaction of the vaccine to them and their parents.  \n \nIn order to raise awareness about COVID -19 vaccines, address vaccine hesitancy and sustain \nvaccine confidence among beneficiaries as well as their parents , a communication strategy is in \nplace which is implemented across all States/UTs.  Administration of 2 nd dose to these \nbeneficiaries has begun after completion of 28 days from the date of administration of 1 st dose of \nCovaxin. Adequate quantity of vaccine doses have been made available to all States/U ts to \nvaccinate all eligible beneficiaries. The State/UT -wise coverage of adolescents aged 15 to 18 \nyears is attached at Annexure I. \n**** \n\nAnnexure I \n \nState/UT-wise coverage of adolescents aged 15 to 18 years (as on 31st January 2022) \n \nS. No. State/UT 1st dose coverage \nNumber of doses  Percentage  \n1 A & N Islands 17,847 77.6% \n2 Andhra Pradesh 24,46,930 100% \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 35,640 36.4% \n4 Assam 8,72,780 42.8% \n5 Bihar 40,14,573 48.1% \n6 Chandigarh 36,747 51.0% \n7 Chhattisgarh 9,82,609 60.1% \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 18,143 53.4% \n9 Daman & Diu 11,251 45.0% \n10 Delhi 8,06,251 79.5% \n11 Goa 43,083 58.2% \n12 Gujarat 26,70,202 75.0% \n13 Haryana 9,25,292 60.1% \n14 Himachal Pradesh 2,96,898 84.3% \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 6,17,023 74.1% \n16 Jharkhand 10,12,815 42.2% \n17 Karnataka 22,55,699 71.0% \n18 Kerala 10,71,480 69.8% \n19 Ladakh 7,999 61.5% \n20 Lakshadweep 2,802 56.0% \n21 Madhya Pradesh 37,40,438 77.9% \n22 Maharashtra 31,31,914 51.7% \n23 Manipur 52,700 30.3% \n24 Meghalaya 36,874 18.0% \n25 Mizoram 42,218 63.0% \n26 Nagaland 33,653 23.5% \n27 Odisha 14,74,101 62.3% \n28 Puducherry 40,337 48.6% \n29 Punjab 4,07,139 28.7% \n30 Rajasthan 29,23,235 62.9% \n31 Sikkim 23,148 56.5% \n32 Tamil Nadu 20,23,119 60.9% \n33 Telangana 12,18,243 66.2% \n34 Tripura 1,13,705 53.4% \n35 Uttar Pradesh 92,74,097 66.2% \n36 Uttarakhand 3,98,301 63.4% \n37 West Bengal 29,20,253 60.5% \n India 4,59,99,539 62.1% \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |567", "t": "Tele Law Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-567 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON, FRIDAY, THE 4th February, 2022 \n \nTele Law Scheme \n567. SHRIMATI SUNITA DUGGAL:  \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of cases registered under Tele -Law scheme along with the \nnumber of beneficiaries who have been provided for free legal aid;  \n(b) whether the Governmnet has proposed to introduce any mobile app to \nreachout to more people and  \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LAW & JUSTICE \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) 12,97,332 cases have been registered and 12,81,591 beneficiaries have \nbeen provided pre -litigation advice till 31stDecember, 2021 under Tele -law \nscheme. \n(b) to (c) Yes, Sir . The Govern ment has launched the Citizens ’ Tele-Law \nMobile App on 13.11.2021. The Mobile Application enables the beneficiary to \naccess pre-litigation advice and consultation directly from the Panel Lawyer , \nfree of cost.The Application is available in 6 languages viz. Marathi, Punjabi, \nTamil, Telugu, Hindi and English on Android and iOS platform.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |568", "t": "Violence Against Women", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO. 568 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2022 \n  \nVIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN \n  \n568.   SHRI S. VENKATESAN: \n  \nWill the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)    whether the Government has taken note of the reports of violence against \nwomen and children in pandemic period; \n(b)    if so, the number of instances reported as per official records in the years 2019 -\n20, 2020- 21 and 2021 - 22 so far; and \n(c)    the details of allocations made, disbursed and spent under “Nirbhaya fund” in the \nFY 2021-22 and so far? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a) to (c):  National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) compiles and publishes information \non crimes against women in its publication “Crime in India” which is available on the \nwebsite of NCRB, viz. https://ncrb.gov.in/en/crime -india. The said report is \navailable  upto the year 2020. As per the data published by the NCRB, the total number \ncases of  crime against women registered during the years 2019 and 2020 are 4,05,326 \nand 3,71,503 respectively. Further, total number of cases of crimes against children \nduring the ye ars 2019 and 2020 are 1,48,090 and 1,28,531 respectively. The data \nshows decline in the crimes against women and children during the year 2020 as \ncompared to 2019. \n \nThe allocation made, disbursed and spent under \"Nirbhaya Fund”  since inception are \nRs. 6612.85 crore, Rs. 4241.91 crore and Rs. 2989.57 crore respectively . \n**** \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |569", "t": "All India Judicial Service Examination", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n                                                            \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †569 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 04.02.2022 \n \n \nAll India Judicial Service Examination   \n \n \n†569.SHRI DULAL CHAND GOSWAMI: \n   \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to conduct All India Judicial Service \nExamination for appointment of judges in district level courts;  \n(b) if so, the details o f the medium of language and pattern in which the \nexamination is likely to be conducted; and  \n(c) the details of the modalities likely to be chalked out to set up an All India \nAuthority to conduct such examinations?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LAW AND JUSTICE \n \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n  \n \n(a) to (c) : In Government's view, a properly framed All India Judicial Service is \nimportant to strengthen overall justice delivery system. This will give an \nopportunity for induction of suitably qualified fresh legal talent selected through a \nproper all -India merit selection system as well as address the issue of social \ninclusion by enabling suitable representation to marginalized and deprived \nsections of society. \n\n \n \n A comprehensive proposal was formulated for the constitution of an All India \nJudicial Service (AIJS) and the same was approved by the Committee of \nSecretaries in November, 2012. Besides attracting some of the best talent in the \ncountry, it may also facilitate inclusion of competent persons from marginalized \nsections and women in the judiciary. The proposal was included as an agenda item \nin the Conference of Chief Ministers and Chief Justices of the High Courts held in \nApril, 2013 and it was decided that the issue needs further deliberation and \nconsideration.  \n \n The views o f the State Governments and High Courts were sought on the \nproposal. There was divergence of opinion among the State Governments and \namong the High Courts on the constitution of All India Judicial Service. While \nsome State Governments and High Courts favoured the proposal, some were not in \nfavour of creation of All India Judicial Service while some others wanted changes \nin the proposal formulated by the Central Government. \n \n The matter regarding creation of a Judicial Service Commission to help the \nrecruitment to the post of district judges and review of selection process of judges/ \njudicial officers at all level was also included in the agenda for the Chief Justices \nConference, which was held on 03 rd and 04th April, 2015, wherein it was resolved \nto leave it  open to the respective High Courts to evolve appropriate methods \nwithinthe existing system to fill up the vacancies for appointment of District \njudges expeditiously. The proposal for constitution of All India Judicial Service \nwith views from the High Cour ts and State Governments received thereon was \nincluded in the agenda for the Joint Conference of Chief Ministers and Chief \nJustices of High Courts held on 05thApril, 2015.  \n\n \n \n The proposal of setting up of an All India Judicial Service was again discussed \non points of eligibility, age, selection criteria, qualification, reservations etc . in a \nmeeting chaired by Minister of Law and Justice on 16 th January 2017 in the \npresence of Minister of State for Law and Justice, Attorney General for India, \nSolicitor Ge neral of India, Secretaries of Department of Justice, Department of \nLegal Affairs and Legislative Department.  Setting up AIJS was also deliberated in \na meeting of the Parliamentary Consultative Committee in March, 2017 and the \nParliamentary Committee on the Welfare of SCs/STs on 22.02.2021.   \n \n In view of the existing divergence of opinion amongst the stakeholders, the \nGovernment is engaged in a consultative process with the stakeholders to arrive at \na common ground. \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |57", "t": "High Speed network", "head": "��\n�\n�\n�\n������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\n�������������� ������������������ �\n�\n��� ������ �\n�� ������������������� �����\n������������������ ��� ��������������� �\n�\n������������������ �\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� �������� ���� ������ ���� ������ ����� ������ ��������� ���� ������� ��������� ��� ���� ���������� ��� ����\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������\n�\n����� ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������