�� ������ ��� ������������ ���\n��������������� �������������������������������������������� �\n� ��� ���� �������� ����� ������ ��������� �������������� ��� ������\n������� �������� ������� ��� �������������� ����� ������� ���� ������\n������������� ��� ��������� ���� ������ ��� ����������� �������� ����������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n� ������� ���� ������� ������������ ��� ��������� ������ ����� ��� ���� ������ ���\n�������������� ������ ������� ������� � ���� ��� ���� ������ ��� ������\n��������������������������������������������������� �\n� �\n\n�\n��������������� ����������������������������������������������\n���� ���� �������� ��� ��������� ���� ������� ��� ���� ������ ���� ���� �����\n�������� ��� ����������� �������������� ������� ���� �������� ���� ���\n������ �\n��� ���� ������� ��������� ��� ������� ���� ����� ������� ������ ����\n��������� ������ ��� ���� �������� ��� ���������� ��� ���������� ���� ���\n����������� ������������� �\n���� �� �������� ����� ���� ����� ����� ����� ����� ���� ����� �������� ���\n������������������������������������������������������������� �\n����� �����������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ������������ ����������� ���� ������� ������ ������ �������� ���\n������������������������������������������������������� �\n�� �� ����������������������������������������������������������������� �\n�������� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� �\n� �� ������������ ���������� ���� ����� ���������� ��� ���� ������� ���\n�����������������������������������������������������������������\n��������\n�� �� ���� ������� ��� ���������� ����� ���� ������� ���������� ���� ������� ����\n�������� ��� ����������� ���� ����� ���� ���������� ��� ���� ������ ������\n����� ��� ����������� ������������ ������������� ��� ����� ������\n����������� ���� ���� ������� ���� �������� ��� ���� ���� �������� ����\n���������� ���� ������� ���� � ����������� ���������� ����������� �\n����������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |770", "t": "NIPUN Bharat Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 770 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07th FEBRUARY, 2022 \n \n \nNIPUN Bharat Scheme \n \n770. SHRI JAYANT SINHA: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is true that the COVID -19 pandemic has significantly impacted the \nfoundational education of children; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) if so, whether NIPUN Bharat Scheme aims to bridge this gap created due to the \npandemic and the details thereof; and \n \n(d) the achievements of the scheme in improving the foundational education of students \nand capacity building of teachers? \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(Dr. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to (d):  COVID-19 pandemic has caused an unprecedented public health \nemergency that has led to severe disruptions in normal life, including closure of schools.   \nA National Achievement Survey across the country has been conducted on 12th \nNovember 2021 for Grade 3, 5, 8 and 10 students of State Government schools, \nGovernment Aided schools, Private Unaided recognized schools and Central \nGovernment schools for assessing the competencies attained by the students vis -a-vis \nlearning outcomes. \nDuring the pandemic, the Mini stry of Education has taken various steps for providing \ncontinued access to education, which are available to each category of students \nirrespective of their region or economic standard. A comprehensive initiative called PM \ne-Vidya has been started which a ims to unify all efforts related to digital/online/on -air \neducation to enable multi -mode access to education. The initiative includes all forms of \ndigital modes to provide wide access - DIKSHA (online), SWAYAM (online), SWAYAM \nPRABHA (TV), other TV Channels including use of Doordarshan and AIR Networks.  \n\n \nFurther, an Alternate Academic Calendar has been prepared for learning solutions \nincluding self-assessment for grade 1 to 12 for both children with and without device.  \nBridge Course Modules have also been developed for bridging the learning gaps of Out \nof School children studying in special training centres (STC) under the provisions of \nRTE Act, 2009. Also, PRAGYATA guidelines were issued to the States/UTs to facilitate \ncontinued education through various m odes. The guidelines inter -alia include situation \nwhere internet connectivity is not available or available with very less bandwidth, \nresources are shared through various platforms like television, radio etc. that do not \ndepend on internet.  Where the digit al facility (mobile device/DTH television) is not \navailable, Ministry of Education has taken many initiatives like community Radio \nStations and a podcast called Shiksha Vani  of CBSE, Textbook,  worksheet supplied to \nresidence of learners, Handbook on 21 st Century Skills and community/mohalla classes \nare being organised. Similarly, Students Learning Enhancement Guidelines were \nreleased in 2020 to support learning of children during COVID -19. Guidelines for \ndevelopment of e-content for children with special needs have been released. Guidelines \nfor parent participation in Home- based learning  during school closure and beyond   \nhave also been released in 2021. \nNational Initiative for Proficiency in Reading with Understanding and Numeracy \n(NIPUN Bharat) Mission lays down priorities and actionable agendas for States/UTs to \nachieve the goal of proficiency in foundational literacy and numeracy for every child by \ngrade 3. Detailed guidelines for the mission have been developed which includes the \nLakshya or Targets for Foundational Literacy and Numeracy starting from the Balvatika \nupto age group 9. In addition , key competencies and learning out comes on \ndevelopmental goals have  been developed and codified covering variou s aspects, \nconcepts and skills for each level of education under NIPUN Bharat Mission.  \nIn addition,  the Integrated Teacher Training Programme called NISHTHA 3.0 on \nfoundational literacy and numeracy was released on 7 th September, 2021 in an online \nmode on DIKSHA platform for 25 lakh teachers and school heads of pre-primary to class \nV.    A special package consisting of 12 modules have been developed for this purpose, as \nper objectives of NIPUN Bharat Mission.   Further, under the mission , Vidya Pravesh  \nguidelines were developed and re leased on 29 th July, 2021 for the children entering in \nGrade-I. This is a three month play based school preparation module especially design \nto help teachers support   children who are coming to grade-I with diverse backgrounds.  \n100 days reading campaign ‘Padhe Bharat’ - a national initiative for creating a joyful \nreading experience for children was also launched on 1st January, 2022. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |771", "t": "Non-Lapsable Central Pool of Resources Scheme", "head": "Government of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.771  \n \nTo be answered on \nMonday, February 7, 2022/Magha 18, 1943 (Saka) \n \nQUESTION  \nNon-Lapsable Central Pool of Resources Scheme \n \n†771.  SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE: \nWill the Minister of DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION be pleased \nto state: \n(a)  whether the work is being carried out under the  Non Lapsable Central Pool of \nResources (NLCPR) scheme in the North-Eastern States of the country; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof as on date, State-wise; and \n(c)  the funds allocated and utilised during each ofthe last three years, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \nThe Minister of the Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region \n[Shri G. Kishan Reddy] \n  \n(a) to (c) Projects were sanctioned State -wise under the Non-Lapsable Central Pool of \nResources-State (NLCPR-State) Scheme till it was replaced by the North East Special \nInfrastructure Development Scheme (NESIDS) in 2017 -18. In total, 1635 projects worth \nRs.16233.78 crore were sanctioned for the North Eastern States,  under NLCPR-State \nscheme, out of which 1219 projects with sanctioned amount of  Rs.9726.59 crore have \nbeen completed  by 31.1.2022 , while  for the remaining 416 ongoing projects, the \nimplementation is at various stages. The State-wise details of projects sanctioned under \nNLCPR-State scheme are as under: \n \n\n(Amount in Rs. crore) \nS. \nNo. \nState Sanctioned projects Completed projects Ongoing \nprojects \n \nNumber \nof \nprojects \nAmount Number \nof \nprojects \nAmount Number of \nprojects \n1 Arunachal \nPradesh \n207 2484.01 141 1544.75 66 \n2 Assam 446 3875.97 260 1616.49 186 \n3 Manipur 177 1857.13 148 1078.44 29 \n4 Meghalaya 107 1533.96 69 709.34 38 \n5 Mizoram 159 1393.1 141 1126.49 18 \n6 Nagaland 170 1805.4 137 1322.1 33 \n7 Sikkim 247 1409.69 226 1123.50 21 \n8 Tripura 122 1874.52 97 1205.48 25 \n  Total 1635 16233.78 1219 9726.59 416 \n \nFurther, the  expenditure under the NLCPR-State scheme is now being made for \nongoing projects only to meet committed liabilities as per reported actual progress , \nwithout specific budgetary allocation of funds State -wise. D uring the last three years \nand in the current year upto 31.01.2022, the total releases made under the scheme by \nthis Ministry are as under: \n \n(Rs. in crore) \nFinancial Year Funds Released \n(NLCPR-State scheme) \n2018-19 450.21 \n2019-20 379.59 \n2020-21 197.28 \n2021-22 369.10 \n(as on 31.01.2022) \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |772", "t": "Bank Credit Schemes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 772 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 7TH FEBRUARY 2022/ MAGHA 18, 1943 (SAKA)  \nBank Credit Schemes \n772. SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the schemes being run by different banks in different States for the purpose of providing credit \nto unemployed youths at present; \n(b) the details of the number of beneficiaries of such self -employed schemes during the last three years, \nState/Bank-wise;  \n(c) whether the Government provides any assistance to banks for such schemes; and   \n(d) if so, the details thereof?    \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a) to (d): Banks provide loans under various credit-linked schemes of Government, under which unemployed youth \nmay also avail credit facilities, for productive purposes. Unemployed youth, amongst others, may avail cre dit \nfacilities for self-employment under various Government run schemes, which include, inter alia, the following — \ni. Prime Minister Employment Generation Program (PMEGP) to bring together widely dispersed traditional \nartisans/ rural and urban unemployed you th and give them self -employment opportunities to the extent \npossible, at their place, wherein financial assistance is provided to the borrowers in the form of margin \nmoney subsidy of upto 35% of the project cost; \nii. Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana -National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY -NULM) scheme through its Self \nEmployment Program (SEP) for providing financial assistance through easy access to credit to \nindividuals/groups including street vendors /hawkers of urban poor for setting up gainful self-employment \nventures /micro-enterprises, suited to their skills, training, aptitude and local conditions, wherein financial \nassistance is provided in the form of interest subvention of interest charged above 7%, with an additional \ninterest subvention of 3% available on prompt repayment; \niii. Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana -National Rural Livelihoods Mission (DAY -NRLM) scheme enables bank credit \naccess to eligible beneficiaries for productive purposes on all credit from banks/financial institutions \navailed under the scheme, wherein  financial assistance is provided in the form of interest subvention of \ninterest charged above 7% subject to a maximum of 5.5%, with an additional interest subvention of 3% \navailable on prompt repayment; \niv. Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojaya (PMMY)  to create an incl usive, sustainable and value based entrepreneurial \nculture, in collaboration with our partner institutions in achieving economic success and financial security, \nwherein financial assistance is provided in the form of interest subvention of 2% for a period of 12 months \nfor credit availed under Shishu loan category of the scheme and credit guarantee cover of National Credit \nGuarantee Trustee Company Limited (NCGTC) for all categories of loans availed under the scheme; and \nv. Stand-up India to promote entrepreneurship at grass root level for economic empowerment and job creation, \nwherein financial assistance is provided in the form of margin money subsidy upto 15% of the project cost \nthrough Credit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme for Technology Upgradation along wit h various other \nsubsidies available under different schemes of Government and credit guarantee cover of NCGTC. \n \nFurther, as per inputs from Public Sector Banks, the bank-wise and state-wise details of number of total beneficiaries \nunder above mentioned five schemes for last three years is as per Annex.  \n*** \n\nAnnex \nNumber of total beneficiaries in last three years viz. FY2018-19, FY2019-20, FY2020-21 — Bank-wise and State-wise \nState/Union-territory Bank of \nBaroda \nBank of \nIndia \nBank of \nMaharashtra \nCanara \nBank \nCentral \nBank of \nIndia \nIndian \nBank \nIndian \nOverseas \nBank \nPunjab \nNational \nBank \nPunjab \n& Sind \nBank \nState \nBank of \nIndia \nUCO \nBank \nUnion \nBank of \nIndia \nAndaman and Nicobar Islands 246 67 0 894 125 1158 152 218 0 2056 128 58 \nAndhra Pradesh 697843 188338 26699 578279 39482 390189 120334 31630 648 341380 22494 275560 \nArunachal Pradesh 398 572 171 1342 1215 683 110 735 90 4186 242 40 \nAssam 26735 13336 23574 39854 34066 27776 5760 95605 1255 40518 47553 9064 \nBihar 943538 146362 73558 104775 132801 60729 9300 202349 1056 119601 140637 21457 \nChandigarh 1466 1328 772 2391 1493 1044 510 3929 883 1220 2456 1221 \nChhattisgarh 231432 23176 31103 31700 51116 17329 13212 43538 3048 38533 19013 9667 \nDadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 4469 279 43 719 204 0 71 809 0 51 529 244 \nDelhi 12905 10479 2208 28350 6187 71239 6297 39147 4652 236 10676 14438 \nGoa 7314 5655 4728 10230 4166 991 2421 1320 105 9948 1030 8051 \nGujarat 616367 72947 26140 34401 27937 20798 16630 27941 1753 881 24860 41495 \nHaryana 14836 10800 22069 79407 23555 13809 7512 116563 9771 70865 29648 19467 \nHimachal Pradesh 8837 2743 611 17216 11590 2436 1444 10205"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |773", "t": "Van Dhan Kendra in Andhra Pradesh", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO- 773 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 07/02/2022 \n  \nVAN DHAN KENDRA IN ANDHRA PRADESH \n  \n773.      SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \nSHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI: \nDR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI: \nSHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV: \nSHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY: \n  \nWill the Minister of Tribal Affairs be pleased to state:  \n(a) the progress achieved in setting up of Van Dhan Kendra Clusters in Andhra Pradesh; \n(b) the total number of sanctioned and operational VDKCs out of the proposed 188 centres for FY 2021-22; \nand \n(c) the details of the funds allocated for the infrastructural development of VDKCs in Andhra Pradesh for \nthe last three years? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA) \n  \n(a) to (b) Ministry of Tribal Affairs is implementing Van Dhan Vikas Karyakaram through Tribal \nCooperative Marketing Development Federation of India (TRIFED) under the Scheme ‘Mechanism for \nMarketing of Minor Forest Produce (MFP) through Minimum Support Price (MSP) & Development of Value \nChain for MFP’. As per the information received from TRIFED, the progress of implementation of Van Dhan \nKendra Clusters in Andhra Pradesh including the Van Dhan Vikas Kendra Clusters (VDVKCs) sanctioned \nduring 2021-22 is as below: \nTotal No. of \nVDVK \nClusters \nSanctioned / \nApproved  \nNo. of VDVK \nClusters \nSanctioned / \nApproved during \n2021-22  \nNo. of Forest \nGatherers \nbenefitted \nAmount \nSanctioned  \n(Rs. in lakhs) \nPost Production \nOperations by \nVDVK Clusters  \n(In Nos.)  \nSales Figures by VDVK  \n  \nIn Rs. lakhs \n415 152 123258 6162.90 148 217.315 \n \n (c) As per the provisions of the scheme, building/ infrastructure support are to be established in one of the \nbeneficiary’s house/ part of house or Government/ gram panchayat building. Hence, the Ministry does not \nprovide any funds for Infrastructure development of VDVKCs. \n*****  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |774", "t": "Employment Survey", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 774 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2022 \n \nEMPLOYMENT SURVEY \n \n774.  SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \n  SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \n  SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA:  \nSHRI RAVI KISHAN: \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK:  \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE:  \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:  \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether as per the quarterly employment survey for the third \nquarter of the current fiscal year 31.0 million jobs were created in \nthe July-September quarter of 2021 in formal employment sector;  \n(b)if so, the names of the sector on which the said survey was \nconducted; \n(c)whether the percentage of female workers in the second quarter \nhas also increased considerably and if so, the details thereof; \n(d)whether the Government proposes to implement the four labour \ncodes, setting up the national social security fund to c over over 38 \ncrore informal sector workers;  \n(e)the progress achieved in pushing ahead with the labour codes and \nthe hiccups faced by the Government in implementing the same \nalong with the participation of State Governments in it; and  \n(f)whether the Government has launched any eShram portal to ensure \nlast mile delivery of benefits of various social security schemes to \nthe informal sector workers and if so, the details thereof along with \nother initiatives for the welfare of workers? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \nContd..2/- \n \n\n:: 2 :: \n \n(a) & (b):  As per the result of the first round of Quarterly Employment \nSurvey as part of All India Quarterly Establishment based Employment \nSurvey, for the period April to June 2021, conducted by Labour Bureau \nunder the Ministry of Labour & Employment, employment in creased to \n3.08 crore (approximately) in the nine selected sectors of the economy \nagainst a total of 2.37 crores in these sectors taken collectively, as \nreported in the sixth Economic Census (2013 -14) reflecting a growth \nrate of 29% . As per the second roun d of QES (July - September, 2021) \nthe employment estimates for these 9 selected sectors came out as \n3.10 crore approximately. \n(c):   The Sector-wise Percentage distribution of total estimated \nfemale workers according to QES 1 st Round (April-June, 2021) and QES \n2nd  Round (July-September, 2021) is given at Annexure.  \n(d) & (e):  The Government has notified four Labour Codes, namely, \nthe Code on Wages, 2019; the Industrial Relations Code, 2020; the \nCode on Social Security, 2020 and the Occupational Safety, Hea th and \nWorking Conditions Code, 2020. The Code on Social Security, 2020, \nprovides for social security benefits for all workers including in \nunorganized sectors as well as gig and platform workers and \nestablishes a Social Security Fund for formulating schemes for welfare \nof the unorganised workers, gig workers and platform workers.  \nUnder the Codes, the power to make rules has been \nentrusted to Central Government, State Government and appropriate \nGovernment and there is a requirement of publication of Rules in their \nofficial Gazette for a period of 30 or 45 days for   public \nconsultation.  As a step towards implementation of the four Labour \nCodes, the Central Government has published the draft Rules inviting \ncomments of all stakeholders including general public. \n\"Labour\" is in the Concurrent List of the Constitution and \nunder the Labour Codes, rules are required to be framed by the Central \nGovernment as well as by the State Governments.   The Central \nGovernment and several States/UTs have pre -published rules un der 4 \nLabour Codes.  \n(f):   Ministry of Labour & Employment has developed e -SHRAM \nportal which is a National Database of the Unorganised Workers \nseeded with Aadhaar. It has been launched on 26.08.2021 and made \navailable    to the States/ UTs for the    registration   of    unorganised  \nContd..3/- \n\n:: 3 :: \nworkers on e -SHRAM portal. As on 1 st February, 2022, 24.75 crore \nworkers have been registered on the portal. \nThe Government has many ongoing Social Security and \nWelfare Schemes for unorganized workers. For example, Life and \ndisability cover is provided through Pradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima \nYojana (PMJJBY) and Pradhan Mantri Surksha Bima Yojana (PMSBY) \nlaunched in 2015. The health and maternity benefits are addressed \nthrough Ayushman Bharat Scheme whic h is a universal health scheme \nlaunched in 2018. Old age protection is provided through a pension \nscheme namely Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan -dhan (PM -SYM) \nYojana launched in March, 2019.  \nApart from these schemes, few more schemes such as \nPublic Distribution System through One Nation One Ration Card under \nNational Food Security Act, Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Act, Deen Dayal Upadhyay Gramin Kaushal \nYojana, Pradhan Ma ntri Awas Yojana, Gareeb Kalyan Rojgar Yojana, \nMahatma G"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |775", "t": "Funds Disposed Under PRASAD Scheme in Gujarat", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.775 \nANSWERED ON 07.02.2022 \n \nFUNDS DISPOSED UNDER PRASAD SCHEME IN GUJARAT \n \n775.  SHRI C.R. PATIL:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of Pilgrimage R ejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage \nAugmentation (PRASHAD) scheme projects which has been \ncompleted so far in Gujarat; and  \n(b)  the quantum of funds disbursed under the scheme in Gujarat \nduring the last three years? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF TOURISM    (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) & (b):  Sir, the information in respect of the projects completed \nunder PRASHAD Scheme in the State of Gujarat is given below: \n \nProject Name and year of approval Approved \nCost \n(Crore ₹) \nFunds \nReleased \n(Crore ₹) \nDevelopment of Dwarka  (2016-17) 13.08 10.46 \nPilgrimage Amenities at Somnath (2016-17) 45.36 45.36 \nDevelopment of Promenade at Somnath (2018 -\n19) 47.12 44.76 \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |776", "t": "Investment by LIC", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 776 \nANSWERED ON – 7.2.2022 \nINVESTMENT BY LIC \n776. SHRI PINAKI MISRA: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of investment made and stake of LIC in PSU stocks and State -run \ncompanies/State-owned firms on the date of buying each stock during each of the \nlast five years, stock/PSU-wise; and  \n(b) the current valuation of the LIC’s stocks including the detail s on profit percentage \nand loss percentage, stock/PSU-wise?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE  \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a) and (b): Information is being collected from all the stakeholders and the same will be \nlaid on the table of the House. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |777", "t": "National Social Security Fund", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 777 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2022 \n \nNATIONAL SOCIAL SECURITY FUND \n \n777.  SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE: \n  DR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE: \n  SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT: \n  SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has set up the National Social Security \nFund for informal sector workers and to improve the ease of doing \nbusiness; \n(b)if so, the details thereof; \n(c)the number of workers of informal sector likely to be covered under \nthe said fund in each State particularly in Odisha; and \n(d)the steps taken by the Government to ensure the last mile delivery \nof benefits of various social security schemes to the informal sector \nworkers? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): The Unorganised Workers’ Social Security Act, 2008 \nmandates the Government to provide social security to all the workers \nof informal/unorganised sector. The said Act has been subsumed in the \nCode on Social Security, 2020. Adequate provisions have been \nprovided under Section 141 of the Code on Social Security, 2020 to \ncreate a Social Security Fund for the welfare of all unorganised \nworkers, Gig workers and platform workers.   \nContd..2/- \n\n \n:: 2 :: \n \n(c):   The workers of unorganised sector of  the State of Odis ha \nare also benefited under various social sec urity schemes detailed as \nunder: \n \nSchemes Number of beneficiaries \nPradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan -\ndhan (PM-SYM) pension Scheme \n1,70,936 (as on 28.01.2022) \nPradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima \nYojana (PMJJBY) \n31,45,850 (as on 27.10.2021) \nPradhan Mantri Surksha Bima \nYojana (PMSBY) \n89,84,859 (as on 27.10.2021) \n \n(d):   On, 26.08.2021, the Ministry of Labour & Employment \nlaunched e -SHRAM portal with an objective to create National \nDatabase of Unorganised Workers (NDUW) to facilitate delivery of \nSocial Security Schemes/ Welfare Schemes of the Central /State \nGovernments to the workers of informal/unorganised sector. As on \n02.02.2022, more than 24 crore workers have been registered under \nthe e-SHRAM portal. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |778", "t": "Financial Assistance to Jhansi For Creation of Infrastructure", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 778 \nANSWERED ON 07.02.2022 \n \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE TO JHANSI FOR CREATION OF \nINFRASTRUCTURE \n \n778. SHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether t he Government has provided any assistance to Uttar \nPradesh specially in Jhansi for creation of infrastructure to \nincrease tourist inflow in the State; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof during each of the last three years and the \ncurrent year, project-wise; \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to provide assistance to the \nState in the current year; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n ANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF TOURISM     (SHRI G.KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (d):  Development of tourism infrastructure is primarily the \nresponsibility of the concerned State Government/ Union Territory \nAdministration. However, Ministry of Tourism provides Financial \nAssistance to State Governments/Union Territory \nAdministrations/Central Agencies for tourism development under its \nSchemes of ‘ Swadesh Darshan’, ‘Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, \nHeritage Augmentation Drive’ (PRASHAD) and ‘Assistance to Central \nAgencies’. The assistance is provided in consultation  with the State \nGovernments/ Union Territory Administrations/ Central Agencies and are \nsanctioned subject to availability of funds, submission of suitable \ndetailed project reports, adherence to scheme guidelines and utilization \nof funds released earlier etc.  \n \nProviding financial assistance to the states under the schemes for \ninfrastructure related development is an ongoing process. Development \nof Jhansi is not included in the projects sanctioned under the above \nschemes in Uttar Pradesh.  \n\nThe details of the projects sanctioned in the State of Uttar Pradesh \nby the Ministry of Tourism u nder the schemes of ‘Swadesh Darshan’, \n‘National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, Heritage \nAugmentation Drive’ (PRASHAD) and 'Assistance to Central Agencies' \nincluding for last three years is as mentioned below.  \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme  \n                                               (Rs. in Crore) \nSr. \nNo. \nName of the Project Year of \nSanction  \nAmt. \nSanctioned \n1.  Development of Buddhist Circuit in \nSrawasti, Kushinagar & Kapilwastu \n2016-17 99.97 \n \n2.  Development of Chitrakoot and \nShringverpur under Ramayana Circuit \n2016-17 69.45 \n3.  Development of Spiritual Circuit in \nShahjahanpur-Basti- Ahar-Aligarh-\nKasganj-Sarosi-Pratapgarh-Unnao-\nKaushambi- Mirzapur-Gorakhpur- \nKairana- Domaryaganj- Baghpat-\nBarabanki- Azamgarh in Uttar Pradesh \n2016-17 65.61 \n4.  Development of Spiritual Circuit in Bijnor- \nMeerut- Kanpur- Kanpur Dehat - Banda- \nGhazipur- Salempur- Ghosi- Balia- \nAmbedkar Nagar - Aligarh- Fatehpur- \nDeoria- Mahoba- Sonbhadra- Chandauli- \nMishrikh- Bhadohi in Uttar Pradesh \n2016-17 67.51 \n5.  Development of Heritag e Circuit in \nKalinjar Fort (Banda)- Maghar Dham (Sant \nKabir Nagar) - Chauri Chaura, Shaheed \nSthal (Fatehpur) - Mauhar Sthal (Ghosi) - \nShaheed Smarak (Meerut) \n2016-17 33.97 \n6.  Development of Ayodhya under Ramayana \nCircuit \n2016-17 127.21 \n7.  Development of Spiritu al Circuit in \nJewar-Dadri-Sikandrabad-Noida-Khurja-\nBanda \n2018-19 12.03 \n8.  Development of Spiritual Circuit in \nGorakhnath Temple (Gorakhpur), \nDevipattan Temple (Balrampur) and \nVatvashni Temple (Domariyagunj) \n2018-19 15.76 \n9.  Development of Wayside Amenities i n   \nUttar Pradesh and Bihar at Varanasi -     \nGaya; Lucknow -Ayodhya- Lucknow; \nGorakhpur-Kushinagar; Kushinagar -Gaya-  \n2018-19 15.07 \n\nKushinagar routes in collaboration with \nMoRTH \n \nPRASHAD Scheme  \n(Rs. in Crore) \nSr. \nNo. \nName of the project Year of \nSanction  \nSanctioned \nAmount  \n1.  Development of Mathura -Vrindavan as \nMega Tourist Circuit (Ph-II) \n2014-15 14.93 \n2.  Construction of Tourist Facilitation \nCentre at Vrindavan, Distt. Mathura \n2014-15 9.36 \n3.  Development of Varanasi –Phase –I 2015-16 20.40 \n4.  Cruise Tourism in River Ganga, Varanasi 2017-18 10.72 \n5.  Development of Varanasi Under Prashad \nScheme – Phase II \n2017-18 44.60 \n6.  Development of Infrastructure facilities \nat Govardhan, Mathura \n2018-19 39.74 \n \nAssistance to Central Agencies Scheme   \n(Rs. in Crore) \nS. No. Name of the project Year of \nSanction \nSanctioned \nAmount  \n1.  Illumination of monuments in \nVaranasi and Sarnath  \n2014-15 5.12 \n \n2.  Illumination of three monuments in \nVaranasi \n2017-18 2.94 \n3.  Development of Jetties at nine (09) \nmain points of \nembarkation/disembarkation of River \nCruise on National Water ways No. 1& \n2 which includes Varanasi & \nPrayagraj in Uttar Pradesh also.  \n2019-20 \n \n \n \n \n \n28.03 \n \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |779", "t": "Digital Divide between Government and Private Schools", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 779 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07TH FEBRUARY, 2022 \nDigital Divide between Government and Private Schools \n \n779. SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN  \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government agrees with the view that the digital divide between private \nand Government schools has put Government school children at a major disadvantage \nand this needs to be remedied as soon as possible; \n(b) if so, the steps taken/ being taken by the Government to address this issue; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to (c)  To facilitate the online learning by all students a comprehensive initiative \ncalled PM eVIDYA has been initiated as part of AtmaNirbhar Bharat Abhiyaan on 17th May, \n2020, which unifies all efforts related to digital/online/on -air education to enable multim ode \naccess to education. The initiative includes:  \n DIKSHA (one nation, one digital platform)  is the nation’s digital infrastructure for providing \nquality e-content for school education in states/UTs and QR coded Energized Textbooks for \nall grades are available on it. \n One earmarked Swayam Prabha TV channel per class from 1 to 12 (one class, one \nchannel). \n Extensive use of Radio, Community radio and CBSE Podcast- Shiksha Vani. \n Special e -content for visually and hearing impaired developed on Digitally Acce ssible \nInformation System (DAISY) and in sign language on NIOS website/ YouTube. \nAll these schemes/programmes are available to all the students across the nation. \n \nGUIDELINES FOR PAB FOR INCLUDING LEARNING RECOVERY PLAN FOR 2022-23 \nCovid-19 pandemic has presented unprecedented challenges for the education sector as \nthe schools could not open for most part of this academic year and large migrations took \nplace all over the country. The extended school closures have interrupted the lear ning \nprocess of children and caused learning loss at every stage. Consequently, the Department \n\nhas taken several initiatives to ensure that the school going students do not lag behind in \ntheir studies during the pandemic.  The following are the guidelines that were issued in this \nregard: \n1. PRAGYATA guideline on Digital Education \n(https://mhrd.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/pragyata -guidelines_0.pdf) \n2. Learning Enhancement Guidelines for Continuous Learning \n(https://ncert.nic.in/pdf/announcement/Learning_%20Enhancement_Guidelines.pdf ) \n3. Guidelines for Children of Migrant Labourers \n(https://www.mhrd.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/Migrant%20labour%20guideline.pdf ) \n4. Guidelines for School re-opening on Learning with Social Distancing \n(https://www.mhrd.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/SOP_Guidelines_for_reopening_schools.pdf) \n5. Continuous Learning Plan (CLP) \n(https://ncert.nic.in/pdf/announcement/Learning_%20Enhancement_Guidelines.pdf ) \n6. NCTE guidelines for TEIs  \n(https://ncte.gov.in/WebAdminFiles/RecentAnnouncement/0_20_08_2020_637335320672297662.p\ndf) \n7. Guidelines for Out of School Children issued on 7th January 2021. \n(https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/guidelines_oosc.pdf ) \n8. COVID Action plan dated 4th May 2021 \n(https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/Covid_Action_Plan.pdf ) \n9. Alternative Academic Calendar \n(https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/pr_aac_0.pdf ) \n10. Guidelines for Parents for Home based Learning \n(https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/document -\nreports/MoE_Home_Learning_Guidelines.pdf) \n11. Guidelines for Vidya Pravesh \n(https://ncert.nic.in/pdf/vidyapravesh.pdf) \n12. NDEAR \n(https://www.ndear.gov.in/resources.html) \n13. Guidelines on School Safety and Security \n(https://dsel.education.gov.in/sites/default/files/update/guidelines_sss.pdf ) \n14. Library guidelines \n(https://dsel.education.gov.in/sites/default/files/update/revised_guidelines_for_promotion_Library_\nReading%20.pdf) \n15. Guidelines of Reading Campaign \n(https://dsel.education.gov.in/dsel2/sites/default/files/guidelines/Guidelines_on_Reading_Campaign\n.pdf) \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |78", "t": "Price Manipulation by Cotton Corporation of India", "head": " \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 78 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2022 \n \n \n \nPRICE MANIPULATION BY COTTON CORPORATION OF INDIA \n \n \n \n78.  SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA:  \n       SHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH:  \n       SHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:  \n       SHRIMATI GODDETI MADHAVI:  \n       SHRI M.V.V. SATYANARAYANA: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) the steps taken by the Government to address concerns regarding the role of Cotton Corporation of \nIndia (CCI) in manipulating cotton prices in favour of MNCs, if so, the details of Kapas procurement \ndone by CCI from the State of Andhra Pradesh for the year 2021- 22;  \n \n(b) whether purchase centres were set up by CCI in the State of Andhra Pradesh for procurement \noperations of cotton for the year 2021-22; if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c) whether the Government has tak en steps to regulate soaring prices of cotton in the year 2021 -22; if \nso, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n \n \n \n(a): Cotton Corporation of India (CCI) sells pressed cotton bales only through a robust independent \ne-auction portal managed by a Public Sector Undertaking.  The present e -auction system for sale of \ncotton by CCI is fool -proof, transparent and buyers’ frie ndly and there is no scope of manipulating \ncotton prices in favour of any buyer segment including MNCs. CCI undertakes cotton procurement, if \nmarket price falls below Minimum Support Price (MSP). Since, right from the beginning of cotton \nseason 2021-22, the ruling price of raw cotton is above MSP, there has been no requirement for CCI to \nundertake MSP operation. \n \n(b): During current cotton season 2021-22 (1.10.2021 to 30.09.2022), CCI has setup 33 procurement \ncentres in 10 districts under its branch office at Guntur in the state of Andhra Pradesh. List of district -\nwise procurement centres is at annexure-I. \n \n(c): Ministry is constantly engaged with various stakeholders across the entire cotton value chain in \nrespect of the prices of cotton and cotton yarn to harmonize their interest. Prices are affected by market \nforces of demand and supply. In addition, cotton being an internationally traded commodity, its price is \nalso affected by the price in the international market. \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n    List of procurement centres under CCI Branch office Guntur in the state of Andhra Pradesh \n     \n Sr.No. Name of District Name of centre  \n 1 \nGuntur \nTadikonda  \n 2 Guntur  \n 3 Guntur-A  \n 4 Narasaraopet  \n 5 Chilakaluripet  \n 6 Pedanandipadu  \n 7 Krosur  \n 8 Mancherla  \n 9 Sattenapalli  \n 10 Nadikudi  \n 11 Piduguralla  \n 12 Phirangipuram  \n 13 \nKurnool \nAdoni  \n 14 Nandyal  \n 15 Yemmiganur  \n 16 Penchikalpadu  \n 17 \nPrakasam \nParchur  \n 18 Inkollu  \n 19 Markapuram  \n 20 \nKrishna \nNandigama  \n 21 Jaggaiahpet  \n 22 Kanchikacharla  \n 23 Mylavaram  \n 24 Gampalagudem  \n 25 A.Kondur  \n 26 East Godavari Pittapuram  \n 27 Kirlampudi  \n 28 West Godavari Jangareddygudem  \n 29 Srikakulam Rajam  \n 30 Vijayanagaram Sulur  \n 31 Ananthapur Gooty  \n 32 Tadipatri  \n 33 Kadappa Proddutur  \n \n*** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |780", "t": "Merger of General Insurance Companies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 780 \nANSWERED ON – 7.2.2022 \nMERGER OF GENERAL INSURANCE COMPANIES \n780. SHRI SUBBARAYAN K.: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether in the budget proposals of 2018 it was announced to merge the three public \nsector general insurance companies - The Oriental Insurance Co. Ltd, National \nInsurance Co. Ltd and United India Insurance Co. Ltd;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the decision taken thereon;  \n(c) whether it is a fact that the employees of the general insurance sector are demanding \nthe merger of all the four public sector general insurance companies for the better \nfunctioning of this sector; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the Government’s reaction thereto?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE  \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a) to (d): From time to time, associations of employees of public sector general insurance \ncompanies submit their demands of their employees and suggestions for strengthening \nthem. The suggestions received have included suggestions in respect of merger of these \ncompanies.  \nThe speech of the Finance Minister on the b udget for the financial year 2018-2019 \nannounced the Government’s intent to merge t hree public sector general insurance \ncompanies, viz., National Insurance Company L imited, United India Insurance Company \nLimited and Oriental Insurance Company Limited.  \nAgainst the above background and upon further consideration of the matter, Government \nsubsequently decided to infuse capital, focus on their growth and not to proceed with \nmerger. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |781", "t": "Extended Producer Responsibility", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO: 781 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 07.02.2022 \n \nExtended Producer Responsibility \n \n781.  SHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has worked out measures on regulations on Extended \nProducer Responsibility in addition to commitments under the Solid Waste \nManagement Rules, 2016, the Plastic Waste Management Rules, 2016, and the \nSwachh Bharat Mission as these regulations denote a backslide, particularly with \nrespect to integration of the informal sector also;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the present status in this regard; and \n(c)  whether the FMCG company will not only include the costs of making, packing and \ndistributing a packet of chips, but also for the collection and recycling/reuse of the \npacket in India including the formal/informal sector also? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (c): The Solid Waste Management Rules, 2016, and Plastic Waste Management Rules, \n2016, have specific provisions with respect to informal waste collectors or waste pickers and \ntheir role in solid waste management including plastic waste management. The Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change has notified the draft Regulations on the Extended \nProducer Responsibility for plastic packaging under Plastic Waste Management Rules, 2016, \nas amended from time to time, in the Gazette of India vide GSR No. 722 (E) on 6th October, \n2021for public consultation. As per the draft notification, the producers, importers &brand-\nowners are mandated to fulfil their extended producer responsibility obligations by providing \ncertificates of plastic waste processing from recyclers and end of life disposal \nentitiesregistered under Plastic Waste Management Rules, 2016.The producers, importers and \nbrand owners have also been obligated to register. The guidelines for extended producer \nresponsibility will allow for development of the waste management sector and the \nformal/informal sector involved in waste management will get engaged.The enforceable \nobligation of minimum level of recycling of plastic packaging waste collected under extended \nproducer responsibility onproducers, importers &brand-owners in the draft notification will \nfurther strengthen circular economy of plastic packaging waste. \n \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |782", "t": "Environmental Clearance to Mekedatu Dam Project", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO: 782 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 07.02.2022 \n \nEnvironmental Clearance to Mekedatu Dam Project \n \n782:  SHRI PRAJWAL REVANNA \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to  \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the environmental clearance to Mekedatu dam project in Karnataka is approved \nby the Ministry and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasonstherefor; \n(b) the details of forest area and villages likely to besubmerged/displacement of people likely \nto be affected; \n(c) the time by which the environmental clearance islikely to be approved; and \n(d) the action taken by the Government for quick environmental clearance to Mekedatu \nProject? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (d) The proposal for grant of Terms of Reference (ToR) to conduct Environment \nImpact Assessment (EIA) study for construction of Mekedatu Balancing Reservoir and \nDrinking Water Project in Ramanagara and Chamarajanagar Districts of Karnataka was \nsubmitted to the Ministry on 20.06.2019 in order to obtain prior Environmental Clearance(EC) \nunder the provisions of the EIA Notification, 2006, as amended. The proposal was considered \nby the Expert Appraisal Committee (EAC), constituted by the Ministry to consider the proposal \nof River Valley and Hydro-electric project for grant of EC, in the 25th EAC meeting held on \n19.07.2019. As per project feasibility report submitted by the project authority a total of 4996 \nha area at FRL 440 m will be submerged due to this project, of which 2925.5 ha is part of \nCauvery Wildlife Sanctuary and 1869.50 ha is part of Reserved Forest.The villages namely, \nSangama, Kongedoddi, Madavala, Muthathi and Bommasandrafall in the areas under \nsubmergence. The EAC after detailed deliberations on the proposed project inter-alia \nopined that in view of inter-State issues between Tamil Nadu and Karnataka, \nan amicable solution needs to be arrived at between the two States.  \n \nThe MoEF&CC, after examination of the issues and in consultation with Ministry of Jal \nShakti (MoJS), has decided to consider the proposal for grant of Terms of Reference(ToR) to \nMekedatu Balancing Reservoir and Drinking Water Projectonly after acceptance of Draft Pre-\nfeasibility Report (DPR) of the Mekedatu project by the Ministry of Jal Shakti (MoJS) and \nCauvery Water Management Authority (CWMA).  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |783", "t": "Closure/ Merger of Schools", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n    \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 783 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2022 \n \nClosure/ Merger of Schools \n \n783.    Dr. Mohammad Jawed: \n \nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state:  \n(a)  the number of elementary schools which have been closed/merged during 2019 -2020 \nand 2020-2021 in various States in the country, State/UT - wise including the number \nof children impacted and details of children who have received additional \ntransportation allowance from Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS); \n(c)  whether the Government has undertaken or planning to undertake any evaluation of \nthe impact of past school mergers given recent protests against closure in several \nStates; and \n(d)  the extent to which the children were able to access adequate and timely last mile \ntransportation after closure of the school? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) to (d): Education is in the Concurrent list of the Constitution and the opening and \nmerger/closing of schools are within the purview of respective State Government and UT \nAdministration. The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009 \nprovides children’s access to elementary s chools within the defined area or limits of \nneighbourhood. In pursuance to Section 6 of the RTE Act, all States have notified area or \nlimits of their neighbourhood norms. Further, Section 8 of the RTE Act, 2009 also \nmandates that the appropriate government  shall provide free and compulsory elementary \neducation to every child and will ensure availability of neighbourhood schools. \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |785", "t": "Monuments Declared As National Importance", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.785 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.02.2022 \n \nMONUMENTS DECLARED AS NATIONAL IMPORTANCE \n \n  \n†785. DR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of monuments or sites declared as of national importance by   the \nArchaeological Survey of India and the State-wise details thereof; and  \n(b) the amount of funds spent on conservation of the said monuments or sites during   the \nlast five years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM AND \nDEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n(SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a)  There are 3693 monuments and sites of national importance. State -wise details are at \nAnnexure-I.  \n   (b) The expenditure incurred on conservation and preservation of these monuments and \nsites (State-wise) during the last five years is at Annexure-II.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \n \n               ANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED    QUESTION NO.  785 FOR 07.02.2022 \n          STATE-WISE NUMBER OF MONUMENTS/SITES OF NATIONAL \nIMPORTANCE IN THE     COUNTRY \n \nSl.N\no. \nName of State / U.T. Number of \nMonuments/Site \n1.  Andhra Pradesh  135 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh  03 \n3.  Assam  55 \n4.  Bihar  70 \n5.  Chhattisgarh  46 \n6.  Daman & Diu (U.T.)  11 \n7.  Go