ERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY & DAIRYING  \nLOK SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION No.932 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8TH FEBRUARY, 2022 \n \nINCREASE IN MILK PRODUCTION \n \n932. DR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL:  \n        SHRI RODMALNAGAR: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nमात्स्यिकी, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the quantum of increase in m ilk production every year along with the details of milk \nproduction during the current year and the last six year, State-wise;  \n(b)  the position of India in milk production in the world; and \n(c) the steps being taken by the Government to increase milk production and details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a): The details of milk production along with quantum of increase for the country during last six \nyears are given as under:  \n \nYear Milk Production \n(In Million Tonnes) \nPercentage increase \n(%) \n2015-16 155.5 6.27 % \n2016-17 165.4 6.38 % \n2017-18 176.3 6.62 % \n2018-19 187.7 6.47 % \n2019-20 198.4 5.69 % \n2020-21* 210.0 5.81 % \nNote:  “*”- Provisional \nState-wise details of milk production and quantum of increase for last six years are placed at \nAnnexure-I and Annexure-II respectively. \n(b):  India is at the First position in  the world in milk production . (Source: Economic Survey report \n2021-22 and Food and Agriculture Organization-STAT) \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n(c): In order to enhance milk production in the country the Government of India has been \nimplementing following major Central Sector and Centrally Sponsored schemes:  \n \ni. Rashtriya Gokul mission: This scheme has been implementing since 2014 with the aim of \ndevelopment and conservation of indigenous breeds, genetic upgradation of bovine \npopulation and enhancement of milk production and productivity of bovines thereby \nmaking milk production more remunerative to farmers engaged in dairying. \n \nii. National Programme for Dairy Development: It has aim to enhance quality of milk and \nmilk products and increase share of organized milk procurement. \n \niii. Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund. \n \niv. Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Organizations : This schem e is  \nengaged in dairy activities and this scheme was approved to provide working capital loan \nto State Cooperatives and Federations. \n \n   \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wise Estimate of Milk Production (‘000 Tonnes) \nS. \nNo. States/UTs 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21# \n1 Andhra Pradesh 10816.99 12177.94 13724.99 15044.37 15263.10 14713.84 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh 50.13 52.53 54.02 55.10 60.63 43.89 \n3 Assam 843.46 861.27 871.89 882.27 919.94 954.07 \n4 Bihar 8288.42 8711.07 9241.50 9818.10 10480.33 11501.58 \n5 Chhattisgarh 1277.32 1373.55 1469.38 1566.88 1675.56 1747.28 \n6 Goa 54.34 51.36 54.88 57.17 61.43 60.14 \n7 Gujarat 12262.35 12784.12 13569.06 14492.77 15292.35 15852.69 \n8 Haryana 8381.33 8974.75 9809.00 10726.09 11734.72 11283.55 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 1282.86 1329.11 1392.18 1460.15 1530.94 1576.44 \n10 Jammu & \nKashmir 2273.35 2376.09 2459.79 2540.11 2506.36 2594.50 \n11 Jharkhand 1812.38 1893.80 2015.62 2183.05 2321.16 2434.02 \n12 Karnataka 6344.01 6562.15 7136.66 7900.63 9031.49 10936.44 \n13 Kerala 2649.82 2520.34 2575.98 2548.28 2544.35 2533.88 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 12148.37 13445.32 14713.17 15911.13 17108.96 17999.30 \n15 Maharashtra 10152.61 10402.15 11102.29 11655.46 12024.26 13703.32 \n16 Manipur 78.97 78.82 81.66 85.75 89.65 71.05 \n17 Meghalaya 83.95 83.96 85.03 86.61 87.60 89.14 \n18 Mizoram 22.00 24.16 25.02 25.75 23.53 25.38 \n19 Nagaland 77.00 79.37 74.09 72.57 61.64 55.93 \n20 Odisha 1930.47 2003.42 2087.96 2311.07 2370.09 2372.81 \n21 Punjab 10774.20 11282.06 11854.88 12598.82 13347.56 13393.99 \n22 Rajasthan 18500.08 20849.59 22427.10 23668.07 25573.09 30723.11 \n23 Sikkim  66.74 54.35 58.67 60.85 83.94 73.85 \n24 Tamil Nadu 7243.53 7556.35 7741.82 8361.68 8759.01 9790.36 \n25 Telangana 4442.45 4681.09 4965.37 5416.13 5589.80 5765.21 \n26 Tripura 152.23 159.59 174.26 185.27 198.60 206.17 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 26386.81 27769.74 29051.72 30518.91 31863.91 31359.10 \n28 Uttarakhand 1655.81 1692.42 1741.69 1792.37 1844.71 1797.45 \n29 West Bengal 5038.47 5182.60 5388.61 5606.82 5868.61 6164.82 \n30 A&N Islands 15.43 16.14 16.99 18.15 18.90 14.67 \n31 Chandigarh 43.18 36.39 42.30 45.23 48.87 52.30 \n32 Ladakh** - - - - - 14.88 \n33 D & N Haveli* 8.52 7.50 7.50 - - 1.40 Daman & Diu  0.80 0.62 0.83 1.01 1.21 \n34 Delhi* 280.83 279.11 279.11 - - - \n35 Lakshadweep 3.25 3.24 3.65 3.66 3.78 3.81 \n36 Puducherry * 48.04 48.31 48.68 49.20 49.50 49.50 \nAll India 155490.50 165404.38 176347.3\n5 187749.46 198439.57 209959.88 \nNote “*” previous year data used, “#” Provisional , “**” Milk production for earlier years is \nincluded in Jammu & Kashmir \n\nAnnexure-II \nState-wise Estimate of Milk Annual Growth Rate (%) \nS. No. States/UTs 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |933", "t": "Allocation of Funds for Agro Processing Clusters", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 933 \nANSWERED ON 08TH FEBRUARY, 2022 \n  \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS FOR AGRO PROCESSING CLUSTERS \n  \n933. SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \n  SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n  SHRI N. REDDEPPA: \n  SHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \n  SHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY: \n             \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government has allocated funds to the States of Andhra Pradesh and  \nMaharashtra under the Scheme for Infrastructure for Agro Processing Clusters for the \nyear 2021-22 and if so, the details thereof \n  \n(b) the details of the total number of agro -processing clusters operationalized during the  \nyear 2021-22 under the Scheme for Infrastructure for Agro Processing Clusters; and \n  \n(c)      the details of the number of food processing units operationalized in Agro Processing  \nclusters during the year 2021-22, State-wise? \n  \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a): The Scheme for Creation of Infrastructure for Agro Processing Clusters (APC) scheme \nis not state specific, therefore, state wise funds are not allocated under the scheme. However, \nduring the year 2021 -22, this Ministry has approved grants -in-aid of Rs. 23.584 Cr. for 3 \nprojects in States of Andhra Pradesh and Maharashtra under APC scheme as per below details: \n \n(Rs. in Crore) \nState No. of projects approved Grants-in-aid approved \nAndhra Pradesh 1 5.879 \nMaharashtra 2 17.7053 \nTotal 3 23.5843 \n  \n(b): The details of 5 agro processing clusters wherein core processing infrastructure & basic \nenabling infrastructure have been completed during the year 2021 -22 under the Scheme for \nInfrastructure for Agro Processing Clusters are at Annexure-I. \n \n(c): the details of the 7 food processing units operationalized in Agro Processing clusters \nduring the year 2021-22 are at Annexure-II. \n  \n \n \n***** \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \n  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 933 FOR ANSWER ON 08 TH FEBRUARY,2022 REGARDING  \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS FOR AGRO PROCESSING CLUSTERS. \n \nThe details of 5 agro processing clusters wherein core processing infrastructure & basic \nenabling infrastructure have been completed during the year 2021-22 under the Scheme \nfor Creation of Infrastructure for Agro Processing Clusters \n  \n (Rs. in Crore) \nS.No. Name of the Project  \nExecution Agency \n(PEA) \nDistrict State Date of \napproval  \nProject \ncost  \nGrant  \nApproved  \n1 M/s Patel Retail Pvt. Ltd. Kutch Gujarat 07.02.2019 13.7 4.36 \n2 M/s Shree Shyam Snacks \nFood Private Limited  \nHisar Haryana 18.02.2019 10.26 3.09 \n3 M/s Great Himalayan \nFarm Fresh \nKangra HP 31.07.2018 24.49 10.00 \n4 M/s Nimar Agro Park  Barwani Madhya \nPradesh \n08.06.2018 31.55 10 \n5 M/s Bharti Agro Cluster Betul Madhya \nPradesh \n19.12.2018 26.13 9.8 \n  \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE-II \n  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 933 FOR ANSWER ON 08 TH FEBRUARY,2022 REGARDING \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS FOR AGRO PROCESSING CLUSTERS. \n  \nDetails of numbers of food processing units operationalized in Agro Processing Clusters:  \n  \n(Rs. in Crore) \nSr. \nNo. \nName of \nthe \napplicant \nName of \nthe APC \nDistrict State Sector Date of \nApproval \nProject \nCost  \nApproved \nGrant  \n1 \nM/s  \nFritfood  \nPrivate  \nLimited \nM/s Karnal \nFood Pack \nCluster \nLimited \nKarnal Haryana Consumer \nProducts 02.12.2019 18.62 4.79 \n2 \nM/s H.V.  \nSeeds  \nIndustries \nM/s Kisan \nSampada \nFood Park \nIndustries \nNagpur Maharashtra Grain \nMilling 22.10.2018 3.60 1.06 \n3 \nM/s  \nSahyadri  \nFarmers  \nProducer  \nCompany  \nLtd (Unit  \n1) \nM/s \nSahyadri \nFarmers \nProducer \nCompany \nLimited \nNashik Maharashtra F&V \nprocessing 09.07.2019 10.63 4.28 \n4 \nM/s  \nSahyadri  \nFarmers  \nProducer  \nCompany  \nLtd (Unit  \n2) \nM/s \nSahyadri \nFarmers \nProducer \nCompany \nLimited \nNashik Maharashtra F&V \nprocessing 09.07.2019 11.12 4.66 \n5 \nM/s  \nSahyadri  \nFarmers  \nProducer  \nCompany  \nLtd (Unit  \n3) \nM/s \nSahyadri \nFarmers \nProducer \nCompany \nLimited \nNashik Maharashtra F&V \nprocessing 09.07.2019 10.71 4.20 \n6 \nM/s  \nBiocritical \nExtract  \nIndia \nM/s \nSwabhimani \nCo-op \nIndustrial \nEstate Ltd. \nKolhapur Maharashtra Consumer \nProducts 16.03.2020 5.01 1.47 \n7 \nM/s  \nSahyadri  \nFarmers  \nProducer  \nCompany  \nLimited  \n(Unit-IV ) \nM/s \nSahyadri \nFarmers \nProducer \nCompany \nLimited \nNashik Maharashtra F&V \nprocessing 21.07.2020 12.56 5.00 \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |934", "t": "Rural Road Network under PMGSY-III", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 934 \n  ANSWERED ON 08/02/2022 \n \nRURAL ROAD NETWORK UNDER PMGSY-III \n \n934.  COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of eligible habitations in the  rural road network covered under \nPradhan Mantri Gram  Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) since 2013, State -wise, particularly  \nthe State of Rajasthan, district-wise; \n(b)  the total number of Grami n Agricultural Markets  established since 2019 along with \nthe details of farmers  engagement with the same, State -wise, particularly the State of \nRajasthan, district-wise; \n(c)  whether the Government has any annual action  plan to achieve the targets and \ndisburse funds set under the PMGSY-III till 2024-25; and \n(d)  the areas covered under rural road network as of  January 2022 combining the work \ndone under all three  phases of PMGSY along with the details of the total area  left to \nbe covered, State wise including the State of Rajasthan, district-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE) \n \n(a) The total number of eligible habitations in the rural road network connected under \nPradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) since 2013, State/UT -wise including \nRajasthan State are given at Annexure-I. District-wise details of habitations connected under \nPMGSY in the State of Rajasthan are given at Annexure-II.  \n  \n(b) The Government of Rajasthan has intimated that its Agricultural Marketing Division, \nRajasthan has not established any Gramin Agricultural Markets since 2019. \n  \n(c)  PMGSY-III was launched i n the year 2019 for consolidation of 1,25,000 Km Through \nRoutes and Major Rural Links. Against the allocated target, 77,129 km road length (61.70% \nof total allocation) has already been sanctioned and 25,120 Km completed. The State of \nRajasthan has been all ocated 8,662.5 km road length under PMGSY -III, out of which 5,821 \nkm road length worth Rs. 3,121.64 crore including state share has been sanctioned. All the \nprojects under PMGSY-III are targeted for completion by March, 2025. There is no dearth of \nfunds required for completion of PMGSY projects. \n  \n(d) All the projects under PMGSY -I & II have already been sanctioned to the states. Against \ntotal 6,95,493 km road length sanctioned under PMGSY -I &II, only  21,999 km length is \nbalance for completion. All the pro jects under PMGSY I & II are targeted for completion by \nSeptember 2022. \n  \nUnder PMGSY-III, against the allocated target of 1,25,000 km road length, 77,129 km has \nbeen sanctioned and 25,120 km has been completed. All the projects under PMGSY -III are \n\ntargeted to be completion by March, 2025. The State/UTs wise details of road length \nsanctioned, completed under all three phases of PMGSY and Road Connectivity Project for \nLeft Wing Extremism Affected Areas (RCPLWEA) are given in Annexure -III. The district \nwise details of road length sanctioned, completed in the state of Rajasthan under all three \nphases of PMGSY are given at Annexure-IV. \n  \n****** \n\n  \nAnnexure –I \nAnnexure referred to in reply to point (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.934 for \nreply on 08.02.2022 \nState/UT-wise details of Habitations connected since 2013 under PMGSY \n  \nS. No. State/UT Name Habitations Connected \n1 Andaman And Nicobar 6 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 678 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 207 \n4 Assam 7,298 \n5 Bihar 20,861 \n6 Chhattisgarh 3,810 \n7 Gujarat 492 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 708 \n9 Jammu And Kashmir 1,307 \n10 Jharkhand 6,651 \n11 Karnataka 27 \n12 Kerala 67 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 7,415 \n14 Maharashtra 305 \n15 Manipur 318 \n16 Meghalaya 238 \n17 Mizoram 91 \n18 Nagaland 3 \n19 Odisha 9,068 \n20 Punjab 8 \n21 Rajasthan 4,662 \n22 Sikkim 166 \n23 Tamilnadu 40 \n24 Tripura 391 \n25 Uttar Pradesh 1,277 \n26 Uttarakhand 1,177 \n27 West Bengal 5,774 \n28 Telangana 178 \n29 Ladakh 41 \nTotal 73,264 \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n  Annexure-II \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to point (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 934 for \nreply on 08.02.2022 \nDistrict-wise details Habitations connected in Rajasthan since 2013 under PMGSY \n  \n  \nS. No. District Name Habitations Connected \n1 Ajmer 63 \n2 Alwar 75 \n3 Banswara 312 \n4 Baran 22 \n5 Barmer 315 \n6 Bharatpur 39 \n7 Bhilwara 101 \n8 Bikaner 164 \n9 Bundi 17 \n10 Chittaurgarh 32 \n11 Churu 25 \n12 Dausa 115 \n13 Dholpur 46 \n14 Dungarpur 605 \n15 Hanumangarh 1 \n16 Jaipur 232 \n17 Jaisalmer 23 \n18 Jalor 377 \n19 Jhalawar 5 \n20 Jhunjhunun 223 \n21 Jodhpur 477 \n22 Karauli 62 \n23 Kota 3 \n24 Nagaur 250 \n25 Pali 106 \n26 Pratapgarh 104 \n27 Rajsamand 92 \n28 Sawaimadhopur 32 \n29 Sikar 127 \n30 Sirohi 62 \n31 Sri Ganganagar 0 \n32 Tonk 9 \n33 Udaipur 546 \nTotal 4,662 \n \n  \n\nAnnexure –III \nAnnexure referred to in reply to point (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 934 for \nreply on 08.02.2022 \nState/UTs wise details of road length sanctioned, completed under all three phases "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |935", "t": "Farmers Suicide", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICU\nLTURE AND FARMERS WE\nLFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARREDQUESTION NO\n. 935\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON TH\nE 8\nTH\n \nFERUARY, 2022\n \n \nFARMERS SUICIDE\n \n \n935. \n \n \nSHRI MAHESH SAHOO\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether the Government has conducted any comprehensive study to figure out causes \nof farmers suicide across the country;\n \n \n(b) if so, the details the\nreof and if not, any future proposal so far;\n \n \n(c): whether the Government has any data of farmers death by suicide and if so, the \ndetails thereof for the last five years, State\n-\nwise; and\n \n \n(d) the details of corrective steps taken by the Government in this \nregard?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n     \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH \nTOMAR\n)\n \n \n(\na & b): \n \nGovernment\n \nh\nad undertaken a study “Farmers Suicide in India: Causes and \nPolicy Prescription” as an all India coordinated study in the work plan 2016\n-\n17 through the \nInstitute of Social and Economic Change (ISEC), Bengaluru. The study covered 13 states \nof the country wh\nich included Karnataka, Maharashtra, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil \nNadu, Kerala, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Punjab, Haryana, Gujarat, Uttar Pradesh \nand West Bengal. \n \n \n \nThe study concluded that frequent crop failure due to vagaries of monsoon, \nabsence\n \nof assured water resources and attack of pest and diseases are the most \nimportant causes of farmers’ distress. The study had made, inter\n-\nalia, following \nsuggestions to address the above problems:\n \n \ni\n)\n \nBringing individual farmers under the ambit of crop insura\nnce;\n \nii\n)\n \nJudicious use of available water is required;\n \n\niii\n)\n \nGovernment intervention through MSP covering cost of production plus \nreasonable profit margin;\n \niv\n)\n \nRisk hedging through crop and enterprise diversification should be encouraged \nto reduce farmers’ distress \naiming at sustainable income; and\n \nv\n)\n \nRegulate informal credit market.\n \n \n(c): \n \nThe National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) under the Ministry of Home Affairs \ncompiles and disseminates information on suicides in its publication titled ‘Accidental \nDeaths and Suicide\ns in India’ (ADSI). NCRB has published reports upto 2020 which are \navailable on its website. \nThe State\n-\nwise details of suicide committed by farmers, as per \nNCRB Report for the last five years, are at \nAnnexure\n.\n \n \n(d):\n \nThe corrective steps taken by the Govern\nment on the suggestions given in the \nabove said study are as under:\n \n \ni\n)\n \nWith a view to provide better insurance coverage to crops for risk mitigation, \nGovernment has introduced flagship yield based Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima \nYojana (PMFBY) alongwith weather in\ndex based Restructured Weather Based \nCrop Insurance Scheme (RWBCIS) from Kharif 2016.\n \n \nii\n)\n \nMinistry of Jal Shakti has been organising competitions on various aspects of \nwater including water conservation, sustainable development of water resources \nand for \nwater use efficiency for the purpose of bringing awareness about \nsignificance of water in the people of the country.\n \n \niii\n)\n \nThe Union Budget for 2018\n-\n19 had announced the pre\n-\ndetermined principle to \nkeep MSP at levels of one and half times of the cost of product\nion. Accordingly, \nGovernment has increased the MSPs for all mandated Kharif, Rabi and other \ncommercial crops with a return of at least 50 per cent over all India weighted \naverage cost of production from the agricultural year 2018\n-\n19.\n \nIn line with the \nsame \nprinciple, Government has announced the increase in MSP for all \nmandated Kharif crops of the year 2021\n-\n22 on\n \n9\nth\n \nJune, 2021\n \nand for all \nmandated Rabi crops of the year 2021\n-\n22 on\n \n8\nth\n \nSeptember, 2021.\n \n \niv\n)\n \nPMFBY scheme aims at supporting the sustainable product\nion in agriculture \nsector by way of providing financial support to farmers suffering crop \nloss/damage arising out of unforeseen natural calamities, adverse weather \nincidence and to help in stabilize the income of farmers to ensure their \ncontinuance in farm\ning.\n \n \n \nComprehensive risk insurance to farmers is provided \nagainst non\n-\npreventable natural calamities such as drought, dry\n-\nspells, flood, \nhailstorm, inundation etc. under the scheme for entire crop cycle including pre\n-\nsowing to post\n-\nharvest losses.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n     \nThe scheme is being implemented on actuarial/bidded premium rate \nbasis.\n \n \nHowever, farmers have to pay a\n \nminimum uniform fixed premium rate at \nmaximum 2% for Kharif, 1.5% for Rabi food and oilseed crops and 5% for \nannual commercial/horticultural crops\n, with balance of actuarial/bidded premium \nbeing shared by the Central and State Government on 50:50 basis\n \nexcept in \nNorth Eastern States, where it is 90:10 with effect from Kharif 2020 \nseason.\n \n \n \n \n \nThe scheme is volunta"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |936", "t": "Computerisation of Primary Agricultural Credit Societies", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO\n.\n \n936\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n0\n8\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n,\n \n2022\n \n \nCOMPUTERISATION OF PRIMARY AGRICULTURAL CREDIT \nSOCIETIES\n \n \n936\n.\n \nSHRI VENKATESH\n \nNETHA BORLAKUNTA\n:\n \n        \nSHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR:\n \n        \nDR. G. RANJITH REDDY:\n \n        \nSHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU\n:\n \n \nWill the \nMinister of \nCO\nOPERATION \nसहका\u0000रता\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government\nis \naware that Telangana has computerized Primary \nAgricultural Credit Societies\n;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the details \nthereof; and\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether there are any plans by the Government to replicate the same in other States \nto strengthen credit societies, if so, the details thereof\n?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF \nCOOPERATION\n \nसहका\u0000रता\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI AMIT SHAH)\n \n \n(a) \nand\n \n(\nb\n)\n:\n \nYes\n \ns\nir\n. \nNational \nBank for Agriculture and Rural Development\n \n(NABARD) has \ninformed that Telangana has undertaken computerization of \nPrimary Agricultural Credit \nSocieties (\nPACS\n)\n \nby the Telangana State Cooper\native Apex Bank Ltd. (TSCAB) in \ncollaboration with \nNABARD\n.\n \n \n(c\n)\n:\n \nCentral \nGovernment is formulating a project for Computerisation of \n63,000 functional \nPACS in all States/UTs in the country.\n \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |937", "t": "Floriculture Regional Station", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 937 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8Th FEBRUARY, 2022 \n \n \nFLORICULTURE REGIONAL STATION \n \n \n937.  SHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \n \n Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE           \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware that there are  demands since long from \nfloriculture farmers for setting  up of full -fledged Floriculture Reg ional Station with  \nrequired scientists and other technical staff to guide Kadiyam nursery; \n(b) if so, the steps taken so far in this regard; and \n(c) the time by which the above station is going to be set up? \n \nANSWER \n \n THE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE      \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h       (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) to (c):  The Government is aware of the demands for setting up full -fledged \nFloriculture Regional Station with required scientists and other technical staff to guide \nKadiyam nursery. \n  \n Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) has set up a Regional Station \nof ICAR-Directorate of Floricultural Research (ICAR -DFR), Pune at Vemagiri (near \nRajamahendravaram), Kadiyam Mandal, East Godavari Dist rict, Andhra Pradesh \nduring 2017 in view of the requirement of nursery industry and farmers of floriculture \nof the region. Five scientist positions have been sanctioned for the Regional Station. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|8|UNSTARRED |938", "t": "Progress of National Horticulture Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE & FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 938\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n08\nth\n \nFEBRUARY, 2022\n \n \nPROGRESS OF NATIONAL HORTICULTURE MISSION\n \n \n \n938\n.\n \nSHRI \nJAYANT SINHA\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to S\ntate:\n \n(a) the progress of the National Horticulture Mission (NHM), the funds disbursed and the \nnumber of beneficiaries under the scheme since 2015, State\n-\nwise,\n \nincluding Jharkhand; \nand \n \n(b) the funds allocated and utilized in Jharkhand for the scheme so far for 2021\n-\n22? \n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \n \nNational Horticulture Mission (NHM) a sub\n-\nscheme of Centrally Sponsored scheme \nof \nMission for Integrated Dev\nelopment of Horticulture (MIDH) is being implemented by \nMinistry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare \nfor the holistic development of horticulture in t\nhe \ncountry. All States/UTs are covered under MIDH\n. \n \n \n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of the progress including Jharkhand under the major components \nof the NHM scheme since 2015 is placed at Annexure\n-\nI. The State\n-\nwise details of the \nfunds disbursed to all the States i\nncluding Jharkhand is placed at Annexure\n-\nII. \n \n \n \n \nSince the \nMIDH \nscheme is being implemented by State Governments, \ntherefore, \nthe beneficiary data is maintained by the States.\n \n \n(b):\n \nDuring 2021\n-\n22, an amount of Rs. 35.00 crore (including State share) has be\nen \nallocated to Jharkhand under NHM scheme and utilization of an amount of Rs. 39.90 \nLakhs (including State share) has been reported by the State.\n \n\nAnnexure I\n \nState\n-\nwise details of the progress under the major components of the NHM scheme\n \nsince 2015 are as under:\n \n \nS.\n \nNo.\n \nState\n \nArea \nCoverage\n \n(ha)\n \nReju